b'MUMBAI: The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has said that the ratio of bad loans has improved from last year and is expected to further decline in March 2019\xe2\x80\x99s financial results. RBI governor Shaktikanta Das has, however, said that despite the improvement, the current levels of bad loans are still too high for comfort.


In its biannual financial stability report (FSR) released here on Monday, the RBI said that the gross non-performing assets (GNPA) ratio of banks has declined to 10.8% in September 2018 from 11.5% in March 2018. Further stress tests undertaken by the RBI show that under its baseline scenario, the GNPA ratio may decline from 10.8% in September 2018 to 10.3% in March 2019.


                    (function(){
                      var adContainer = '<div id="div-gpt-ad-1485328452682222244" data-size="fluid" data-adsslot="TOI/TOI_ROS/TOI_ROS_AS/TOI_ROS_AS_Mrec_MID_300" class="webonly alC"></div>';
