It's another matter that the experts still can't decide if the 25-year-old is better off bowling with the newer or older piece of leather. But what needs to be highlighted is that coaches at the underage level didn't cajole or coerce Bumrah to change his odd bowling action, which is turning out to be his greatest strength.

The speedster, who is proving his worth, series after series, has picked 48 wickets after nine Tests in the last one year and that too at an impressive average of 21.02.

"His different bowling action is what caught my eye and even though many felt I need to work on his action, I didn't bother to change it as he was generating serious pace and leaving batsmen puzzled," says Kishore Trivedi (father of former Gujarat and Rajasthan Royals quick, Siddharth), Bumrah's first coach at Ahmedabad's Nirmal High School.

Incidentally, the bowler's mother was the school principal and Bumrah picked up the ropes bowling on the concrete pitch, laid out by the school to train the boys for inter-school matches. It's probably there where he picked the habit of banging the ball in.

Once Bumrah started picking bucketful of wickets at the school-level, the Gujarat Cricket Association didn't waste time to take him under its wings.

Vijay Patel, the coach under whom the GCA has gone from strength to strength in the last two decades, reiterates what Trivedi says.

"There have been minor adjustments here and there but nothing major. He also had a stint under Dennis Lillee at the MRF Pace Academy but there too his action was not changed," says the 59-yearold Patel.

"There were many experts who reckoned his action would get him into trouble with injuries because his bowling action puts certain parts of his body under extra pressure. But through biomechanics, we have learnt which part of his body needs to be strengthened and accordingly his training programme has been set to keep that part of the body strong," says Patel.

'

