b'PUNE: Kevin Anderson had what could be undeniably described as a successful last year. The South African world No. 6, one of the biggest servers in the game, reached the second major final of his career at Wimbledon, after which he touched a career-high No. 5 in rankings. He made it to the year-end Finals in London for the first time and pocketed his first ATP 500 title in Vienna.


Anderson defeated Roger Federer in the Wimbledon quarterfinals despite being down by two sets and facing a match point. He prevailed over John Isner in the Wimbledon semis 26-24 in the decider in a match lasting six hours 36 minutes that prompted the organisers to introduce tie-breaks at 12-12 in the deciding set from this year. The 32-year-old, returning to Pune for the Tata Open Maharashtra, opened up on various topics including the privileges Roger Federer enjoys in an exclusive conversation with TOI Sports team. 


