                      console.log(isIndia && randomNumber < 0.9 );
                      if(isIndia){
                        // code for showing ctn ad
                         $('#fluid-container').remove();
                         return;
                      }	
                      $('.top2brdiv').remove();
                      $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                    })()




Wakhare then sliced through the Mumbai batting line-up as no one showed that 'khadoos approach' and went after Vidarbha's bowling. The visitors lost three wickets each in the post lunch session and tea session to end Day 2 at 169-6, trailing by 342 run in their Ranji Trophy Elite Group 'A' tie here on Monday.

While Mumbai ended the day on a disappointing note, the start was good for them. They got rid of a well set Ganesh Satish (90, 160b, 5x4, 1x6) in the first hour. Satish missed the opportunity to score a hundred in his second successive match. Shardul Thakur trapped him in front of the wicket to end his fifth-wicket 95-run stand with Mohit Kale.

Vidarbha handed over the new ball to left-arm spinner Sarvate. He never allowed Mumbai's openers Jay Bista and Vikrant Auti to settle down. Soon, they introduced Wakhare into the attack, and he struck on his third ball, removing Auti with a fuller delivery. In the next over, Sarvate caught Mumbai skipper Siddhesh Lad inside the crease and in front of the wicket. If that was not enough, Wakhare got the big fish in his second over.

He castled Shreyas Iyer as the ball kept a little low and Iyer was playing on the backfoot. Three wickets in space of 14 balls put Mumbai in trouble. Bista (64, 111b, 6x4, 2x6) and Shubham Ranjane (52, 3x4, 5x6) then adopted an attacking approach. While Bista was flashing hard and taking too many chances, Ranjane was a bit calculative in his approach.

Ranjane took some time before launching Wakhare for two sixes over deep mid-wicket. He also hit Karnewar for a couple of sixes besides depositing Sarvate for the biggest six of the match. Vidarbha, all along kept patience, as they knew the duo will miss hit at some stage. Bista did that to Wakhare when long-on was perfectly placed. Wankhade completed a good running catch to break the 99-run stand.

Two overs later, Ranjane inside edged Wakhare to hand a double blow to Mumbai. The wicket of Aditya Tare at the fag end came as a bonus for Vidarbha.

'

b'MELBOURNE: The ICC has rated the MCG pitch used for the third India vs Australia Test as "average", very similar rating to the Perth Stadium track for the second Test.


According to a report in Sydney Morning Herald, "The Melbourne Cricket Club's bid to hold on to the Boxing Day Test has been boosted after the MCG was given an average rating by the International Cricket Council following India's 137-run win.


