            The liquidity deficit has seen a sustained increase during December\xe2\x80\x94from Rs 3,471 crore on Dec 4 to Rs 1,83,913 crore on December 26.

Incidentally, the RBI infused liquidity by way of open market operation (OMO) purchases to the tune of Rs 50000 crore during the month. The high liquidity deficit in the last week of December is being attributed to the higher growth in bank credit (6.7% during April 1-December 7), which has been exceeding growth in bank deposits (4%). In addition, liquidity was pressured due to the auction of government securities (Rs 25,846 crore) and the wedding and festive season, and year-end demand for funds. This can be evidenced from the increase in currency in circulation during the month.

The build-up in surplus cash balances of the central government with the RBI since the second week of December 2018 (Rs 56,794 crore as of December 28) has also been constraining the banking system\xe2\x80\x99s liquidity, data compiled by CARE Ratings showed. The call market rate ended the week at 6.33%, 12 bps or 0.12% lower than the previous week\xe2\x80\x99s close after it scaled a 14-week high of 6.58% on December 27.

The average daily borrowings in the call market during the week ended December 28 was 28% lower at Rs 15,535 crore compared with the average borrowings of Rs 21,278 crore in the previous week. The call rates and daily borrowings in the call money market were high during December 24-27 following which a sharp fall in both was observed on December 28.

Government borrowings (central and state governments), at Rs 25,846 crore, were markedly higher during the week. It was Rs 19,824 crore in the previous week. The benchmark 10- year G-Secs ended the week at 7.39%, 11 bps or 0.11% higher than the previous week\xe2\x80\x99s close. Concerns over fiscal slippages (with fiscal deficit surpassing the full year target in the first eight months) coupled with the liquidity deficit in the banking system and auction of government securities drove the movement in yields.


'

b'MUMBAI: Almost 80% of credit growth in the first eight months of the current fiscal has taken place between September and November. Much of the bank credit has gone towards personal loans and industry, with only the non-banking finance companies (NBFCs) showing a decline in bank loans. Besides NBFCs, the medium and small enterprises (MSEs) segment also saw bank credit shrinking. 


Within industry, food processing, fertiliser, iron & steel, cement, petroleum and infrastructure (roads & power) led the growth. Of the total incremental credit expansion of Rs 4.35 lakh crore in the current fiscal, Rs 3.46 lakh crore is accounted for in the three months ended November.


