                      $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                    })()



            The volatile, uncertain, complex and ambiguous (VUCA) universe of business heralds a chaotic \xe2\x80\x98new normal\xe2\x80\x99 that will change the face of human resources (HR) in coming years. It can no longer afford to be on the periphery if it wants to be a force of consequence. Getting a seat at the business table becomes redundant \xe2\x80\x94 it has to consider itself the business. Its tedious identity that vacillates between being a people and business function has to go. Unless HR sees itself as a prime mover of organisational capability, it will fast become obsolete. 

Tomorrow\xe2\x80\x99s HR will not see its effectiveness in theories or textbook manuals. It is best drawn from today\xe2\x80\x99s realities and business experiences. The HR of the future will be business experts who will design people solutions based on four powerful forces that we see sweeping the business landscape. From these causal forces will emerge the consequential fifth force of a re-imagined HR \xe2\x80\x94 an agile organisation with new structures, competencies, technologies and governance. HR professionals will organise themselves as niche players with relevant business stakeholders, supported by centres of expertise to design need-based programmes. They will demonstrate highly strategic business-enabling capabilities, and work hand-in-hand with enterprise leaders to co-create people solutions. They will make design-led thinking, advanced analytics and change management capabilities as \xe2\x80\x98must-have\xe2\x80\x99 skills. HR technologies (knowledge-based systems and \xe2\x80\x98software-as-a-service\xe2\x80\x99 platforms) will provide consumer-quality experiences to simplify and enhance efficiency for all stakeholders.

Let us have a closer look at the five forces of a rejuvenated HR.

Minding the gap for a new-age HR avatar: The emerging demands of HR are exacting. HR executives must speak like a CEO and coach like a CFO to add credible business value, break HR silos and drive the organisation\xe2\x80\x99s culture. As a highly business-savvy and HR technologist hybrid, the HR professional must acquire an in-depth understanding of business levers and organisational dynamics for \xe2\x80\x98equal eye\xe2\x80\x99 stakeholder discussions. And they must leap the gap in technology and analytical skills to have an agile, tech-savvy thought process. 

Additionally, the new HR must architect business systems to be a step ahead of their CEO. Differentiating between training for skills and coaching for capabilities, they should identify leadership gaps in a sustained manner and devise development strategies to close them.

Embracing a \xe2\x80\x98millennial-first\xe2\x80\x99 workforce: The millennial brigade today constitutes more than 20% of leadership positions. And is rapidly increasing too. Their audacious confidence, their innovative attitude towards new-gen technologies, and their fresh outlook to work are tremendous advantages in gearing business to meet disruptive demands. 

For HR in transition, this is a golden opportunity to establish themselves as agents of positive change through strong and proactive leadership.

Can the strategic HR authentically align millennial priorities with that of business? Can they harness the collaborative and critical workplace balance of the \xe2\x80\x98Gen X-Millennial\xe2\x80\x99 strengths?

Managing a \xe2\x80\x98day-after-tomorrow\xe2\x80\x99 workplace: The future space in which HR will operate makes interesting viewing. No more \xe2\x80\x98nine-to-five\xe2\x80\x99, full-time job norms. Liquid, flexible and gig working models with highly personalised user experiences. An open approach to collaboration. Data and technology as prime movers of business. 

Opportunity is the other side of challenge here. Through innovative employment contracts, HR can bring huge competitive advantages of a well-managed liquid workforce. The \xe2\x80\x98future-forward\xe2\x80\x99 HR will make strategic decisions on the mix of full-time employees, contractors and gig workers. Their blueprints of future scenarios for an ideal workforce mix will architect winning customer experiences.

Pushing the boundaries of human capabilities in the Industry 4.0 era: The fourth Industrial Revolution will radically change the way we live and work \xe2\x80\x94 in scale, scope and complexity. It may seem like a dehumanising development that could robotise people out of jobs. HR will need to act as an influencer to assert that there is, and there will be work for everyone despite automation. HR leaders need to be prime movers of collaboration that puts people first and empowers them with cognitive readiness and competencies.

A re-imagined HR as the ultimate consequential force: The four causal forces steer HR thinking to the pivotal question: Why should we look at HR as orbiting the business and not as a business itself?

When HR sees itself as the \xe2\x80\x98business\xe2\x80\x99, it will become primarily responsible to help people get to the core of their capabilities and address them with business insights. They become co-creators of solutions rather than siloed creators of tool kits.

In such a re-imagined role, HR truly becomes part owners of business. It empowers managers to deal with people, and people to manage their careers. The HR function will need to be a few steps ahead of business with an incisive capacity to understand their industries into the future, strategise and innovate, to execute with expertise.

The writer is MD and 

CEO, Randstad India. Views are personal


