            \xe2\x80\x9cFrom space tech to waste management to electric vehicles and more, our incubatee companies are solving some of the core challenges the world faces today,\xe2\x80\x9d Priya Mohan, senior incubation associate & communications manager at IIT-M IC, told TOI. Mohan expects 2019 to bring increased focus on intellectual property-led startups working on healthcare, environment, and other social impact areas that are successful in translating strong research into commercial potential.

TOI spoke to six IIT-M IC companies building disruptive solutions, for the world about what 2019 holds in store for them

1. Planys Technologies

With a founding team comprising three IIT-M alumni and two faculty members with background in robotics and underwater NDT (non-destructive testing), Planys wants to pioneer underwater asset inspections in the country. As a maker of underwater robots called Remotely Operated Vehicles (ROVs), Planys inspects and maintains underwater structures. The startup has developed three ROVs and completed over 45 projects across maritime, infrastructure (dams & bridges) and energy sectors, since 2014. \xe2\x80\x9cWith companies stressing on data-driven intervention and maintenance of assets, Planys is on the right track to realise its full commercial potential,\xe2\x80\x9d Krishnan B, Head, professor centre for non destructive evaluation (CNDE) at IIT-M, said.

What\xe2\x80\x99s in store

Co-founder and CEO Tanuj Jhunjhunwala expects the second leg of the Union government\xe2\x80\x99s dam rehabilitation and improvement project to be a key driver for Planys\xe2\x80\x99 growth in 2019. \xe2\x80\x9cIndia has around 5,000 dams, and we hope to play a key role in inspecting and rehabilitating these structures in the years ahead,\xe2\x80\x9d he said.

2. Uniphore Systems

Uniphore is perhaps one of IIT-M IC\xe2\x80\x99s biggest and earliest success stories \xe2\x80\x94 an instance of \xe2\x80\x98built in India, built for the world\xe2\x80\x99 model. Founded in 2008, the Chennai-based conversational Aritificial Intelligence (AI) enterprise has developed three speech recognition products \xe2\x80\x94 auMina for speech analytics, Akeira for voice-based customer assistance, and amVoice, a voice biometric security solution. Serving more than 100 clients and with presence in the US and Singapore, Uniphore received crucial validation in 2017 when Cisco\xe2\x80\x99s former chairman John Chambers came in as a lead investor, joining the existing group of prolific investors such as IDG Ventures, IIFL, and Kris Gopalakrishnan, among others. 

What\xe2\x80\x99s in store

Umesh Sachdev, co-founder and CEO, Uniphore, believes that we can see \xe2\x80\x9ctransformative business impacts\xe2\x80\x9d of the key research Uniphore has been working on in the fields of AI and natural language processing, in 2019. \xe2\x80\x9cWe have managed to develop some of the world\xe2\x80\x99s best performing products with our core engineering in Chennai. We projected to get to $100 mn in recurring revenue in 3 years and we are on course to get there,\xe2\x80\x9d he said.

3. Agnikul Cosmos

Engineers and space enthusiasts Srinath Ravichandran and Moin SPM believe India, with its rich space history and expertise is the best place for them to find success with their startup Agnikul Cosmos. Designing and building launch vehicles capable of carrying up to 100 kg in weight to lower earth orbits, Agnikul wants to provide smaller space operators a viable launch option. As one among 24 global startups selected by Airbus for its in-house accelerator program, the Chennai enterprise stands to gain global opportunities. \xe2\x80\x9cWith 3D printing tech levelling the playing field in manufacturing sector, products such as those of Agnikul will make smaller space launches a reality, bringing rocketing to the common man,\xe2\x80\x9d SR Chakravarthy, professor at dept of aerospace engineering, IIT- M, said.

What\xe2\x80\x99s in store

Increased investor interest in deep tech startups, mainstreaming of space businesses, and growth of additive manufacturing are a few inflection points Moin SPM, co-founder and COO of Agnikul, looks forward to in 2019. \xe2\x80\x9cWe are also excited about the first-level sub-scale testing of our product that we intend to conduct in the second quarter of 2019,\xe2\x80\x9d he said.

4. Swadha Energies

As air conditioners graduate from being a luxury to a necessity, energy pressures of the appliance continue to be a concern. Swadha Energies wants to tackle this by building an intelligent system, which when attached to regular ACs, makes them smarter to enable energy savings and optimise cooling. Using sensors tech and other hardware, Swadha offers a better cooling experience in centralised AC settings, and facilitates savings of close to 30% for users. Emerging from a problem statement offered by IIT\xe2\x80\x93M to engineer and founder Ankit Poddar (who joined as a project associate), Swadha Energies today works with clients such as TATA Group, HDFC, Blue Star and others.

What\xe2\x80\x99s in store

\xe2\x80\x9cWe look forward to 2019 as being the year when we incorporate AI and machine learning capabilities in our product to make it smarter,\xe2\x80\x9d said Poddar, expecting energy consumption and sustainability to take centre-stage this year.

5. Zasti

Zasti is an AI platform using proprietary deep learning algorithms to predict behaviours and offer solutions across sectors. Founded by three friends, who quit their cushy corporate jobs to be a part of the AI revolution, Zasti was incubated by IIT-M\xe2\x80\x99s Health Tech Incubation Cell (HTIC), and has filed applications for five patents. Among a few early use cases of the algorithm can be seen in simplifying glaucoma testing, predicting survival rates of pancreatic cancer patients, and in solving business problems in verticals such as aviation and logistics.

What\xe2\x80\x99s in store

\xe2\x80\x9cIn 2019, the definition of AI will move from artificial intelligence to augmented intelligence,\xe2\x80\x9d Ramanathan Srinivasan, co-founder and chief strategy officer, said. \xe2\x80\x9cAI is not going to replace humans, but is going to help us achieve 10 times more efficiency in our lives,\xe2\x80\x9d he added, noting that Zasti is on track to take advantage of this inflection point.

6. DocsWallet

DocsWallet predicted the push for digital, paperless governance that has engulfed economies around the world today, back in 2013, when it launched digital locker services to electronically store and share documents like identity proofs and educational certificates. The startup was also equally quick to capitalise on the blockchain boom, getting into educational certificates and transcripts verification in 2016. Developed in Chennai, the startup has found early adoption in the UAE, garnering attention and funding from Abu Dhabi-based organisations. \xe2\x80\x9cDocsWallet\xe2\x80\x99s product is a proven solution amongst educational institutions, and as governments go electronic, any independent and reliable validation of document is possible with DocsWallet,\xe2\x80\x9d L S Ganesh, professor at dept of management studies, IIT-M, said.

What\xe2\x80\x99s in store

The growing calls for digitization of governance and increasing buzz around blockchain make the upcoming year a ripe one for DocsWallet\xe2\x80\x99s growth. \xe2\x80\x9cThe recent announcement of producing only electronic copy of driver\xe2\x80\x99s license, and the growing focus on verifying credentials digitally on-the-go make us look forward to 2019,\xe2\x80\x9d Avira Tharakan, CEO, DocsWallet, said.



