The PM also rejected the opposition charge that Patel was forced to resign because of differences with the government on a host of issues including the transfer of surplus reserves of the central bank.


Read: Full text of PM Modi's interview

Patel, the 24th governor of the central bank, resigned last month citing personal reasons. His resignation came against the backdrop of a spat between the RBI and the government including the issue of central bank autonomy. The government has appointed Shaktikanta Das, a former economic affairs secretary, as the new governor.

The PM also rejected the opposition charge that Patel was forced to resign because of differences with the government on a host of issues including the transfer of surplus reserves of the central bank.

"No such question arises. I acknowledge that Patel did a good job as RBI governor,\xe2\x80\x9d Modi said reiterating his praise for Patel. He also said Congress party had forced governors to leave before their term expired and the party had called the Planning Commission \xe2\x80\x9ca bunch of jokers\xe2\x80\x9d. \xe2\x80\x9c Do you remember who the vice-chairman was?\xe2\x80\x9d he asked.

The PM, who spoke on a number of issues linked to economic policy said the Centre wants to bring construction related materials in the 5% GST slab and reiterated the view that changes to the tax regime will continue as there is room for improvement.

Modi said the government wants to put material used for construction, both for under construction and fully constructed houses, under 5% slab, similar to what has been done in the case of restaurants.

"But there were reservations by some. So the GST Council could not do it. It has now gone to the committee. We will try to ensure that the committee report is expedited," the PM said.

"So, continuously, we have been trying to make GST easy and ensure that interests of the consumers are safeguarded. In such a short time, in such a big nation, whatever has been achieved is not small... There is scope for improvement. We will continue to make changes," said Modi.

The PM said the GST was a new system and was ushering in a "big transformation" using technology. 

"So some small traders have faced inconvenience. We acknowledge this. But the government's responsibility is to be sensitive to their concerns. So, whatever comes to our notice, are referred to the GST Council. We decide collectively how to simplify (GST)," he said.

Modi said the loan waivers unveiled by Congress-led governments in some states as \xe2\x80\x9cpolitical stunts.\xe2\x80\x9d He said his government was committed to empowering farmers.

The PM said majority of farmers resort to loans from money lenders and only a fraction of them access bank loans and those farmers who depend on money lenders are left out of the purview of farm loans.

"(So) when governments make such announcements (of loan waivers), those farmers ( who take loans from money lenders) do not become beneficiaries of the waivers. The farmers who are dying are out of the purview of such schemes," Modi said.

v
'

