b'Coimbatore: Madhya Pradesh (MP) and Rajasthan may find it difficult to carry out the farm loan waivers in a single year due to fiscal constraints. Madhya Pradesh, which has announced a waiver of about Rs 38,000 crore, has already breached its fiscal deficit target of 3% in 2018-19 (FY19). Rajasthan that has announced a waiver amounting to nearly Rs 18,000 crore has already reached the 3% target in FY19.

\xe2\x80\x9cMP and Rajasthan will find it difficult to carry out these waivers in a single year and (they) will (have to) be spread over a couple of years. Here too, there would be compromises made along the way if the entire money is to be spent,\xe2\x80\x9d Madan Sabnavis, chief economist and Sushant Hede, associate economist at CARE Ratings said. \xe2\x80\x9cThese schemes will presumably be provided for in FY20 (2019-20) rather than FY19 because as per CARE Ratings analysis, it shows that for FY19 the ability of states to accommodate such expenses is constrained by FRBM (Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management Act),\xe2\x80\x9d they said.\xe2\x80\x9cChattisgarh is better placed but will also have to do it over at least two years provided all other factors remain unchanged. In short, given the FRBM norms, loan waivers do not look possible if conditions are not relaxed. This also means that these allocations are unlikely to fully materialise and will be done in a piece meal manner,\xe2\x80\x9d the economists stated.



