            \xe2\x80\x9cRajasthan and Madhya Pradesh have low additional fiscal space in comparison to the announced farm loan waiver assuming an unchanged growth in FY20 and the fiscal deficit ratio in FY19 (Budget Estimates) continuing in FY20,\xe2\x80\x9d Sabnavis and Hede said. \xe2\x80\x9cBoth Rajasthan and Madhya Pradesh will at least take 3-4 years to fully implement the loan waivers. Chattisgarh appears to be relatively better placed as the scheme announced is of a smaller magnitude,\xe2\x80\x9d they said.

\xe2\x80\x9cBarring Maharashtra, other states which have announced farm loan waivers will have to walk a tight fiscal path if they have to implement the loan waivers at the earliest,\xe2\x80\x9d Sabnavis and Hede said. \xe2\x80\x9cStates like Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, Tamil Nadu and Chhattisgarh have additional fiscal space, but the amount is a small proportion of the farm loan waiver announced. Hence, this indicates that the farm loan waiver implementation will take 2-3 years,\xe2\x80\x9d the economists said.



