b'MUMBAI: Housing finance companies (HFCs) will see slower growth and lose market share to banks in FY19 due to tighter liquidity conditions. Also, they will need to raise Rs 1.7-1.9 lakh crore in the current fiscal to meet their 15% growth target. According to ratings agency ICRA, bad loans in HFCs are expected to increase as more finance is extended to self-employed and in the construction segment. The total of all home loans outstanding in India is estimated at Rs 17.8 lakh crore as of end-September 2018 \xe2\x80\x94 a 17% increase over the same period in the previous year. This is an average of the 18% growth rate recorded by HFCs and 16% by banks.


One of the growth drivers in HFCs was the non-housing portfolio (includes loans to builders), which grew 29% year-on-year. Incidentally, loans to builders, which are around 19% of HFCs\xe2\x80\x99 portfolio, have had higher levels of non-performing assets (NPAs) at about 2.2%.


