The brokers have also been given the option to pay Rs 1 lakh with a commitment of Rs 5 lakh in total as and when required. The total mobilisations could rise in excess of Rs 15 crore. 

The letter mentions four recent instances that the two bodies believe are attempts to \xe2\x80\x9cmalign the image of the brokers\xe2\x80\x9d. First, NSEL had petitioned the Bombay high court seeking attachment of properties of all the brokers who were registered with the spot exchange that defaulted under questionable circumstances in August 2013. Second, the government\xe2\x80\x99s Serious Fraud Investigation Office (SFIO), which probed the NSEL scam, has reportedly said that NSEL brokers had lured clients to trade on the exchange. 

The markets regulator has also served show-cause notices on 300 brokers for their role in the scam. And lastly, Sebi has also filed an FIR with Mumbai police\xe2\x80\x99s Economic Offences Wing (EOW) for violations of foreign contribution laws by some of those involved in the scam, including the brokers. 

Members of the two associations feel that while large members can fight the legal battle on their own, it is the medium and small-size members who need to join hands to fight their cases. According to BBF chairman Uttam Bagri, \xe2\x80\x9cThe true masterminds of the scam are using their financial clout and are attempting to shift the blame on brokers. The associations are, therefore, assisting small and medium brokers for obtaining good legal counsel.\xe2\x80\x9d

According to top officials of the two associations, these bodies will help only those brokers in their legal fight who are slotted to receive payments from NSEL while those which have defaulted on payments to the exchange will not be helped. \xe2\x80\x9cThe associations have come together to assist and fight the false charges levelled upon brokers who are themselves victims of the scam as much their clients,\xe2\x80\x9d said Rajesh Baheti, national president, ANMI.

The brokers are being helped since internal estimates show that if they fight their cases individually, the costs for each of them could shoot up to Rs 50 lakh, a top official with a broker\xe2\x80\x99s body said. So the associations are asking them to create a war chest to fight their legal battle as well as to spend on public relations and other related issues jointly, the official said.

'

