Part of the reason for the export boom is the Indian market\xe2\x80\x99s alignment with global demand in terms of radial tyre production. 

\xe2\x80\x9cIndian production has become radial-focussed which is also what the rest of the world needs,\xe2\x80\x9d added Sharma. 

The rupee depreciation also helped though it pinched on the raw material import front, he added. The export boom has also given Indian tyre companies greater exposure in the car segment whereas 65% of the domestic revenue comes from truck tyres due to higher price and wear and tear. Tyre makers have have invested nearly $7 bn in creation of additional capacities. \xe2\x80\x9cAn investment of nearly Rs 51,000 crore ($7 billion) has just been completed or is currently lined up in both greenfield and brownfield expansion,\xe2\x80\x9d said an ATMA official. \xe2\x80\x9cCurrently about 2% of the revenue is being invested on R&D by Indian tyre industry,\xe2\x80\x9d said Anant Goenka, chairman, ATMA.

Tyres made here are being exported to more than 100 countries with US topping the export destination list accounting for 13% of total export turnover. Germany is the second largest with more than 7% share. Bangladesh, Netherlands, France and Philippines all account for 4% each. Brazil, Italy and Nepal contribute 3% each to the pie.

\xe2\x80\x9cThe industry is expected to end the current fiscal with an export turnover of Rs 12,000 crore as order book position is healthy,\xe2\x80\x9d said Goenka. \xe2\x80\x9cIn 2007-08, tyre production numbers stood at nearly 80 million units which has increased to nearly 180 million units in FY 2017-18,\xe2\x80\x9d he added.


'

b'MUMBAI: Ahead of drawing up their results for the third quarter, lenders \xe2\x80\x94 through the Indian Banks\xe2\x80\x99 Association (IBA) \xe2\x80\x94 have written to regulator RBI, seeking relaxation in bad loan classification norms on their exposure to IL&FS. The beleaguered infrastructure and financing group, which has Rs 60,000-crore bank loans, has defaulted in nearly Rs 35,000 crore of that after a court-ordered moratorium on cash outflows to creditors prevented the company from making repayments.


According to bankers, the National Company Law Tribunal (NCLT) order placing a moratorium on all repayments amounts to a force majeure event. They said that unlike in any normal default when banks can move in and seize and sell assets or initiate bankruptcy proceedings, the court order imposes a standstill on recovery action. \xe2\x80\x9cWe want banks to be exempted from classifying the loans as NPAs until the moratorium is lifted or until banks can initiate recovery proceedings,\xe2\x80\x9d said a senior official with a public sector bank.


