Given that banks have an exposure of Rs 60,000 crore, they will need to make provisions of around Rs 9,000 crore by March, when the fiscal year ends, if they are not granted relief. The IBA is expected to raise this issue in their meeting with the newly-appointed RBI governor Shaktikanta Das on January 14.

The new board of IL&FS, headed by Uday Kotak, has already initiated the process of selling various businesses. The board has sought expressions of interest for various firms, including those in financial services, energy, roads and education. Sources in IL&FS say that a substantial portion of the debt will be recovered, but it is likely that banks may have to take a small haircut.

Besides a dispensation on their loans to IL&FS, the IBA is seeking relief on several other issues on behalf of banks (see graphic). Many of these are old issues but they are hopeful that the new governor will be more receptive to their representation. One of them is the contentious February 12, 2018 circular tightening the norms on defaulters. Lenders have made a strong pitch for doing away with the one-day default condition, whereby a borrower is classified as an NPA if he misses the final deadline. Lenders say that in India, where many government departments delay payments, it is difficult to enforce this rule.

Other issues on which the banks have asked the RBI to relax rules are the central bank\xe2\x80\x99s directive to replace all magnetic stripe-only debit cards with chip-and-pin cards. Banks also want the RBI to soften its stand on cash management for ATMs. According to RBI\xe2\x80\x99s new guidelines, banks should replenish cash in ATMs using the cassette-swap method, where cash vans will transport locked canisters containing cash. These canisters or cassettes, as they are called, will release cash only when placed inside the ATMs.


'

