\xe2\x80\x9cIncrease in conversion of NPAs (non-performing asset) into standard accounts and decline in new accounts falling in the NPA category show a definite improvement in the lending and borrowing behaviour,\xe2\x80\x9d the FM said, while praising the two years of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code (IBC).

He said the NCLT has become a trusted forum of high credibility.

\xe2\x80\x9cThose who drive the companies to insolvency, exit from management. The selection of new management has been an honest and transparent process. There has been no political or governmental interference in the cases,\xe2\x80\x9d the FM said.

He said the recoveries of funds parked in insolvent companies have taken place through three methods: First, after the introduction of Section 29 (A), such companies are paying up in anticipation of not crossing the red line and being referred to NCLT.

\xe2\x80\x9cAs a result, the banks have started receiving monies from potential debtors who pay in anticipation of the default. The defaulters know well that once they get into IBC they will surely be out of management because of Section 29 (A).\xe2\x80\x9d

Also, the FM said that once a petition of the creditor is filed before the NCLT, many debtors have been paying at the pre-admission stage so that the declaration of insolvency does not take place. \xe2\x80\x9cThird, many major insolvency cases have already been resolved and many are on the way of resolving. Those which cannot be resolved move towards liquidation and the banks are receiving the liquidation value,\xe2\x80\x9d the FM said. 

