Sources said the APA was signed in December 2016 for the period from April 2009 to March 2018. The APA pertained to IBM\xe2\x80\x99s export of IT services from India. Under the agreement, IBM India agreed to increase the rates for the services rendered to its entities in the US, UK and other countries for this entire period. This meant higher revenues in each of those years. And since costs remained the same, profits in each year rose. And the I-T department got more taxes.




The company incurred additional taxes of Rs 1,167 crore for the period, sources told TOI. An email sent to IBM did not elicit a response till the time of going to print.

APAs are signed in order to avoid disputes on transfer pricing. For the company, it brings predictability on taxes. Infosys last year signed an APA with the US Internal Revenue Service (IRS). IBM India is said to be negotiating a new APA, since the last agreement expired in March last year. 

IBM India has four primary business segments \xe2\x80\x93 hardware, software, financing, and sale of services (including domestic IT services and export IT services). Export services accounts for about 65-70% of total revenue. But IBM is also one of the biggest IT providers in the India market. It has over the years won some large deals from Bharti Airtel, Vodafone India and Idea Cellular. More recently, it signed a 10-year IT outsourcing deal with Aditya Birla Fashion and Retail (ABFRL) to manage the latter\xe2\x80\x99s IT infrastructure through IT systems, employee productivity through self-help solutions, and improve disaster resilience.

