The action on the field resembled a cat-and-mouse game. India lost an early wicket but every time they recovered through a decent partnership, they threw one away to keep Australia on their tail. When Australia dismissed Ajinkya Rahane, the game was truly in balance with India at 228 for 4. But a fabulous unfinished 75-run partnership for the fifth wicket between Vihari and Pujara helped India win the final session.



India and Australia made two changes apiece. The drama over R Ashwin ended tamely after the off-spinner failed to clear a fitness test before the toss. India included left-arm chinaman bowler Kuldeep Yadav in place of Ishant Sharma, while KL Rahul returned to the top of the order in place of Rohit Sharma. Australia brought in Peter Handscomb and Marnus Labuschagne for Aaraon Finch and Mitchell Marsh, respectively.

Rahul failed to make the god-sent opportunity count. The out-of-form opener scored nine runs off edges, before nicking Josh Hazlewood\xe2\x80\x99s third delivery of the day to Shaun Marsh at first slip. It was the fourth time in the series that Rahul had succumbed to Hazlewood.

But young Mayank Agarwal appeared to continue the form he had displayed on his Test debut in Melbourne. Hazlewood and Mitchell Starc bowled lively spells with the new ball, but along with Pat Cummins, were guilty of overdoing the short-pitched stuff. Pujara was struck on his helmet, while Agarwal copped a blow on his arm, but both batted on without much fuss.

Australia captain Tim Paine was quick to review a caught-behind decision against Pujara off Cummins when the batsman was on 12, but it didn\xe2\x80\x99t yield the desired result.

Agarwal, playing only his second Test, reached his halfcentury after lunch by punching Starc through cover and followed it up with another boundary \xe2\x80\x94 a pull off Cummins. He then twice danced down the pitch and lifted Lyon over the boundary rope at longon before perishing for 77 \xe2\x80\x94 his highest score \xe2\x80\x94 while trying to repeat the stroke.



Kohli, who walked in to a mixture of cheers and boos from the SCG crowd, looked in his zone. He began with a masterly cover drive off Hazlewood before dispatching Cummins to the boundary twice.

Pujara, on the other hand, took full toll of Labuschagne\xe2\x80\x99s part-time leg-spin by smacking him for three boundaries \xe2\x80\x94 the first of which brought up his half-century. Just when the two batsmen were beginning to assert themselves, Kohli fell, gloving a short ball from Hazlewood down the leg side to Paine.

Pujara and Ajinkya Rahane made sure that there was no collapse. But when Rahane fell to a snorter from Starc, there was unease in the camp, but Hanuma Vihari, batting at No.6, stroked a fearless 39*.


'

