b'SYDNEY: There is a certain sameness about Cheteshwar Pujara\xe2\x80\x99s batting that doesn\xe2\x80\x99t always appeal to your senses but rates very high on the utility scale. It was the same in Adelaide. Melbourne, too, had witnessed something similar last week. On Thursday, there was another exhibition at the Sydney Cricket Ground (SCG).


Pujara\xe2\x80\x99s unbeaten 130, made over six hours, not only gave India\xe2\x80\x99s first innings a solid foundation but also frustrated the Australian bowling attack which toiled hard. It was unadorned but certainly not unsurprising because it spurred Mayank Agarwal and later Hanuma Vihari to rise to the occasion.


