A spokesperson for RCom declined to comment.

Ericsson has already scaled down its dues towards Anil\xe2\x80\x99s group from Rs 1,600 crore to Rs 550 crore as part of a court-monitored settlement where it was originally promised to be paid by September 30 last year. However, troubles for RCom \xe2\x80\x94 which has run up a cumulative debt of nearly Rs 45,000 crore \xe2\x80\x94 have been compounding despite Anil striking a deal with elder brother Mukesh\xe2\x80\x99s telecom venture Jio. While the company has received around Rs 5,000 crore through the sale of fiber and media convergence node (MCN) set-up, the bigger deal \xe2\x80\x94 estimated at Rs 15,000 crore through sale of spectrum and towers \xe2\x80\x94 still hangs in balance over payment of a bank guarantee.

With the bigger deal looking difficult to pass, and money collected from the previous sale parked in an escrow account controlled by the banks, Anil has been unable to pay Ericsson despite giving specific assurances for the same. Ericsson had last year petitioned the Supreme Court to bar Anil and two of his senior executives from leaving the country. Anil\xe2\x80\x99s lawyers have said that they are waiting for the passage of the larger deal before lenders can be paid.

The Swedish company claimed that it is left without any protection even while RCom\xe2\x80\x99s assets have been sold and money received. 

