Interestingly, US-based companies emerged as the top acquirers of Indian firms in terms of value and number of announced deals. Acquisitions by US-based companies in India totalled $22.7 billion in 2018, up 273.5% from a year ago, and accounted for 40.6% of India\xe2\x80\x99s inbound M&A activity. This was largely due to US-based Walmart Inc\xe2\x80\x99s acquisition of Flipkart Group, a provider of online e-commerce retail services, from Softbank and Naspers, for $16 billion in 2018. Luxembourg, UK and Canada followed behind with 12.2%, 9.1% and 7.7% market share, respectively.




Domestic M&A hit record levels to $57.3 billion, more than double the value from over a year ago, and surpassed annual all-time high of $26.7 billion achieved in 2017. The number of announced domestic deals was up 17.1% compared to last year. Total cross-border M&A reached $69.2 billion, up 102.9% from last year. Inbound M&A activity increased 77% from a year ago, while outbound M&A activity grew five-times in value from last year. The average M&A deal size for transactions with disclosed values increased to $127.8 million in 2018 compared to $82.8 million in 2017, data compiled by Thomson Reuters India showed. India-involvement M&A activity this year witnessed five mega deals of above $5 billion, with a combined value of $39.8 billion, compared to only one during the same time-frame last year when the $11.6 billion Idea-Vodafone merger was announced. Completed M&A deals involving Indian companies saw the best-ever annual period and totalled $85.1 billion, up 40.3% y-o-y. The value of foreign firms acquiring Indian companies reached $55.8 billion in 2018, a 77% increase in deal value after surpassing annual record of $31.5 billion in 2017. Private equity-backed M&A in India increased 72.5% y-o-y to $13.1 billion in 2018.

Overseas acquisitions made by Indian companies stood at $13.4 billion, a 418.4% y-o-y increase in value, as number of outbound deals grew 13% compared to 2017. This is the highest annual period for India outbound M&A deals since 2010 when the value of such deals touched $29.1 billion.

The Walmart deal to acquire Flipkart propelled the retail sector to capture 33.1% of India\xe2\x80\x99s inbound M&A activity for a total of $18.5 billion, a significant increase compared to last year\xe2\x80\x99s $1.7 billion. Materials and healthcare sectors followed behind in inbound M&A activity with 17.2% and 12.3% market share, respectively. The materials sector accounted for majority of the acquisitions involving Indian companies with 21.9% market share worth $28.3 billion, more than a twelve-times increase in deal value compared to 2017, making it the highest-ever period for the sector in terms of value.

Three deals above $3-billion targeting materials sector involving India were announced this year. Financial services sector was placed second with 15.3% market share as deal value reached $19.8 billion, up 74.9% on a y-o-y basis.



'

b'MUMBAI: The onset of spring this year would usher in a celebration for the world of advertising in God\xe2\x80\x99s own country. The International Advertising Association (IAA) \xe2\x80\x94 a global organisation representing interests of the marketing, media and communications industry \xe2\x80\x94 will hold its first ever World Congress in India. The last three such events were conducted in Washington DC, Moscow and Beijing.

Spread over three days from February 20-22 in Kochi, this IAA marque event will see an eclectic mix of thought leaders, spiritual gurus, entertainers, domain experts and industry professionals discussing the future. 



