             \xe2\x80\x9cThe EBITDA margin for our OEM (ex-JLR) universe is likely to contract 140 bsp y-o-y (70 bsp q-o-q) to 12.6%, impacted by raw material inflation, currency depreciation and higher variable marketing expenses. We expect operating performance for most of the auto OEMs to bottom out in 3QFY19,\xe2\x80\x9d said Gautam Duggad, head of research, Motilal Oswal Securities.Analysts say car and SUV makers are likely to be most impacted by the margin squeeze. \xe2\x80\x9cThere will definitely be a decline in margins are operating leverage diminishes because of low volumes. Overall passenger vehicle companies will be affected the most followed by commercial vehicle companies and then two wheeler makers,\xe2\x80\x9d said Aditya Bapat, analyst, IIFL Securities. \xe2\x80\x9cThe raw material pressure, which was not that evident in Q2, will have the majority impact in Q3 with the low cost inventory getting stocked out. To top it up there was huge discounting so operating leverage will be less,\xe2\x80\x9d another analyst said.The third quarter of the current fiscal saw a sharp dip in volumes across segments as vehicle ownership costs rose due to high fuel prices, increased insurance costs and an increase in the cost of financing along with rural market stress. However, analysts expect the fourth quarter to be better as demand picks up speed. \xe2\x80\x9cThere may be a recovery from February as the inventory rationalization going on currently will show effect from then on,\xe2\x80\x9d said Bapat. \xe2\x80\x9cWith fuel prices softening, liquidity situation improving and new product launches, there may be normalization of demand from January onwards which will impact margins too,\xe2\x80\x9d added Duggad. However, he added, the Q3 numbers, though lower, should be in the same 11%-14% range as the last 5-6 quarters.Steel prices have started correcting so the raw material squeeze is easing up. Most car/SUV makers have already taken a price hike. And the interest rates are not likely rise now. \xe2\x80\x9cAll these indications point to a better Q4 compared to the Q3 but y-o-y it may still be flat as last Q4 was a bumper year,\xe2\x80\x9d said an analyst. '

b'MUMBAI: With two government investigating agencies, along with markets regulator Sebi, closing in on the alleged perpetrators of the Rs 5,600-crore National Spot Exchange (NSEL) scam, brokers and the spot exchange have both moved the Prime Minister\xe2\x80\x99s Office (PMO) with opposing demands. 


Deven Choksey, MD of broking house K R Choksey Shares & Securities, has written to the PM, alleging a witch hunt by investigating agencies and the regulator who have filed complaints against some of the brokers for their role in the NSEL scam. Meanwhile, Prakash Chaturvedi, MD of NSEL, has countered Choksey\xe2\x80\x99s claims, saying such attempts by brokers are misleading and the law as well as the investigation should take its own course without any external influence.


