The letter from Choksey, who has written it as an investor affected by the NSEL scam, comes after a series of recent developments. The Serious Fraud Investigation Office (SFIO), which probes white-collar crimes, has said that brokers on NSEL had lured clients to trade on the spot exchange for quick gains. Markets regulator Sebi has also served show-cause notices to 300 brokers for their role in the scam. Sebi has also filed an FIR with the Mumbai police\xe2\x80\x99s Economic Offences Wing (EOW) for violations of laws governing foreign contributions by those involved in the scam, including some of the NSEL brokers. 

\xe2\x80\x9cI am an investor and awaiting my dues for the last five years. I am aware that my dues can only be recovered by selling assets of defaulted borrowers. And accusing brokers and asking them to pay for it must be stopped,\xe2\x80\x9d Choksey wrote to the PM. \xe2\x80\x9cI must get (the money) back from those who have siphoned off my funds.\xe2\x80\x9d He also said that there should not be any threat in resolving the crisis that was more to do with the failure of the market system. 

\xe2\x80\x9cRather than accusing stakeholders, it should be a collaborative approach to bring up a solution,\xe2\x80\x9d he said. And if it comes to assigning responsibility for the scam, all the five stakeholders should be hauled up. These are the ministry of corporate affairs (MCA), which issued a licence to NSEL to operate the exchange, the Forward Market Commission (FMC), which has since been merged with Sebi and which allowed the exchange to operate, all the banks that acted as clearing houses for the trades, the state governments, and the tax authorities which collected taxes on NSEL trades. 

In its letter to the PM, the NSEL said that the letter by Choksey could create a wrong impression about the crisis and divert the investigation process. It also pointed out that when, during the initial days of the crisis in late 2013 and 2014, NSEL itself had moved the court to work out a solution jointly with all stakeholders, the same was \xe2\x80\x9cvehemently opposed\xe2\x80\x9d by investor forums and brokers. \xe2\x80\x9cEver since the payment default occurred (in August 2013), the brokers have used every trick...to steer and drive all investigations, and blame the NSEL and its promoters\xe2\x80\x9d for every problem and wrongdoings, Chaturvedi wrote. 

It requested the PM to not fall for dubious claims and misrepresentations by brokers and ensure there is no \xe2\x80\x9cinterference in the ongoing regulatory, law enforcement and legal actions\xe2\x80\x9d against the brokers who had a role in the NSEL scam.


'

b'Chennai: Despite a seasonally weak period for the Information Technology (IT) sector, there is optimism about their performance in the December quarter, with analysts predicting close to 9% year-on-year revenue growth, compared to the average 6.5% growth reported by companies during the same quarter last year.The December quarter is traditionally dominated by furloughs and experiences slower momentum due to the holiday season, but analysts TOI spoke to say they are bullish about the earnings trend. Transformational deal wins reported by the IT companies will increase revenue, and all eyes are on the calendar year 2019\xe2\x80\x99s IT budget trends across sectors, they said.

