            \xe2\x80\x9cYear-on-year growth is expected to accelerate despite seasonality thanks to the steady order bookings by companies in the past two quarters,\xe2\x80\x9d Ashish Chopra, senior VP (research), Motilal Oswal, said.Motilal Oswal\xe2\x80\x99s recent research note on the sector estimates 8.9% year-on-year growth in cross currency terms, and the brokerage house expects TCS to lead the pack with 11.7% y-o-y growth, and Tech Mahindra to lag with 4.7% growth. Infosys is expected to post a 9% increase in December quarter, compared to 8.1% it reported in the previous quarter.Edelweiss Research estimates the top-5 IT players \xe2\x80\x94 TCS, Infosys, Wipro, HCL Tech and Tech Mahindra- to clock 2\xe2\x80\x933.3% sequential revenue growth in the sluggish quarter. \xe2\x80\x9cDespite digital gaining heft, we expect growth rates to further accelerate yoy as enterprises move towards implementation from the current testing/proof of concept phase,\xe2\x80\x9d research analyst Sandip Agarwal writes in the note.Even as depreciation of major global currencies against the US dollar is likely to hurt revenue, the rupee\xe2\x80\x99s depreciation against the dollar is expected to lift margins for the IT players.In terms of cross-currency impact, the top tier IT players may experience cross-currency headwinds of 40\xe2\x80\x9370 bps (100 bps = 1%), as per Edelweiss. The USD appreciated vis-\xc3\xa0-vis the global currencies this quarter, rising 1.3% versus the Pound, and 1.9% versus the Euro. \xe2\x80\x9cAt the same time, the rupee\xe2\x80\x99s depreciation against the USD would lift margins 40\xe2\x80\x9370 bps q-o-q,\xe2\x80\x9d the note said. During the quarter, the rupee, on an average, depreciated 2.7% against the dollar. Return of cash to shareholders is also a key theme, with TCS, Infosys, Wipro, and others hinting at share buy-backs to make better usage of cash balances.The industry is also watching for macro factors that are likely to upset things for the sector. Slowdown of US market, escalation of the US-China trade war, and immediate ramifications of Brexit are a few factors that may thwart continuous growth into FY 19.\xe2\x80\x9cThere is a bit of cautiousness among companies about macros, and a few of them have already called out challenges about upcoming demands,\xe2\x80\x9d Abhishek Shindadkar, research analyst, Equirus Securities, said. Besides commentaries about client\xe2\x80\x99s IT budgets, the spend patterns among clients in the US and UK markets would be key to watch, he added.'

