No country has ever posted a 600-plus score in the first innings in a Test and lost. Bangladesh, though, have the dubious distinction of losing to New Zealand after posting 595/8 declared in their first innings of the Wellington Test in January 2017.

No match is over till the last ball is bowled, but the manner in which this one is poised, it looks like India\xe2\x80\x99s maiden series win in Australia is on the cards. It remains to be seen whether the final scoreline is 2-1 or 3-1 in India\xe2\x80\x99s favour.

It will require a Herculean effort from the Aussies \xe2\x80\x94 who folded twice in two days at the MCG \xe2\x80\x94 to first make sure that their team avoids the follow-on before they get down to tackling the bigger task of saving the match. To begin with, their batsmen have to take a leaf out of Cheteshwar Pujara\xe2\x80\x99s book and bat through the entire third day without losing too many wickets. For a team that has been forced to toil almost two whole days in the field under the scorching sun, it will be a huge challenge. And to hold firm against an Indian attack that has picked all 20 wickets in each of the three Tests so far is an even more difficult proposition.

However, unlike in Melbourne where the pitch had variable bounce that batsmen couldn\xe2\x80\x99t trust, the playing surface here seems benign. But having baked nicely under the sun for two days, it could well start offering the Indian spinners some purchase as they look to bowl Australia out twice over the next three days.

India\xe2\x80\x99s much-maligned lower-order drew inspiration from Pujara\xe2\x80\x99s magnum opus and battered Australia\xe2\x80\x99s tired bowlers into submission. India\xe2\x80\x99s total was their second-highest in Australia \xe2\x80\x94 after their 705/7 declared here in 2003-04. Apart from Pujara\xe2\x80\x99s dogged 193, it also included a flashy unbeaten 159 by Rishabh Pant and a hard-hitting 81 from Ravindra Jadeja. The 21-year-old, who had become India\xe2\x80\x99s first gloveman to score a Test century in England last year, achieved a similar feat here on Friday even as he shared a record 204-run partnership for the seventh wicket with Jadeja.

It extended Australia\xe2\x80\x99s toil as their bowlers sent down 169.4 overs before Kohli cried halt following Jadeja\xe2\x80\x99s dismissal. Earlier, Pujara resumed on 130 and kept doing what he does best \xe2\x80\x94 batting. Unlike on Day 1 when he was content to defend, Pujara was more enterprising on Friday and prospered in the company of overnight partner Hanuma Vihari. Vihari\xe2\x80\x99s freak dismissal, which couldn\xe2\x80\x99t be overturned even after being reviewed, failed to upset Pujara\xe2\x80\x99s concentration. So convinced was Vihari (42) that his attempted sweep off Nathan Lyon had ricocheted off his left arm into forward short-leg\xe2\x80\x99s palms that he sought a review even as umpire Ian Gould raised his finger. Vihari\xe2\x80\x99s case looked solid but a freak spike on the \xe2\x80\x98snickometer\xe2\x80\x99 prompted third umpire Marais Erasmus to uphold the on-field decision.

There was, however, no respite for Australia as Pant and Jadeja toyed with their bowling for three hours.


