b'A recent list of India\xe2\x80\x99s next-gen intellectuals riled many who dissed it as clubby and unrepresentative. But it has also stirred up a debate on who can claim the title of \xe2\x80\x98public intellectual\xe2\x80\x99

When an online news website compiled a list of next-generation intellectuals recently, it set off a flutter on social media. The list, which was nominated by big-name academics, policymakers and pundits, was criticised for being clubby and unrepresentative. It was largely Hindu, upper-caste and male, said one critic. Those who work and write in Indian languages are not considered, said another. Others chipped in with their own diverse lists of bright minds.


