Shekhar Gupta, editor of The Print, explained the reasoning behind the choices. \xe2\x80\x9cWe wanted to find people with a body of scholarship, who also engage with the wider public,\xe2\x80\x9d he said. So someone merely influential or popular, or an amateur with a large Twitter following, wasn\xe2\x80\x99t going to cut it.

\xe2\x80\x9cThe problem with these lists is the focus on personalities rather than ideas,\xe2\x80\x9d says Pratap Bhanu Mehta, vice-chancellor at Ashoka University, who had declined to nominate any people to the list, knowing it would be \xe2\x80\x9cassured destruction\xe2\x80\x9d. Rather than creating hierarchies of personalities, he says, it might make more sense for list-makers to focus on \xe2\x80\x9cfive great arguments or policy ideas\xe2\x80\x9d.

Of course, lists like this always cause heartburn and bickering. Recently, there\xe2\x80\x99s been great analysis of \xe2\x80\x98young achievers\xe2\x80\x99 lists with people drawing attention to the social capital, educational advantages and hidden privileges that allow someone to be so influential so early. But more than that, the lack of consensus over the list prompts the question: what is a public intellectual for our time? Why do people like Yuval Noah Harari or Stephen Pinker get rockstar receptions wherever they go? Why is a historian like Ramachandra Guha such a sought-after commentator on TV? Why are people like Fareed Zakaria so busy that they can\xe2\x80\x99t keep up with the demands on them?

It\xe2\x80\x99s because we have shifted from a world of public intellectuals to a world of thought leaders, says politics scholar and commentator Daniel Drezner in his book \xe2\x80\x98The Ideas Industry\xe2\x80\x99. A thought leader, is \xe2\x80\x9can intellectual evangelist\xe2\x80\x9d, someone who pushes one big idea to improve the world. Intellectuals tend to be sceptical, critical. Thought leaders are buoyant, solution-oriented, and they are adored at big buzzy conferences like Davos, TED and other think confabs. Intellectual stardom demands that people make themselves into brands, find nice little market niches. For instance, as New York Times points out, a futurist philosopher like Harari is adored by tech CEOs even though he criticises Silicon Valley for undermining democracy and free will.

It\xe2\x80\x99s interesting, because until recently, a public intellectual was not a specialist or an academic at all. Those whose ideas have shaped our thinking have not been professors and think-tankers, but free-range thinkers. The whole debate over public intellectuals began with US historian Russell Jacoby\xe2\x80\x99s book \xe2\x80\x98The Last Intellectuals\xe2\x80\x99, which lamented the way academic professionalisation ruined the bohemian, open intellectual culture that lasted until the 1960s. Some claim that an intellectual needs to be engaged, in the thick of life, before formulating strong ideas, the way George Orwell went and lived among 
the poor in Paris, or the industrial north in England. Others have argued for politically engaged \xe2\x80\x98organic intellectuals\xe2\x80\x99 who represent their own class, but guide them forward.

So who is a public intellectual? Is she the fox who knows many things or the hedgehog who mainly knows one thing well? An intellectual, Mehta says, is someone who goes beyond academic specialisation to articulate public reason \xe2\x80\x94 someone who believes that everyone is capable of being engaged by ideas. \xe2\x80\x9cA public intellectual cuts through the specialist-generalist divide,\xe2\x80\x9d says Shiv Visvanathan, a science and society professor and commentator. \xe2\x80\x9cIn a country like ours, they need to be mediators, translators, people who can go between languages and disciplines,\xe2\x80\x9d he says \xe2\x80\x94 \xe2\x80\x9cthey take esoteric and difficult ideas to the people\xe2\x80\x9d.

Both the \xe2\x80\x98public\xe2\x80\x99 and the \xe2\x80\x98intellectual\xe2\x80\x99 in public intellectual are now open to question, says Mehta. For one, who can really lay claim to the title in a time of so many fragmented publics? \xe2\x80\x9cThere are many circles of influence, those who shape social movements matter, those who influence things in Delhi matter in a different way,\xe2\x80\x9d he says.

Now, platforms and voices have proliferated, and because of ideological polarisation, there is less general trust in credentialled expertise \xe2\x80\x94 so a historian or economist can be dismissed out of hand by people who don\xe2\x80\x99t like the political implications of their work. There are no longer any voices of authority, there are context-specific intellectuals \xe2\x80\x94 every political ideology lionises its own \xe2\x80\x98best minds\xe2\x80\x99, however subpar they seem to outsiders.

The cult of celebrity has also hurt intellectual life, says Mehta. \xe2\x80\x9cThere\xe2\x80\x99s a craving for attention, a need to wade into controversy, to show up at the right festivals. In fact the role of an intellectual should be to say \xe2\x80\x98slow down\xe2\x80\x99 to the public discourse, he says.

Of course, when it comes to evaluating ideas and minds, traditional barriers apply. The much-criticised intellectuals\xe2\x80\x99 list only illuminates our larger inability to see the perspectives of those who lack social power.


'

