            Ankur Warikoo, an entrepreneur in Delhi, purchased Alexa especially for his seven-year-old son, Vidur who uses it to play songs and get answers to questions that he has.

Some like six-year-old Prisha Chatterjee treat the voice with blue lights like a friend. Every day, once she\xe2\x80\x99s home from school she asks Alexa how her day was, and talks to her while playing Lego. When her friends come over to play they include Alexa in their games, ask her questions, and laugh at her answers. It\xe2\x80\x99s a big girly party.

A 2017 study by MIT found that young children are more likely to speak to voice assistants as if they are people. That\xe2\x80\x99s why they ask it personal questions.

Often, parents unknowingle encourage this. Swati Thacker told her three-year-old daughter, Siya, that \xe2\x80\x9can aunty is sitting inside the box\xe2\x80\x9d. \xe2\x80\x9cWe got Alexa last year and when we used it for the first time Siya looked around the room to see who was talking. So, we made it simpler for her,\xe2\x80\x9d says Thacker, a technical writer with Oracle in Bengaluru.

Children, clearly, are turning out to be important consumers for voice assistants. Though Amazon India didn\xe2\x80\x99t share data on users, they confirmed that \xe2\x80\x9cwhile Alexa was positioned as family device, there\xe2\x80\x99s a special focus on children\xe2\x80\x9d. The company has added localised content to its voice apps: rhymes from ChuChu TV, Amar Chitra Katha quizzes, Suppandi\xe2\x80\x99s brain teasers etc. In the US, it\xe2\x80\x99s introduced a kid-friendly version of the Echo speaker.

But there is a downside to the Alexa generation getting used to giving instructions to a bot without so much as a thank you or a please. Plus there are concerns of data privacy. \xe2\x80\x9cWhen we are not using it, we turn it off because of privacy concerns,\xe2\x80\x9d says Neetha Ann, an entrepreneur, whose six-year-old daughter uses a voice assistant to play music.

Dr Manoj Sharma, professor clinical psychology at NIMHANS, Bengaluru, points out that there\xe2\x80\x99s not too much data yet on how AI devices impact children. \xe2\x80\x9cBut what we do know is that over-dependence on any object or device can lead to psychological issues later in life. And there\xe2\x80\x99s always a possibility that the child starts spending more time with the device than with people,\xe2\x80\x9d he warns. He suggests that parents explain to the child that the voice is a bot and not a person.

Most parents, however, seem pleased that these digital nannies are helping their kids learn and play. \xe2\x80\x9cIt has taken my daughter Prisha off mobile screens,\xe2\x80\x9d says Prateek Chatterjee, senior VP, corporate communications, NIIT, Delhi. 

