                      $('.top2brdiv').remove();
                      $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                    })()



            \xe2\x80\x98Shakti \xe2\x80\x94 Political Power to Women\xe2\x80\x99 is a platform of non-partisan women working towards the objective of enabling more women to be elected as MLAs and MPs. Seventy years into a democratic republic, we have barely 11% women in Lok Sabha, and 9% in state assemblies. As of last week, there was not a single woman legislator in Mizoram or Nagaland! The organic process of democracy and political party machineries has utterly failed to provide women reasonable opportunities to govern in the states and the centre. Women need a fair amount of seats at the table of decison-makers, which is the rationale behind our initiative.


Why is the time right for such an initiative?
It has been decades of waiting since the Constitution promised political equality. With the confluence of the upcoming general elections, an increased socio-political consciousness for women\xe2\x80\x99s rights, #MeToo gains in India, recent worldwide electoral wins for women, and an unprecedented penetration with mobile phones and social media, our ability to organise, mobilise and transform couldn\xe2\x80\x99t be better.


What are some of the misconceptions around women in politics?
There are so many myths and even complete falsehoods propagated about women in politics.


1. Women are not interested in politics, that is why there are so few of them in politics.
Political parties have lakhs of women members. Yet only 4-8% of their candidates are women. Plus, about 10 lakh or so women are elected every 5 years to local bodies. Hypothetically, even if only 10% are interested and competent, that is lakhs of women who have no further career destination except their kitchens!


2. Women politicians are usually someone\xe2\x80\x99s wife or sister, and hence dummy representatives.
Most male politicians are someone\xe2\x80\x99s son or nephew, or selected because of certain caste or community equations. Do we therefore dismiss men as dummies? Dynasty and nepotism are two pillars of Indian politics, regardless of gender. As for the men usurping the power of the duly elected women, the flaw is with parties that harbour such men instead of encouraging competent women party workers or proven women leaders from local bodies. 


3. Women are not winnable candidates, hence not given tickets by parties.
The Election Commission\xe2\x80\x99s gender-wise statistics show exactly the opposite. For every single Lok Sabha election since the beginning, women have been winning at a rate 3 to 20% greater than men. In other words, if the party is viable, women are more winnable than men!


4. They are not competent enough to govern.
Numerous studies on 25 years of panchayats show that women leaders prioritise health, education and sanitation, and deliver better projects with less corruption. A UN study of 4,000+ assembly constituencies from 1992-2012 showed that women-led constituencies experienced 15% more economic growth and contributed 1.8% more per year to the GDP. Indeed 53% of women candidates are graduates, as against 48% for males.


What about corruption?
Neither corruption nor criminality are gendered. That said, as per their own affidavits, twice as many men have criminal charges against them as women. Academic research also shows that corruption is 10% points lower in women political leaders than men.


What is the difference that women\xe2\x80\x99s leadership makes?
On the policy front, lack of women in governance, law and policy formulation means less diversity of lived experiences and perspectives. The evidence for this is the woeful state of public schools, public health facilities, substandard infrastructure and crumbling farming. Women\xe2\x80\x99s leadership not only improves governance outcomes, it enables women constituents, i.e. half the population to come forward and communicate their governance demands and woes to women leaders. Women as elected leaders engender half the population to participate in governance which itself has the ability to transform India.

While we keep searching for that elusive \xe2\x80\x9calternative\xe2\x80\x9d and voting for the same, recycled men over and over, perhaps we need to open our minds. The real alternative is women: women across parties, ideologies, religions, castes and demographics. Gender balance in Parliament and state assemblies is the alternative that can transform India.

In fact, in Shakti\xe2\x80\x99s Call Your MP campaign on December 27, 127 of the 130 Lok Sabha MPs across 23 political parties and 21 states reached said \xe2\x80\x9cyes\xe2\x80\x9d to passing the Women\xe2\x80\x99s Reservation Bill in this Parliament session. Nevertheless, the challenge remains to persuade politicians to walk the talk \xe2\x80\x94 the ruling dispensation to table the bill, and parties to select women candidates.


'

b'The lights are dim, the audience is rapt, and the spotlight is on Raghav Mehta as he tells the story of the Mahabharata, weaving an intricate tale with his voice. Using facial expressions, gestures and practiced pauses, the medieval art form of dastangoi is underway. The catch? He is only eight years old. For a generation that learns its favourite dance moves from video games, and says things like \xe2\x80\x9cFacebook is dead, and Snapchat is dying\xe2\x80\x9d, harbouring the desire to learn and perform the art of dastangoi is unusual, to say the least.

A group of eight children, belonging to the Noida-based children\xe2\x80\x99s theatre company Dramebaaz have taken on the challenge of learning the storytelling form. These kids have been learning improvisational theatre for the last few years, which makes it quite the task to get used to the exactitude that dastangoi requires. Mehta, the youngest among the performers, performed his rendition of the Dastan-e-Mahabharata at the Times Litfest to much acclaim. \xe2\x80\x9cThe story is in Urdu and I liked learning the meaning of all the words and telling a story that I have known since I was very young,\xe2\x80\x9d he says.



