            Garima Arya, the founder of Dramebaaz, wanted to challenge the children with a new form of theatre. She approached Fouzia Dastango, India\xe2\x80\x99s first female dastango, to help train the kids. Thus began the six-month long journey to find stories that the kids liked, getting them written in the form of dastangoi and teaching them the essentials of emoting and using your voice to convey nuance.

A born storyteller, Fouzia was the kind of child who read and recited stories to her class. She began her journey with the ancient form in 2005, after a friend encouraged her to do so. Perhaps it was destiny; she grew up in Pahari Bhojla in Old Delhi, the same area which had been the home of the last of the legendary dastango, Mir Baqar Ali.

\xe2\x80\x9cOne major challenge for the kids was the language. They aren\xe2\x80\x99t used to speaking in Hindi,\xe2\x80\x9d she says. After months of practice, not just from the children but from their committed parents as well, they got more comfortable. \xe2\x80\x9cFor me, the aim of dastangoi is holding on to the things we are forgetting. If you forget your language, that\xe2\x80\x99s a big cultural transformation. I want the kids to fall in love with their language, and get closer to their roots,\xe2\x80\x9d she adds.

It wasn\xe2\x80\x99t just that they had to talk in Hindi, but they had to master the intricacies of the language\xe2\x80\x94from understanding how a nuqta changes the pronunciation of a word, to learning the script exactly to not disturb the natural rhythm of Dastangoi. Fouzia sent over audio recordings of the correct pronunciation for the kids to learn. Arya says, \xe2\x80\x9cDastangoi is all about words. Even if you change one word here and there, the rhythm breaks and the flow changes completely.\xe2\x80\x9d

Dastangoi isn\xe2\x80\x99t easy to learn for an adult, let alone kids\xe2\x80\x94it\xe2\x80\x99s just you on stage, trying to command the attention of the audience. Amrapali Makhija, 12, experiences major nerves when it comes to taking the stage. So, when she had to perform on her own for a full ten minutes of uninterrupted speaking, it made her rather uneasy. \xe2\x80\x9cInitially, I had a partner but she left so I was really scared about forgetting my lines, or messing up,\xe2\x80\x9d she says. Makhija points out that she did actually have a moment where she forgot her line at her first performance, but Arya warmly assures her that absolutely no one could tell.

The kids have performed a handful of times over the last year, and are set to accompany Fouzia in her future performances. Ten-year-old Mahuaa Agarwal tells a thirteen-minute long story about a witch and her two brothers with her partner, Shobhita Nehra. \xe2\x80\x9cFouzia Ma\xe2\x80\x99am showed us how to tell stories properly. She taught us when to pause and how to move our hands, which part of the story is the climax. So, if you\xe2\x80\x99re telling a running scene, you should narrate it faster,\xe2\x80\x9d says Agarwal, who performed at the Samanvay Indian Languages Festival last year.

Arya plans on introducing more kids to the art form after they complete two years at her theatre company. She argues that being outside your comfort zone is a must for artists. \xe2\x80\x9cFor an actor, variety is really important. We need to keep experiencing new things, and keep learning for the rest of our lives,\xe2\x80\x9d she says. A good point, but her ten-year-old collaborator Mahuaa disagrees, saying: \xe2\x80\x9cI only want to perform dastangoi from now on.\xe2\x80\x9d

'

b'Whenever the pressures of the world get too much for me, I lighten the burden by going over to Donald Trump\xe2\x80\x99s Twitter feed. Trump, the only US president who would fail the Turing Test, keeps lashing out at opponents and saying outrageous things, often in ALL CAPS, the internet version of screaming. It\xe2\x80\x99s wonderfully entertaining, and got even more so when he lashed out at Narendra Modi last week. 


Trump said: \xe2\x80\x9cI could give you an example where I get along very well with India and Prime Minister Modi. But he is constantly telling me, he built a library in Afghanistan. Library! That\xe2\x80\x99s like five hours of what we spend. And he tells me. He is very smart. We are supposed to say, oh thank you for the library. Don\xe2\x80\x99t know who\xe2\x80\x99s using it in Afghanistan.\xe2\x80\x9d


