Modi fanbois also tend to be Trump fanbois because they like macho men, and this must have been confusing to them at multiple levels. One, why was Trump lashing out at Modi? Two, if Trump is telling the truth, why does Modi keep boasting about building a library? Three, what library?
After much googling in the PMO, our officials figured that despite having spent over US $3 billion in Afghanistan since 2001, we hadn\xe2\x80\x99t built a library. Maybe Trump confused the parliament building with a library \xe2\x80\x94 but it was unlikely that Modi would boast to Trump even about that, especially \xe2\x80\x98constantly\xe2\x80\x99. So Trump was lying, right?

No, Trump wasn\xe2\x80\x99t lying, I say. He was bullshitting.

I\xe2\x80\x99m not just dropping slang here. \xe2\x80\x98Bullshit\xe2\x80\x99 may appear to be a pejorative, but it has become a technical term after the philosopher Harry Frankfurt published his seminal book, On Bullshit, in 2005. And if we are to understand the likes of Trump, it is important to understand the distinction between \xe2\x80\x98bullshit\xe2\x80\x99 and \xe2\x80\x98lies\xe2\x80\x99. 

A liar, as per Frankfurt, acknowledges the truth, and aims to deceive. For a bullshitter, the truth is irrelevant. Bullshitters aim not to deceive, but are just winging it, saying whatever comes into their head at a given point in time, which may or may not be true. For Trump, everything comes down to how macho he is, and how others are messing around at America\xe2\x80\x99s expense and only he can stop them. His boast about Modi \xe2\x80\x98constantly\xe2\x80\x99 talking to him about a library was in this vein. He could also have said that Modi wears ladies underwear, or Modi begged him to open a casino in Lutyens\xe2\x80\x99 Delhi, or Modi wore a suit with his name on it \xe2\x80\x93 any relationship that bullshit has with the truth is usually coincidental. 

Trump bullshits a lot \xe2\x80\x94 and then doubles down on his bullshit once he has released it. For example, the wall that Mexico will pay for. I am sure he was winging it when he first came up with it. 

Ironically, his latest target is a master of the art himself. When Modi spoke of the first plastic surgery in India being done on Lord Ganesha, or climate change being a consequence of people getting older, he wasn\xe2\x80\x99t lying but bullshitting.

Politicians can get away with bullshit (and lying) because people don\xe2\x80\x99t care about facts. We tend to form echo chambers with like-minded people, form whatever worldview appeals to us, and shut ourselves off from conflicting views. 

Behavioural scientists point to hard-wiring in the brain that strengthens this process. The Confirmation Bias ensures that we only consider facts that agree with our worldview and ignore the rest. The Backfire Effect, worryingly for fact-checkers, ensures that our worldviews actually grow stronger when we are presented with conflicting evidence. 

These echo chambers, once formed, tend to grow more and more strident. Experiments by social scientists have found that when people with similar opinions are thrown together in a group, they tend to take decisions more extreme than any one individual would take. (This is known as \xe2\x80\x98group polarisation\xe2\x80\x99.) You see this in social media, where discourse is polarised and opposing sides are talking past each other, not to each other, with every individual performing to impress his own side. 

When all discourse takes place along tribal lines, rhetoric matters, facts don\xe2\x80\x99t.

Is bullshit a feature or a bug? An entire nation will vote upon it this year, as India goes to the polls. Narendra Modi has focused entirely on optics and not on performance, betting that once people have formed a narrative inside their heads, facts are irrelevant. Is an A+ in campaigning more important than an F in governance? We will soon find out what the people of India think.

'

b'MUMBAI: In a January 4 letter issued to all top incometax (I-T) officials, Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT) chairman Sushil Chandra raised concerns over the unsatisfactory rate of growth in direct tax collections till December-end. Now, in an effort to boost I-T collections, Chandra has drawn up a three-month strategy, which includes targeted surveys for tax recovery and prosecution of wilful defaulters, if necessary.


Particularly jarring is the poor growth in direct tax collections of just 1.1% or Rs 33,218 crore as on December 2018 via regular or scrutiny assessment compared with 15.6% during the corresponding period last year, states the letter. Mumbai collection figures, in particular, showed a slight dip whereas Delhi recorded a significant increase of more than 90%. (See table).


