Scrutiny assessment refers to a detailed assessment or examination of an I-T return filed by a taxpayer. I-T authorities must complete the assessment within the prescribed time limit.

Not all returns filed are picked up for scrutiny, though. To illustrate, during the year ending March 2018, merely 0.35% I-T returns filed were selected for scrutiny. Certain criteria are laid down for the purpose.




For instance, if during assessment in an earlier year the addition to income exceeded Rs 25 lakh and the taxpayer was based in a specified city, say, Mumbai, his or her case would be picked up for scrutiny. Cases are randomly picked up for scrutiny purposes too. Some I-T officials in Mumbai attribute the poor collection of scrutiny assessments to technical glitches in the internal software (ITBA) system for e-assessment, which has slowed down the entire process.

Given this, on December 31, the directorate of I-T (systems) had issued an internal memo calling upon I-T officials to process all time barring cases, due by December-end, offline. These instructions came too late though, says an I-T official.

Without giving exact figures of the total direct taxes collected as on December 31, 2018, CBDT chairman\xe2\x80\x99s letter indicated that against the target growth rate of 14.7%, net collections are growing at 13.6%.

The position of growth in gross collections is marginally better at 14.1%, though still below the required rate for achieving the Budget estimates of Rs 11.5 lakh crore, it added.

To drive up recovery from tax arrears and current tax demands, Chandra has listed an eight-step plan for the next three months ending March 2019. These include targeted recovery surveys in potential cases where high amount of recovery is likely. Also, sale of attached properties in \xe2\x80\x9cappropriate cases\xe2\x80\x9d to recover confirmed tax demands where normal measures of recovery have not yielded results.

A few steps, such as initiation of proceedings under Section 179 to recover outstanding tax dues of private companies and serving prosecution notices under Section 276C (2) upon persons who wilfully evade payment of outstanding taxes, are causing anxiety about the possible return of a \xe2\x80\x9ctaxterror regime\xe2\x80\x9d in professional circles.

A chartered accountant, though, said it remains to be seen how I-T officials will act in the coming months. Section 179 empowers I-T officials to serve a notice on directors of a private company for recovery of outstanding tax dues.

The onus is on the directors to prove their innocence \xe2\x80\x94 that the non-recovery of taxes cannot be attributed to any gross neglect, misfeasance or breach of duty on their part, in relation to affairs of the company. \xe2\x80\x9cThe controversy over I-T demands for collection of angel tax (tax on the amount in excess of fair market value) received from equity investors, has not died down. Now, this letter will create a climate of further uncertainty,\xe2\x80\x9d says a private equity investor. Over the past few years, prosecution notices have been issued, including on startups, largely under Sections 276B for defaults in collection and deposit of tax deducted at source (TDS).

This is likely to continue, as the CBDT chief \xe2\x80\x99s letter calls for \xe2\x80\x9cverification of deductors where there is non-payment of TDS to the government, though TDS has been deducted\xe2\x80\x9d. It also calls for examination of instances where there is substantially low TDS compared to the previous year, including prosecution in cases of substantial default. The CBDT chief \xe2\x80\x99s letter also calls for prosecution notices under Section 276 (C) for wilful evasion of tax demands, interest and penalties.

Other steps outlined in the letter to shore up I-T collections include verification of payment of advance taxes by sellers of properties and monitoring payment of dividend distribution tax by companies. For verification of the latter, information will also be obtained from the Securities and Exchange Board of India. 

