Resuming their chase of India\xe2\x80\x99s mammoth first-innings score of 622/7 declared, Australia made a bright start and dominated the morning session before displaying familiar failings and ending the day on 236/6 \xe2\x80\x94 386 runs in arrears.

Fighting a grim battle for survival against India\xe2\x80\x99s spinners Ravindra Jadeja (2/62) and Kuldeep Yadav (3/71) were Peter Handscomb (28) and Pat Cummins (25).

Earlier in the day, Australia failed to build on the fine platform provided by Harris and Marnus Labuschagne. Harris, who has got a start in every inning he has played in the series so far, produced the best score (79) of his fledging career, but failed to kick on and get to his maiden hundred.

The manner in which Harris and Usman Khawaja batted on the third morning suggested that Australia may have finally found a cure for their batting woes.

Australia\xe2\x80\x99s new opening pair \xe2\x80\x94 their fifth in the past one year \xe2\x80\x94 produced the second-best first-wicket stand (72) of the series, before Khawaja (27), who had played with a lot of freedom till then, tried to hit wrist-spinner Kuldeep down the ground but only got an inside edge and popped up a simple catch to mid-wicket.

Labuschagne, who came in next, was lucky to survive the first ball he faced from Jasprit Bumrah. The Indian pacer fired a pin-point yorker which Labuschagne somehow managed to dig out with the toe of his bat. The youngster, however, settled down and fared much better with the bat than he had with the ball.

Twice he drove Kuldeep through cover, while a reassuring sweep off the same bowler fetched him his second boundary. With Harris going great guns at the other end, runs came thick and fast. Harris raced to his half-century off just 67 balls, five of which he despatched to the boundary. The two batsmen shared a 56-run stand and took their team\xe2\x80\x99s score to 122/1 at the lunch break as Australian batsmen creamed 98 runs in the first session.

Harris raced to his personal best of 79, but brought about his own downfall with a poor choice of shot. In trying to steer a sharply spinning delivery from Jadeja to point, he ended up dragging the ball on to his leg-stump. Incidentally, Harris\xe2\x80\x99 79 was the second-best score by an Australian batsman since Khawaja\xe2\x80\x99s 141 against Pakistan in Dubai last year.

Harris\xe2\x80\x99 dismissal triggered a mini-collapse which saw Australia losing three wickets for the addition of just 24 runs. Shaun Marsh smacked Jadeja twice through cover before pushing forward to one that held its line and offered a regulation slip catch to Ajinkya Rahane, who was again called into action \xe2\x80\x94 this time at short mid-wicket \xe2\x80\x94 when Labuschagne (38) failed to keep his intended flick off Mohammad Shami down.

Travis Head (20) and Handscomb held up India\xe2\x80\x99s charge briefly with a sedate fifth-wicket stand of 40, but the former wasted another start when he drove a low full-toss from Kuldeep straight back into the bowler\xe2\x80\x99s hands.

Australia will have to cope with the new ball \xe2\x80\x94 which is already due \xe2\x80\x94 when play resumes on Day 4.


'

