            What has changed is that a lot of interest has been generated around Indian football. So there will be a lot more focus on us and it will be important to give a good account of ourselves. It\xe2\x80\x99s not going to be easy because we don\xe2\x80\x99t play a lot of matches against the giants of Asian football. But the good part is we are not afraid of any opponent this time around.


Are you well prepared than last time, considering India eked out goalless draws against heavyweights China and Oman recently?
We did well against some tough opponents like Iran, Oman and China recently. Those results have made us confident. We aren\xe2\x80\x99t a team which enjoys a lot of possession and you won\xe2\x80\x99t see us playing attacking football. However, I wouldn\xe2\x80\x99t say our style is defensive. We do give a lot of thought in planning and analysing our opponents.


How would you describe our three group stage opponents?
We are not even thinking about Bahrain and UAE. We are just focusing on our first game against Thailand. In the last 6-8 years, Thailand have been one of the most improved nations in Asia. They have shown how comfortable they are with the ball against big opponents. We have to make sure we defend in numbers and not give them that space.


Realistically, what do you think about India\xe2\x80\x99s chances?
