            According to Kotak, the immediate trigger for the challenges facing the sector was the default by IL&FS which raised issues among investors about the quality of rating agency and the accounting process. Sharing his learnings of the sector, Kotak said that a greater focus was needed on intra-group transactions. He also warned against quid-pro-quo deals, \xe2\x80\x9cAt times in the financial sector we have seen cases of \xe2\x80\x98You scratch my back I scratch yours\xe2\x80\x99. We have to be watchful of these,\xe2\x80\x9d said Kotak. He cautioned against re-routing of funds in the sector. \xe2\x80\x9cRe-routing is good for Google Maps but not for the financial sector,\xe2\x80\x9d said Kotak.

IL&FS Financial Services has been raising funds and lending to other group companies. The RBI had warned the group over its breach of the regulatory limit. The company, hoever, continued its borrowing spree, to repay old debt, until it finally defaulted.

Interestingly, in 2015 when Ajay Piramal sought to buy into IL&FS, Life Insurance Corporation, one of the promoters of the infrastructure company, had stymied the deal, objecting to the pricing. However, in subsequent years when the pressure on IL&FS became evident, the promoters held back from investing at a much lower price. In October 2018, Hari Sankaran, former vice chairman and MD of IL&FS, in an affidavit, blamed LIC for its woes, stating that the 2015 equity offering would have addressed the group\xe2\x80\x99s capital issues. 


