            The lack of a common industry voice was also highlighted by economic affairs secretary S C Garg. Speaking at a CII event on Saturday, Garg said that \xe2\x80\x9cwhen we want to speak to NBFCs, we do not know whom to talk to.\xe2\x80\x9d CII has responded by proposing to nurture a self-regulatory organisation for at least the 276 systemically important NBFCs. However, some industry players feel that the association should be independent and not affiliated to any industry body.

According to Rajiv Sabharwal, MD & CEO, Tata Capital, besides the middle and the top level of NBFCs, companies are still facing challenges. \xe2\x80\x9cAnother issue is the cost of funds. This could prove to be a real challenge for the sector if they are not able to pass on the costs,\xe2\x80\x9d he said.

Besides providing the industry with a common voice, an association is also expected to help in gathering data from the systemically important NBFCs and pass them on to the regulator. Some of these issues will be raised by industry chiefs in their meeting with the RBI governor Shaktikanta Das next week. The main concern of the industry is that it requires close to Rs 6 lakh crore of funds next year to continue growing at 20% whereas there are restrictions on various avenues.

NBFCs want floating of retail debt to be made easier. They also want insurance companies and pension funds to be granted more headroom to invest in the NBFC paper. The other suggestions from NBFCs is that they should be provided emergency funds from the RBI under a repo facility to inspire confidence among investors. However, since NBFCs do not hold government bonds, there was a suggestion that the repo facility could be done using corporate debt.


