\xe2\x80\x9cThere is no question of giving any kind of immunity to the new buyer. It is public money, and we will take it from whoever owns it in case the corporate guarantee that RCom is providing us does not prove to be sufficient,\xe2\x80\x9d a senior official in the telecom department told TOI. The potential dues that could arise towards RCom are estimated at around Rs 3,000 crore, though these are stuck in various litigations that the telecom industry is fighting with the government.

In the normal course of deal-making, the government procures a bank guarantee towards meeting the possible future liabilities. But the debt-laden RCom has been unable to procure the same, and later managed to receive a special permission from the court to provide a corporate guarantee backed by its land holdings.

Jio is not confident about the strength of this corporate guarantee and wants a promise by DoT that no liabilities would flow towards it in case RCom\xe2\x80\x99s guarantee is not found to be sufficient, or fails to deliver.

The DoT official, however, said that either of the two have to be responsible for the dues, whenever they arise. \xe2\x80\x9cThey had knowledge of this from Day one, and there is no way we can give a leeway \xe2\x80\x93 or a special undertaking \xe2\x80\x93 to Jio which wants to be the new owner. The rules are pretty clear here.\xe2\x80\x9d

The issue has the potential to become a deal-breaker if the government or Jio decide to maintain their position. Sources say that Jio is now working on a \xe2\x80\x98Plan 2\xe2\x80\x99 to prepare for a life in case it has to lose RCom\xe2\x80\x99s airwaves, which it currently uses through a spectrum-sharing (rental) deal.

However, the two companies recently extended the term of their pact (towards the trading deal) by around six months till June 28. This gives them more time to negotiate the various conditions, including those sought by DoT, without ending the deal.

If the deal collapses, it would be a major setback for RCom which wants to reduce its Rs 45,000 crore debt through the sale. It wants to use the proceeds to retire its heavy debt, which includes Rs 550 crore owed to Ericsson that is now seeking a jail term for Anil Ambani for non-payment despite repeated requests.



'

