\xe2\x80\x9cUntil now, we have only heard the accusation.\xe2\x80\x9d But Anthony Ghosn says his father faces a paradox: confess to crimes of which he is innocent or remain in detention. It is quite common for prosecutors to keep people in detention for months or even years, he said. \xe2\x80\x9cWhen the only condition for his release is a confession, you want to find a solution to end this nightmare,\xe2\x80\x9d Anthony Ghosn told the newspaper.

While Carlos Ghosn was indicted on allegations of under-reporting his compensation, the length of detention and lack of clarity on the case has drawn criticism. Ghosn was initially held without charge for longer than would be permitted in the UK for a suspected terrorist.

The executive was re-arrested on fresh, potentially more serious charges on December 21 just when it looked like he may be able to apply for bail. Prosecutors have accused him of transferring personal trading losses to Nissan, but are yet to indict him on this allegation.

In Japan, indictment paves the way for prosecutors to lay formal charges. His detention was last extended on December 31, putting him behind bars until at least January 11.

The French government, meanwhile, has told Renault to provide more details on compensation paid to senior executives via a Dutch holding company jointly owned with alliance partner Nissan, finance minister Bruno Le Maire said on Sunday.

Le Maire made the demand after France\xe2\x80\x99s CGT trade Union voiced concerns over payments made to certain high-ranking executives via the alliance\xe2\x80\x99s Renault-Nissan BV (RNBV) Dutch venture and called for more transparency at the carmaker. Corporate governance inside the alliance has come under tight scrutiny after Japanese authorities arrested its chairman Carlos Ghosn in mid November on suspicion of under-reporting his income at Nissan. The French state is Renault\xe2\x80\x99s biggest shareholder.

