                      $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                    })()




Resuming on 236 for 6, the hosts\xe2\x80\x99 first innings folded up for 300 and they ended up conceding a substantial 322-run lead to India. Skipper Virat Kohli did not hesitate to enforce the follow-on, but could not dent Australia\xe2\x80\x99s second innings as the light deteriorated significantly for the two umpires to take the players off the ground.

Incidentally, it is the first time in nearly 31 years \xe2\x80\x94 since the one-off Bicentenary Test versus England in Sydney (Jan 29-Feb 2, 1988) \xe2\x80\x94 that Australia have been asked to follow on in a home Test.

Openers Marcus Harris, who was once again seen fending off a bouncer from Jasprit Bumrah ungainly from in front of his chin, and Usman Khawaja saw off a torrid four overs. Weather permitting, the duo will resume Australia\xe2\x80\x99s battle for survival on Monday. With more cloud cover and showers forecast on the final day, the Indian team will have one eye on the sky as they go all out to seal the series 3-1.

India, however, are virtually assured of a maiden Test series win in Australia following a 70-year wait as a draw will help them triumph 2-1.

Whether India are successful in forcing a victory on the final day remains to be seen, but there can be no doubt about their domination of the series. On a sedate SCG track, which is still good for batting, India rattled up 622 for 7 before declaring their first innings and then dismissed Australia for less than half of that score.

When play started on Sunday morning after a three-hour delay, the conditions were hardly perfect for batting. Indian pacer Mohammad Shami got the last ball of his first over of the day to shoot through the defence of Pat Cummins and crash into the stumps.

Peter Handscomb, unbeaten on 28 overnight, did not last long either. The right-hander, who was reinstated in the middle-order for his considerable ability against spin bowling, added nine runs to his tally before playing on a delivery from Bumrah.

Nathan Lyon was adjudged leg-before wicket while attempting to sweep left-arm spinner Kuldeep Yadav. The Australian batsman surprisingly did not ask for the decision to be reviewed.

When it appeared that the end was near, the Australian innings received a boost from the last pair of Mitchell Starc and Josh Hazlewood \xe2\x80\x94 who was dropped by Hanuma Vihari when he was yet to open his account \xe2\x80\x94 combined for a 42-run stand that kept India waiting for 14 overs.

Hazlewood was eventually dismissed by Kuldeep with a wrong one for 21, and even though the batsman reviewed the leg-before decision, the onfield verdict stood. Kuldeep finished with five for 99.

'

