However, if India\xe2\x80\x99s bowling coach Bharat Arun is to be believed, the visiting team had chalked out their plans against Australian batsmen well in advance. \xe2\x80\x9cWe have a lot of respect for their fast-bowling attack \xe2\x80\x94 probably one of the best in the world. But we were more focused on what we could do. Having come here previously, we knew the pitches here would be very similar to the ones we got in South Africa. In order to be successful in Australia, we needed to make sure that we took away the cut and pull from their batsmen. That\xe2\x80\x99s exactly what we did,\xe2\x80\x9d Arun disclosed at the end of the fourth day\xe2\x80\x99s play here on Sunday.

Talking about the previous two overseas tours of South Africa and England, Arun felt that even though India had their chances in both series, they ended up losing both. \xe2\x80\x9cWe made mistakes there but focused hard on where we went wrong even as we moved forward. These two tours were of great experience and helped us do well in Australia,\xe2\x80\x9d Arun added.

