b'Mumbai: Boston Consultancy Group (BCG) has suggested giving individuals the right to share with lenders their personal data stored with banks and other service providers. According to the management consultancy firm, this will extend financial inclusion as those without any collateral or credit history can get access to financing if they were allowed to grant lenders the right to access their repayment histories with other service providers. 

\xe2\x80\x9cCustomers need to be given control of their data. This is a huge potential asset which customers can leverage for cheaper credit,\xe2\x80\x9d said Saurabh Tripathi, partner, BCG. According to Tripathi, fintechs have been able to tap a large segment of new-to-credit customers by leveraging data. \xe2\x80\x9cThe Aadhaar infrastructure is very much capable of allowing customers to instruct banks to share their data with other lenders,\xe2\x80\x9d said Tripathi. \xe2\x80\x9cThis is similar to what is happening in Unified Payments Interface (UPI) where instructions can be given to the customer\xe2\x80\x99s bank from any bank\xe2\x80\x99s platform,\xe2\x80\x9d he added. 



