            \xe2\x80\x9cThe NBFC-aggregator platform proposed by the RBI can be used for facilitating this,\xe2\x80\x9d said Tripathi. He added that besides bank data, customers can choose to share their data stored with telecom and power utilities, and with the Employees\xe2\x80\x99 Provident Fund Office, to get cheaper loans. \xe2\x80\x9cWhat is required is regulation that forces the person using the data to delete it later so that it is not misused. This is the only part of the law that needs to be changed,\xe2\x80\x9d said Tripathi. 

In India, the debate around data has largely focused on privacy concerns, security of data and the need to store data locally. While the RBI has made the first move towards open banking by introducing the concept of account aggregators and bringing in the UPI, the Bharat Bill Payment Platform enables payments service providers access billing data from different utilities across the country. 

According to a BCG report, one of the factors for facilitating digital lending is the access to surrogate data such as telecom and other utilities. \xe2\x80\x9cFintechs have had particular success reaching new-to-credit (NTC) borrowers. In commercial bureau reporting, the proportion of NTC borrowers is three times higher for fintechs versus private banks, NBFCs, and public sector banks, highlighting the role that fintechs can play in expanding credit coverage among India\xe2\x80\x99s MSMEs,\xe2\x80\x9d BCG said in the report \xe2\x80\x98Credit Disrupted \xe2\x80\x94 Digital MSME Lending In India\xe2\x80\x99.


