            It was indeed a moment to cherish, for it ended India's 71-year wait to breach the 'last frontier'. Since Lala Amarnath's side were hammered 0-4 on their maiden trip to Australia by Donald Bradman's team in 1947-48, India had failed to win a single series on each of their 10 subsequent tours Down Under.

With a series victory in Australia eluding successive generation of Indian players, the current series (12th overall) represented the 'last frontier' for Kohli and Co. The team succeeded in breaching it with their steely resolve. For the record, India have now recorded series wins in all Test-playing nations barring South Africa, with whom their cricketing ties are relatively new - dating back to 1991-92, when the South Africans were readmitted to international cricket in the post-Apartheid era.



Both Kohli and coach Ravi Shastri described it as the biggest achievement of their lives. "It (series win in Australia) rates at the top of the pile. When we won the 2011 World Cup, I was the youngest member of the side. I saw everyone emotional there, but I didn't feel it. After coming here (in Australia) three times, this win means something else. It is will give us a different identity... it's something to be proud of," Kohli said. Shastri echoed his captain. "I will tell you how satisfying it is for me. World Cup '83, World Championship '85. This is as big, or even bigger, because it is in the truest format of the game. It's Test cricket, which is meant to be the toughest."

The Indian captain lauded his players. "I've never been more proud of being part of a team, than today. To lead this team is an honour and privilege. The boys make the captain look good," said Kohli, hinting at a night-long party. "We all deserve to enjoy the moment. The celebrations, I assure you, will run long. The boys are physically and mentally drained, and we have no more alarm clocks in the morning."



There were joyous scenes at the SCG after the presentation ceremony, with the Indian players acknowledging a handful of Indian supporters who had braved the drizzle. Young Rishabh Pant was seen performing an impromptu jig and his teammates were quick to join in even as the crowd roared its approval. Asked about the jig, Kohli said: "He (Rishabh) came up with it and we just went along. It just felt good, it was very easy but (Cheteshwar) Pujara couldn't even do that!"

Both Kohli and coach Ravi Shastri, who later addressed the media jointly, poked fun at Pujara, who was named Man of the Match as well as Man of the Series. While Shastri suggested that Pant was trying to get Pujara to shake a leg, Kohli insisted that Pujara's movements represented a dance. "When he walks, he doesn't move his hands," Kohli said with tongue firmly in cheek.

"They would have given him (Pujara) the trophy without even mentioning his name, once they saw him padded up," Shastri chuckled.




HE HELD INDIA TOGETHER

Cheteshwar Pujara won his first Player of the Series award. He was also Man of the Match in Adelaide and Sydney. Pujara has also become the first batsman to average 60-plus in Tests involving India-Australia (minimum 1500 runs), his tally being 1622 runs (ave.60.07) in 16 Tests.

