"You don't need to tell the guys to run in and bowl with full intensity. They take ownership of their skills and you can see the way they bowl and set their own fields. They are dictating terms now and this is the most amazing thing you can ask for from your bowlers, especially pacers. When they are happy and firing, you always feel like you can win anywhere and you can beat any side," he said.

The Indian captain agreed with coach Ravi Shastri that preparations for the Australian tour had started 12 months back in South Africa.



"For us, this tour didn't start in Australia. It started 12 months ago in South Africa, where we decided to play a certain brand of cricket and experiment with combinations in order to find out what suits the team best. We learnt a lot in South Africa and England. We also made mistakes there and learnt from them and didn't repeat them in Australia," said Shastri.

Kohli said: "As a team we focused on the things that we did not do well. We laid out a lot of those things on the table and no one took offence to anything and rectified their mistakes. Here, in game situation, we were better than the opposition in those areas. That's the progress we made as a team. We always had the right intent and that's why we have been rewarded with this win."

Kohli admitted that he had been playing mind games before the Sydney Test when he had said that he didn't care about making history. "It's obviously a very proud moment. It's the cherry on top of the cake," he said. "We didn't want to be a one-match wonder (winning the odd Test).

"So, having stuck to our task, executed what we wanted and got the result, we as a team feel complete now," Kohli said. Then, like an able general, Kohli outlined his vision. "I see this win as a stepping stone to inspire the next lot of Test cricketers. The vision is to promote Test cricket back home," he added.

