A sportsman all his life, the 44-year-old Kolkata man used to play cricket regularly before he was diagnosed with dilated cardiomyopathy, a condition where the heart becomes enlarged and cannot pump blood effectively. After visits to several doctors, he was advised to go in for a heart transplant in Chennai, where he met Dr K R Balakrishnan. 

Rupayan says the doctor\xe2\x80\x99s optimistic outlook gave him the courage to undergo the transplant. 

The donor heart belonged to an Andhra resident who had met with an accident. 

Post-surgery, Rupayan underwent the mandatory recovery period. In four months, he started walking. He gradually increased his speed and started slow running too. He then registered for a 5-km run conducted by the BSF, a decision that left his wife and other family members aghast, for obvious reasons. Rupayan says the reassurance from Dr Balakrishnan settled their nerves. \xe2\x80\x9cI was told to be active. I felt I was ready to do a 5-km run for the martyrs of BSF. I felt attached to the cause. Luckily, the run went well.\xe2\x80\x9d 

Having completed a few successful runs, he is now looking forward to the Mumbai Marathon, where he will participate in the 21-km half marathon. \xe2\x80\x9cI have never run in Mumbai and this will be my first big marathon,\xe2\x80\x9d he says, as he gets reflective about the man whose heart beats in him now. \xe2\x80\x9cHe was a young man and must have had many dreams and aspirations. I feel like meeting his family, telling them their son\xe2\x80\x99s heart is fine, running marathons, finding happiness,\xe2\x80\x9d Rupayan says, adding, \xe2\x80\x9cUnlike the West, we are not allowed to meet the family of the donor. But I hope through the media I will be able to trace them one day and thank them.\xe2\x80\x9d

Rupayan is now a keen advocate for organ donation, not just the heart. \xe2\x80\x9cIn heart transplant, doctors always look for a healthy heart. If a person has a healthy organ and he donates it after his/her death, he just helps another human being live some more years peacefully,\xe2\x80\x9d he says.

Meanwhile, when asked if such patients need to take special precautions, Mumbai-based heart transplant surgeon, Dr Nandkishore Kapadia, said they only need to be vigilant in the first year after the transplant. \xe2\x80\x9cIn the West, it is not uncommon for heart transplant patients to go on treks, mountaineering or ultra-marathons. It only shows how people\xe2\x80\x99s lives change for the better after heart transplant. They have a new heart, which is basically like getting a new pump, and they can be as active as they want to be.\xe2\x80\x9d Dr Kapadia said cadaveric heart donations were fewer in Mumbai because of several myths. \xe2\x80\x9cMany heart failure patients don\xe2\x80\x99t want to undergo a transplant due to fear. Hopefully, after hearing a heart transplant patient is running the marathon, this mindset will change.\xe2\x80\x9d

