                    })()



            What if we picked up the rules of business success from these organisations and applied them to our professional lives? Here are seven lessons from how successful companies approach growth, which apply even as we seek to unlock our own career growth:

1) Start with and be obsessed about your consumer: The core strategy behind any company that truly dwarfs the competition and creates massive customer loyalty is to anticipate and meet customers\xe2\x80\x99 needs in a way that no one else can. These are the organisations that create raving fans of their products/service. If you aspire to career growth, you can\xe2\x80\x99t be complacent. And the first step to staying alert and agile is staying attuned to the customer you serve, the consumer of your wares. Listening intently to their pains, to appreciate their unmet needs helps you shape the agenda for where you can add value in whatever role you play. By investing in staying close to them and expanding the boundaries of what you understand, you become more valuable to your team, customers and company. In business, we talk a lot about finding a target customer segment and a product or service that addresses the demand space for that target group. Likewise, success in your role within the organisation comes from sharply defining your priority customer pool and prioritising your agenda and offering. Are you clear on where you play? 

2) Know your core, build a unique value proposition: Your value isn\xe2\x80\x99t based on who your employer is or what\xe2\x80\x99s your job title. It\xe2\x80\x99s based on what you stand for and what you are able to bring to the table. This often starts with a strong understanding of what makes your heart sing. The unique combination of your passions, your skills, your beliefs and the insights from your experiences comes together like a secret sauce to help you reflect on your USP. What is it that you do extremely well and how can you bring it to the fore in solving some of the key challenges that your business or consumer is facing, thereby leaving a strong impact? On the other hand, outside the organisation, it helps you narrow down the list of potential customers (or employers) that you can best help and who you would most enjoy working with, given your unique skills, passions and preferences. Have you found that sweet spot yet for yourself?

3) Build that brand: Your personal brand is something you\xe2\x80\x99re constantly building, because it\xe2\x80\x99s the affirmation of who you are. Whether you\xe2\x80\x99re a full-time employee, freelancer or business owner, your personal brand speaks before you in any interaction. Being intentional about what you want it to stand for is perhaps the most critical aspect that shapes the opportunities you attract. For your personal brand to truly shine, it is important that you spend time in each project, role and stage of your career \xe2\x80\x94 not just learning, but also delivering. Even if once every year, you take the time to record what you\xe2\x80\x99ve learned and accomplished, soon you will have a rich archive of material that you can use to deepen your self-awareness, hone your career goals, and reflect on the value you create. While on personal branding, if I had one piece of advice in building your own brand, it would be, \xe2\x80\x98Show, rather than tell\xe2\x80\x99. Just as great marketing of a lousy product doesn\xe2\x80\x99t go too far, professionals who talk more than they deliver end up being the butt of jokes, losing even more credibility in the process. You will always need to promote your product or service. But if you give more value than they expect, if you surprise and delight them with added value, they will reciprocate in kind, sharing stories of your terrific impact. When was the last time that you had your customers positively surprised by how you anticipated and delivered? Can you think of some opportunities to try that in your current role? 

4) Strive for something larger, leave a legacy: If you think that leaving a legacy is something only large corporations or CEOs can do, you can\xe2\x80\x99t be farther from the truth. Each role and career stint allows you to leave your unique stamp. And it is that stamp that gives you the licence to unlock the next big bet. Have you done something in this role that your successors will remember you for? If not, is there an opportunity for you to do that now?

5) Nurture partnerships that propel: Apple and Nike, Cornetto and Oreo \xe2\x80\x94 there are so many examples of brands or even companies coming together to unlock growth. Personal partnerships are no different. Establishing and nurturing long-term, mutually beneficial relationships without expecting instant gratification or return is a crucial skill if you desire to unlock your career. These partnerships could be in the form of mentors (within or outside your organisation), industry peers, or even team members you have invested in developing. All these can be just as significant sources of perspective and support as one reflects on career choices \xe2\x80\x94 very often, they also help you stay on top of the latest trends in your industry, keep a pulse on the market, meet prospective mentors, partners and clients, and gain access to the necessary resources that will foster your career development. Have you invested in nurturing a diverse set of career sponsors and partners?

6) Never settle, take that risk: Successful businesses are agile, keep on testing and learning, and never complacent. They are perpetually listening to the consumer and undertaking small pilots to unlock the next growth phase. If they didn\xe2\x80\x99t do that, they could be the next dinosaur. Likewise, not taking risks is perhaps the riskiest career move of all. In today\xe2\x80\x99s marketplace, where jobs and job categories are being destroyed and invented at an accelerating rate, the riskiest move one can make is to assume that your industry or job is secure. I\xe2\x80\x99m certainly not suggesting that we be reckless and take a 180-degree turn in the pursuit of something new perpetually. However, career growth needs us to constantly and intently listen to feedback. Seek it on an ongoing basis from your customers and your managers. That will give you the cue to know when to change track. Equally, listen to your inner voice. If there is a chasm between the work you enjoy and the work that pays you, it\xe2\x80\x99s time to take that risk as no time will be riper than today to experiment with something that\xe2\x80\x99s closer to your passion. I strongly believe that risk-taking isn\xe2\x80\x99t for entrepreneurs only. Rather, conscious, consistent and modest risk-taking can help us become more able to recognise opportunity when it strikes.

7) Give back to society: Organisations that thrive are admired also because they contribute to a larger cause. They give back more than they take from society and the community they serve. Iconic professionals gain respect and grow because they make time to give back. Are you making the time to mentor others? Are you engaging the fraternity you belong to and sharing perspectives and learning? Are you helping others grow? If yes, you are indeed well on the path of a fulfilling and thriving career journey. 

Reflect now if any of these are under-indexed for you, and create your action plan to hand-craft the next phase of your career growth. All the best.

 The writer is director (HR), Mondelez India


'

b'MUMBAI: Lenders to Jet Airways, which defaulted on loan obligations last week, have three options to recover their dues, bankers have told TOI even as they are unlikely to extend any additional credit to India\xe2\x80\x99s second-largest carrier. 


The lenders, led by SBI, will not release cash until they have a look at the forensic audit report by EY on Jet\xe2\x80\x99s business viability. Jet, which is in loss, is under pressure to raise funds before it is classified as a non-performing asset (NPA).


