India coach Ravi Shastri's insistence on bringing in Bharat Arun as the bowling coach was largely based on Arun's time spent at the National Cricket Academy as chief coach. The evolution of the current batch of pacers, comprising Ishant Sharma, Jasprit Bumrah, Mohammed Shami, Bhuvneshwar Kumar and Umesh Yadav, can be, to a large extent, credited to Arun's experience of working on the supply line.



Here is the catch now. Arun quit the NCA in August 2013, but the board is yet to get a bowling coach at the academy, let alone hire a designated chief coach. Entangled in legal issues since the spot-fixing saga in 2013, the board has struggled to sustain a strong structure through the U-23 and U-19 levels.

Paras Mhambrey, bowling coach of India U-19 and India 'A' teams, has been entrusted with the job of preparing the next line of pacers. Over the last three years, Mhambrey has catered to the senior team's demands and has worked on the accuracy of upcoming pacers without them having to compromise on pace. That the NCA lies dormant for most part of the year has been a given for the past few years. New pace find Khaleel Ahmed had told TOI that he was left all by himself after the U-19 World Cup in 2016 at the NCA since he was not part of the India 'A' teams.

"We conduct camps in summers where Mhambrey supervises the fast bowlers while people like WV Raman, Kiran More and Narendra Hirwani work on batsmen, wicketkeepers and spinners respectively. Players these days are playing for most of the year. That is the only time (summer) they are available," BCCI GM (cricket ops) Saba Karim told TOI while pointing at the system where the workload of all the 'identified' pacers is being monitored.

"We are looking at enhancing our coaching staff at the NCA. We will bring in more people now," the former India wicketkeeper and selector said.

Given the constraints in the current BCCI set-up, selectors have devised a strategy to give more pacers exposure to quality training. "The selectors have decided to rotate the bowlers for the India 'A' team as well, irrespective of performances. It will give Mhambrey and India 'A' coach Rahul Dravid more options to work with," a source close to the selectors told TOI.

A theory going around in the BCCI circles is that the board has not created a lucrative post at the NCA for real big names to apply for the job. "The board has advertised for posts like a consultant. There needs to be the post of a director or a chief coach for people to get interested. At the moment, there is only a chief operating officer at the NCA who doesn't have a cricketing background and hence struggles with the nuances of the sport," a top BCCI official told TOI.

'

