            \xe2\x80\x9cInvestors were seeing negative returns in their portfolios on a year-to-date basis. This turned out to be dampener,\xe2\x80\x9d said D P Singh, executive director and chief marketing officer, SBI MF.\xe2\x80\x9cNew inflows were affected as the markets had not done well. The MF industry had significant inflows in the last three years. But investors are now booking profits,\xe2\x80\x9d said Chandresh Nigam, MD & CEO, Axis MF. \xe2\x80\x9cThere are concerns about the global markets as well,\xe2\x80\x9d he said. \xe2\x80\x9cSome long-term investors in equity MF schemes are booking profits ahead of elections. The returns from the equity (MF) category have also not been good in the last one year,\xe2\x80\x9d said Gopal Agrawal, senior vice president, investments, DSP MF.All the equity MF categories with the exception of funds that invest in shares of information technology companies have given negative returns in the last one year. In contrast, MFs that invest in fixed income securities including liquid funds and FMPs (fixed maturity plans) have gained 6.9%-8% during the timeframe. \xe2\x80\x9cInvestors are also showing more interest in debt funds and FMPs (fixed maturity plans) as the yields are high,\xe2\x80\x9d Singh said. Incidentally, inflows into SIPs (systematic investment plans), the bulwark of investor interest in equity MF schemes, have remained stagnant at around Rs 8000 crore, during the last three months. SIPs now account for about 80% of the inflows into equity MF schemes.SIPs are done almost entirely in equity funds. The fixed income segment contributes only about 5% in volume terms and about 2% in value terms to overall SIPs. \xe2\x80\x9cInflows into balanced funds that were around Rs 15,000 crore per month has come down to Rs 3,000 crore,\xe2\x80\x9d industry officials said. Inflows into equity MF schemes would remain tepid till the general elections are over, officials said. \xe2\x80\x9cInflows would be affected till there is stability in the markets,\xe2\x80\x9d Nigam stated.'

b'MUMBAI: Tata Power, Torrent, JSW Energy, Greenko, Sterlite Power and Statcraft are among the 15-plus applicants who have submitted their expressions of interest (EoIs) for IL&FS group\xe2\x80\x99s renewable energy assets worth Rs 8,000 crore \xe2\x80\x94 the second largest vertical after roads.


Bidders for the business, which has wind and solar power assets, include public sector entities and private equity funds as well. The bidders are expected to commence a due diligence process, which includes physical inspection. They will also review the power generation capacity, the power purchase agreements with governments, the tenure and longevity of the projects and the outstanding debt. 


