                      $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                    })()




However, as it increasingly became difficult to go greenfield, since the rewards from the market for scale became disproportionately higher and India became more integrated into the global economy with companies now competing on the world stage, it became imperative for Indian companies to acquire domestically to build scale. 

The rationale for domestic M&As are varied: 


Building global scale at a rapid pace: The Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code provides a transparent way to acquire high-quality assets without the risks and litigation associated with these companies. Till recently, promoters of distressed firms were widely seen beyond the law. However, the rules of capitalism got redefined two years ago, and the new law has provided prospective firms an opportunity to bid swiftly for failing companies. The successful outcomes of Bhushan Steel, Binani Cement and others have not just validated the process, but given the incoming bidder unprecedented scale and market share. A quick integration of these companies is already getting these players the reward from shareholders and financial markets.


Being nimble around disruption: The domestic consolidation theme is not restricted to the bankruptcy process alone. Rapid consolidation among domestic players is the way forward in many industries. In the new world order, where constant disruptions are challenging existing business models, it has become imperative to be nimble while building scale. The merger of Idea-Vodafone was a deft response to the disruption being created by a deep-pocketed new entrant Reliance Jio, while also giving the players greater control of their most prized asset \xe2\x80\x94 spectrum. India is riding a wave of digital adoption, as a result of which innovative startups are emerging rapidly, posing a threat to larger industry players who are bolstering their own digital capabilities. Axis Bank\xe2\x80\x99s acquisition of FreeCharge demonstrates the need for lenders to move up the technology chain to compete with the nimble fintech players.


Entry into new segments: The merger of Bharat Financial with IndusInd Bank and acquisition of BSS Microfinance by Kotak Mahindra led the two large banks\xe2\x80\x99 entry into the micro-lending sector, providing them instant access to the large customer base of the target companies, which they could never have built so swiftly on their own and helped them set up an enviable \xe2\x80\x98priority\xe2\x80\x99 portfolio that they need to build by mandate. These mergers became valueaccretive and increased the earnings per share immediately. As consumers across sectors are looking at a \xe2\x80\x98onestop solution\xe2\x80\x99 for all their needs, it has become imperative for companies to provide the \xe2\x80\x98Amazon\xe2\x80\x99 solution and acquire alternate segments to retain customers. Flipkart\xe2\x80\x99s acquisition of Myntra was a direct response to consumers\xe2\x80\x99 response to have complete fulfilment at one stop.


Acquisition of brands and markets: The acquisition of Unichem by Torrent Pharma, where the latter outbid global private equity players, provided the Ahmedabad-based pharma major 120 valued brands, giving them an inherent advantage in the increasingly competitive domestic market. These brands are business enablers that helped Torrent reach new customers and markets. Recently, Hindustan Lever merged with GSK Healthcare in an all-stock deal, giving the country\xe2\x80\x99s largest pure-play consumer goods company access to large and strong brands like Horlicks and Boost, making the acquisition a very strategic and transformative move for HUL. Using the same distribution channel, the acquirer can push diverse products into the market, thereby enhancing existing margins. Similarly, the acquisition of Ranbaxy by Sun Pharma gave it unprecedented scale and high-quality brands, making it the largest pharma company in India and the 5th largest generic player globally.

Of the total M&A volume of $100 billion in 2018, consolidation in India was $60 billion, showing that domestic M&A is here to stay as a strong theme for the next few years and local players are going to give a fierce run to the global players as everyone tries for a meaningful foothold in one of the world\xe2\x80\x99s fastest growing economies. More complex deal structures will also be seen as the market matures, whether it is share-swaps, reverse mergers, stock deals, asset sale, transfer of brands and earn-out structures. The year 2019-2020 should continue to see increased domestic M&A volumes with Indian companies getting improved access to overseas capital, the booming consumer market, digital transformation that the economy is undergoing and the \xe2\x80\x98winnertakes-it-all\xe2\x80\x99 approach of investors, financial markets and creditors to the business.



(The writer is CEO of Moelis India)
'

