b'SBI, the country's largest lender, has selected underwriters for an institutional share sale that could raise at least Rs 10,000 crore ($1.4 billion), people with knowledge of the matter said. The state-run lender picked Bank of America, CLSA and HSBC Holdings to arrange the offering, according to the people, who asked not to be identified because the information is private. Kotak Mahindra Bank and SBI Capital Markets were also chosen to work on the deal, the people said.


SBI's sale will help bolster its capital buffers as it attempts to grow loans at a faster pace. Credit growth in the country\xe2\x80\x99s banking system is rising at the fastest pace in five years after a cash crunch in 2018 curtailed new loans from shadow financiers. The deal will add to the $11.8 billion raised through equity offerings in India over the past 12 months.


