            In the latest list, there are four members of an European team called \xe2\x80\x98Sans Domicile\xe2\x80\x99 who were caught. As a result of this, the team has lost its Contenders League spot. It can only re-join again with a set of new players.

To prevent such events from happening in the future, PUBG Corp has announced that the professional players would have to undergo some serious background checks of their accounts before they even enter into a competition.


The cheating program uses "radar hacking"

Shedding a little light on the cheating program, PUBG Corp has said that this is an "unauthorized program, the type of which has been severely damaging the integrity of the game". According to vieesports.com, this program uses radar hacking: a way of cheating which uses a VPN to read game data packets being sent to servers.

PUBG recently banned four professional players on account of suspected use of unauthorised software and the investigation confirmed the suspicions. The four professional PUBG players to be banned were Christian \xe2\x80\x9cCuhris\xe2\x80\x9d Narvaez, Liam \xe2\x80\x9cLiammm\xe2\x80\x9d Tran, Tyler \xe2\x80\x9cDevowR\xe2\x80\x9d Sti and Mark \xe2\x80\x9cTefl0n\xe2\x80\x9d Formaro. Now, these players will not be able to participate in all official esports competitions for three years, starting December 31, 2018.

