Bravo, who made his international debut in 2004 against England in Georgetown, represented West Indies in 40 Tests, 164 ODIs and 66 T20Is.

In his 40-Test career, Bravo scored 2,200 runs, including three hundreds and 13 fifties. He also picked up 86 wickets in the format. In his 164 ODI appearances, Bravo scored 2,968 runs and claimed 199 wickets, while his T20 International statistic sheet shows 1142 runs and 52 wickets.

His last ODI appearance was way back in 2014 against India in Dharamsala.

Timesofindia.com caught up with Bravo during the recent T10 League in UAE. The 35 year old spoke about his cricketing journey, the Indian Premier League, West Indies cricket, playing under Mahendra Singh Dhoni and Brian Lara and much more.


You entertained fans with your batting, bowling and fielding for almost a decade-and-a-half. How will you sum up your international cricket journey?
It\xe2\x80\x99s good. I had some good times and some bad but most of it was good. I am a positive type of individual. I am really happy with whatever I have achieved in my career. My fans love to watch me play. In sports, you need to be an entertainer and you need to entertain people. That\xe2\x80\x99s how I get to drive my passion. I have been doing something I love. It was my motto since childhood to live my dream and have success in it. I think my career is highly decorated.


You are 35 and you still play the way you did 15 years back. What made you decide to retire from international cricket?
I could have achieved a lot in international cricket, but it was cut short. I am happy that I was able to represent West Indies at the international level. Not too many people get the opportunity to represent their country. I am fortunate enough to have been a part of the West Indies set up for more than 12 years. I enjoyed every moment of it. I haven\xe2\x80\x99t achieved much (at the international level), having not got too many of opportunities. Such is life. There are some things you don\xe2\x80\x99t have control over. But my career went on and my brand developed (and became) even bigger without playing international cricket because I was able to play around the world and fans welcomed me.




Do you think, T10 is the future of the shortest format of the game?
I won\xe2\x80\x99t say it is the future of the game. I would say it will add to the game and it is another opportunity for players. I am happy that a new format has been added to the game of cricket along with Test, ODI and T20. Let\xe2\x80\x99s go with the flow and see where cricket goes. May be T10 can go to the Olympics.


Who was the best captain you played under and why?
MS Dhoni is the best captain I played under. I have played two years under (Brian) Lara who is also a good captain. But I have played most of my cricket under Dhoni and have most of my success there (in the IPL). Be it as an individual or collectively as a team. We have won trophies together.



West Indies are two-time ICC ODI World Cup champions (1975 and 1979). They have also won two World T20 titles (2012 and 2016). They are a competitive side in limited overs cricket. What should they do to improve as a Test team?
I follow West Indies cricket. But I don\xe2\x80\x99t know what they should do to improve on their Test record. It\xe2\x80\x99s not my place to say what they need to do. I talk to most of the players individually. I wish them all the luck and I am ready to pass on the knowledge, whatever I have. They are going through hard times now.


Do you think, Dhoni along with captain Virat Kohli can take India to the 2019 ICC ODI World Cup title?
All 10 teams have an opportunity to win the World Cup.



'

