Rohit Sharma started the 2016 ODI series with a bang, smashing an unbeaten 171 at the WACA Ground in Perth on January 12, 2016. India lost Shikhar Dhawan (9) early after then captain MS Dhoni won the toss and opted to bat. But Rohit and Virat Kohli (91) added 207 runs for the second wicket to deny Australia any advantage. Rohit smashed as many as 7 sixes and 13 fours in his swashbuckling knock that came off 163 balls to power India to 309/3 in their 50 overs. Rohit reached his fifty off 63 balls, 100 off 122 deliveries and 150 off 155 balls. This was Rohit's third ODI hundred in Australia and it brought him at par with VVS Laxman, with three ODI centuries in Australia. Rohit then went on to score his fourth ODI ton in Australia in the next match of the series in Brisbane.

Steve Smith: 149 in Perth (2016)


After Rohit Sharma's heroics, skipper Steve Smith led the Australian run chase in the same match in Perth, smashing a 135-ball 149 with the help of 2 sixes and 11 fours. Australia were tottering at 21/2 when Smith and George Bailey came together at the crease in the fifth over. The captain and former captain put up a 242-run stand to defy India. Smith reached his century off 97 balls and he and Bailey (112) ensured the Aussies finished on the winning side, to take a 1-0 series lead in the five-match series.


Yuvraj Singh: 139 in Sydney (2004)


One of the cleanest strikers of the white ball, Yuvraj Singh mesmerised the crowd at the Sydney Cricket Ground with a fabulous knock of 139 on January 22, 2004. Yuvraj had announced himself at the international stage with a fluent 84 against Australia in Nairobi in 2000. But the knock in Sydney is etched in most cricket fans' memory because of the clean hitting and the precision with which runs flowed from the blade of Yuvraj. Such was the elegance in Yuvraj's strokeplay that he over-shadowed VVS Laxman (106) with effortless ease. Yuvraj reached his second ODI century off 104 balls and smacked 2 sixes and 16 fours in his knock to propel India to 296/4 in their 50 overs.


Rohit Sharma: 138 in Melbourne (2015)


In the second ODI of the Tri-series in 2015, Rohit Sharma notched up another special knock, this time the stage was the magnificent Melbourne Cricket Ground on January 18, 2015. MS Dhoni chose to bat first but Rohit was left to wage a lone battle as the other Indian batsmen fell like nine pins. The 138 runs that were caressed off Rohit's bat came off 139 deliveries with the help of 4 sixes and 9 fours. The next highest scorer in the Indian line-up was Suresh Raina (51). Rohit's knock helped India post a respectable 267/8 in 50 overs but it wasn't enough to prevent an Indian defeat.


Matthew Hayden: 126 in Sydney (2004)


Talk of a big score by Matthew Hayden and this bludgeoning image of the burly Aussie opener comes to mind. It was exactly like that. The Aussies were at the height of their invincibility in cricket and Hayden pulverised the Indian bowling attack in the second final of the VB Series at the Sydney Cricket Ground on February 8, 2004. After Australian captain Ricky Ponting opted to bat, Hayden tore apart the Indian bowling attack, hitting 3 sixes and 11 fours in his 126-run knock that came off 122 balls. Hayden reached his century off 95 balls and his 157-run stand with Damien Martyn (67) powered Australia to 359/5 before an out of sorts India were crushed by 208 runs, as Australia won the best-of-three-finals 2-0.

'

