It is to be noted that only 7% of the yearly quota of employment-based and family-based green cards can go to applicants from the same country, no matter the size of its population. And only 10,000 visas are given out per year in some categories, like \xe2\x80\x9cother\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cunskilled workers\xe2\x80\x9d. It is here that Chinese green card applicants have caused close to a 12-year backlog.

Categories can get backlogged even further if a large number of applicants from the same country flock to the same category, given that such categories can take up only certain fractions of total yearly green cards

As of April 2018, a total of 306,601 Indian nationals \xe2\x80\x94 mostly IT professionals \xe2\x80\x94 were in line for green cards. Indians account for a whopping 78% of the 395,025 foreign nationals waiting for green cards in just one category: employment-based applications.

With some lawmakers planning moves to abolish at least some of these quotas, India and China could dominate the green card queue in the future \xe2\x80\x94 at least until the backlog is cleared.

