Did the former French Open doubles champion have a valid point about Federer being the beneficiary of favourable scheduling? There was no doubt in the minds of ESPN commentators Patrick McEnroe and Chris Evert, who, while calling for some sort of balance, also pointed out that players like Federer and Serena Williams were simply reaping the rewards for their immense contribution to the game.

\xe2\x80\x9cIs there favouritism for the top players at majors when it comes to court assignments and schedule? Absolutely, there is, okay!\xe2\x80\x9d McEnroe said in a conference call ahead of the 2019 Australian Open. \xe2\x80\x9cDoes it need to be held in check at times, called out a little bit? Of course. We\xe2\x80\x99ve done that plenty of times at ESPN when it comes to why is Venus Williams on Court 2 at Wimbledon, for example.

\xe2\x80\x9cThe fact of the matter is that the players who get the better scheduling or the better courts, et cetera, basically deserve it. At the end of the day, the people running the events are looking at filling the seats and keeping the sponsors happy. That\xe2\x80\x99s part of the game and part of the deal.\xe2\x80\x9d

Evert, a tennis legend herself, agreed. \xe2\x80\x9cIs there a little bit of favouritism towards them? Yes. But they\xe2\x80\x99ve earned it. It\xe2\x80\x99s all about earning it,\xe2\x80\x9d said the 18-time Grand Slam singles champion.

