b'An appearance on a talk show with a leading filmmaker should be a massive brand boost for two up-and-coming cricketers. Maybe that\xe2\x80\x99s what Hardik Pandya and KL Rahul thought when they sat on 'Koffee With Karan\xe2\x80\x99s couch earlier this week. But what transpired over the next one hour was nothing short of what they would call a series of false shots in cricketing parlance. Pandya uttered one sexist remark after another with a nonchalance that would have made Donald Trump proud and Rahul giggled along. The two cricketers are now facing criticism from all corners and they only have themselves to blame for this hit wicket. They aren\xe2\x80\x99t the first international cricketers to be so blatantly sexist on prime-time TV, just the first Indians. The episode has highlighted the need of sensitising young cricketers who many believe live in a \xe2\x80\x98bubble\xe2\x80\x99 due to the adulation they receive. The Indian cricket board has proposed that not only do these two cricketers not be allowed back into the team without proper sensitisation but the exercise be extended to all centrally-contracted cricketers in India.


Players won\xe2\x80\x99t be welcome into the team without proper sensitisation: BCCI
This is the first time that the BCCI has had to deal with their players \xe2\x80\x93 and star players at that \xe2\x80\x93 indulging in casual sexism on prime-time TV. Their initial response has been quick. The Committee of Administrators issued show cause notice to both Pandya and Rahul, asking the cricketers to respond on why they made the crass comments, and also said they were mulling banning players from appearing on non-cricket shows too. A BCCI source tells us, \xe2\x80\x9cThe comments have really angered the top brass because they were considered unworthy of a national cricketer. Administrators feel that it reflects badly on the BCCI and Indian cricket as a whole. As a result, we feel cricketers under contract with the BCCI should not be allowed to appear on non-cricket shows.\xe2\x80\x9d In fact, the officials have made it clear that the two players will only be back in the team after undergoing a sensitisation programme. BCCI Treasurer Anirudh Chaudhary told PTI, \xe2\x80\x9cThe players must be allowed to join the team (if selected) only once they have gone through a proper sensitisation in addition to serving a ban, if imposed upon them. In any case the entire team and support staff must go through a sensitisation process.\xe2\x80\x9d


