BCCI considering extending sensitisation to all contracted playersMany in the cricket fraternity feel that the solution does not lie in a blanket ban on TV appearances of the players but in sensitising and educating them. The BCCI officials say that they are considering expanding the sensitisation exercise for all centrally-contracted cricketers. \xe2\x80\x9cWe don\xe2\x80\x99t want such instances to be repeated so we will make sure it doesn\xe2\x80\x99t happen again. We are exploring all avenues for that, including having sensitisation for the players in the central contracts list. They should behave like representatives of India at all times and conduct themselves with the dignity that the tag demands,\xe2\x80\x9d says a source from the board. Currently, there are 26 cricketers in BCCI\xe2\x80\x99s central contracts list across four categories- A+, A, B, and C. Both Pandya and Rahul are in the B category, which gives them a retainer of `3 crore per year, apart from match fees.



Sensitisation is the need of the hour: experts
Experts associated with the game say it\xe2\x80\x99s high time that sensitisation be mandatory for all international cricketers. Commentator Harsha Bhogle was the first to demand such an exercise when he tweeted, \xe2\x80\x9cIt is all very well to issue show-cause notices now to KL Rahul and Hardik Pandya. But I do wish the BCCI spends time in sensitising these young players to life beyond the dressing room; to getting them outside the bubble that is inevitable given the adulation they get.\xe2\x80\x9d Others say that sensitisation needs to be expanded to even those playing at the grass root level. Fellow commentator Gautam Bhimani tells us, \xe2\x80\x9cNo doubt, there is a need to sensitise the young players, and that needs to begin when they are young colts \xe2\x80\x93 14-15 years of age. That\xe2\x80\x99s when scouts see them and sign them with their agencies. That gets both the player and agents money but parallelly, we must ensure that the players are trained on how to conduct themselves in the media and how to be sensitised. That training needs to begin before they are exposed to fame and money. That needs to begin at the grass root level and while it will be facilitated by the player agents, the BCCI must take the lead in it.\xe2\x80\x9d


International parallel to such sensitisation exercises exists
There is an international parallel for this exercise. Australia engages all their centrally-contracted players in sensitisation and media training every season. Bhimani informs that the Australian cricket authorities have a three-day workshop for their players every year, which he attended this season as part of the broadcasters\xe2\x80\x99 team. \xe2\x80\x9cCricket Australia has this media workshop for their centrally contracted players before the start of every season there but it\xe2\x80\x99s much more than what the name suggests. They have former players, broadcasters, and media advisors talk to the players. It\xe2\x80\x99s high time BCCI starts something similar because it is their responsibility. They can simply get media personnel, psychologists, and senior cricketers who are respected and they can talk to the youngsters. It doesn\xe2\x80\x99t have to be a grand process,\xe2\x80\x9d he tells us.


The team does not align with those views: Virat Kohli
We as the Indian cricket team and responsible cricketers don't align with those views, those were individual opinions... The two concerned players felt what has gone wrong and they have understood the magnitude of what\xe2\x80\x99s happened. Definitely it has to hit anyone hard, they will definitely understand the things that have not gone right... From the Indian cricket team's point of view, this changes nothing in our beliefs in the change room, it does nothing to the spirit we have have been able to create
\xe2\x80\x93 Virat Kohli at a press conference in Sydney on Friday


'

