            While choosing mutual funds, you should ask following questions yourself to get clarity about the fund you are going to invest in- \xe2\x80\x98what is your financial goal?\xe2\x80\x99, \xe2\x80\x98when do you expect your returns?\xe2\x80\x99, and \xe2\x80\x98what is risk tolerance\xe2\x80\x99. You would find it easier to choose the asset class if you define these parameters.

Based on investment objective, mutual funds are classified into four, such as Growth/ Equity schemes, Income/Debt Schemes, Balanced funds, and Money Market/Liquid Schemes. 


Growth/ Equity Schemes
The Growth or Equity schemes are ideal for investors who have mid-term or long- term financial goals to achieve. This will provide capital appreciation to investors over a period of time. In this scheme, investors can prefer different options such as dividend option, capital appreciation, etc. Compare to other funds, growth funds have high risks. 


Income/ Debt Schemes

The income or debt schemes provide regular income to investors. In income schemes, investors can prefer different investment schemes such as bonds, corporate debentures, Government securities and money market instruments. Such funds are comparatively less risky.


Balanced funds

The balanced fund schemes are suitable for investors who are looking for moderate growth. It provides both growth and regular income. In balanced funds, Investors can prefer different investment schemes such as equities and fixed income securities. 


Money Market/Liquid Schemes

The Money Market or Liquid Schemes also provide moderate income. These schemes are convenient for easy liquidity. This scheme aims to preserve the capital of the investor. In this scheme, investors can put money in safer short-term instruments such as treasury bills, certificates of deposit, commercial paper and inter-bank call money, government securities; etc.


