The tool uses AI, machine learning and behavioural analytics to spot the various locations a particular account is simultaneously active at, and provides real-time reports to the clients, who can either turn this into incremental revenue or take steps to curb the practise. 

Synamedia cited data from research firm Park Associates to note that over $9 billion of pay-TV revenues and $1.2 billion of OTT revenues are estimated to be lost to credentials sharing by 2021.

\xe2\x80\x9cWe are targeting Credentials Sharing Insight at any OTT service provider that wants to combat the rapid rise in account sharing between friends and families and turn it into a new revenue-generating opportunity,\xe2\x80\x9d Jean Marc Racine, CPO and GM EMEA of Synamedia, told TOI in an email response.

With regard to how OTT players can benefit from the tool, Marc Racine said it is up to each Synamedia customer to determine how they intend to use it. \xe2\x80\x9cCredentials Sharing Insight provides marketing teams with detailed insight that can be used to help them tread the fine line between finding account sharers and harassing a customer,\xe2\x80\x9d he said. The product is in trials with a number of OTT providers in the developed markets, but Synamedia did not mention the names of the clients. Synamedia currently sells its other services to large media companies including AT&T, Disney, Sky Media, and Verizon, among others.

OTT player Netflix, whose pricing in India is steeper than domestic competitors Amazon Prime Video and Hotstar, declined to comment on TOI\xe2\x80\x99s queries about the tool and tackling such practices.

Netflix has three subscription packages for Indian users. A monthly fee of Rs 500 grants access to one screen at a time, Rs 650 for a maximum of two devices and full-HD quality, and a monthly fee of Rs 800 offers Ultra-HD 4K video content for simultaneous viewing in up to four screens.

\xe2\x80\x9cThe Netflix service and any content viewed through our service are for your personal and non-commercial use only and may not be shared with individuals beyond your household,\xe2\x80\x9d Netflix also adds in its terms of service. 

However, despite all these checks, industry trackers note a rampant practice among users -both Indian and global- to bring down costs by sharing accounts beyond defined limits, and it remains to be seen if an algorithm will stop them from doing so.


