b'Aniket Sule is a man who must pick his battles. Otherwise, as a scientist who likes to bust myths, this country can give him too much to do. Last year, when Union Minister Satyapal Singh famously said Darwin\xe2\x80\x99s theory of evolution was \xe2\x80\x9cscientifically wrong\xe2\x80\x9d, it was Sule \xe2\x80\x94 a research fellow at Homi Bhabha Centre for Science Education at TIFR \xe2\x80\x94 who promptly filed a petition asking him to retract his statement. But, increasingly he finds that what prevails over fact in the land that gave birth to clinical minds such as Ramanujan is a potent cocktail of mythology and bad science fiction featuring Vedic flying machines, ancient missiles and gods with engineering skills. 

\xe2\x80\x9cRidiculous\xe2\x80\x9d is how he describes the two notorious speeches at the recent Indian Science Congress (ISC) at Jalandhar, where Andhra University vice chancellor G Nageshwar Rao said that the Kauravas of the Mahabharata were born due to stem cell and test tube technologies while another speaker, K J Krishnan, dismissed the theories of physicists Isaac Newton and Albert Einstein as wrong. \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s not enough for scientists to ignore such ridiculous claims,\xe2\x80\x9d says Sule. \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s important for us to tell the layman that these claims are not true.\xe2\x80\x9d 



