            This sense of duty is now brewing a steady myth-busting movement in India where scientists, academics and other groups like the Breakthrough Science Society (BSS) are resorting to hard data, petitions and marches to tame the rabidly mutating beast called \xe2\x80\x98pseudo-science\xe2\x80\x99.

While Sule \xe2\x80\x94 who defines pseudo-science as \xe2\x80\x9canything that uses scientific jargon but not the process of science\xe2\x80\x9d\xe2\x80\x94 recalls a 2001 move by then HRD minister Murli Manohar Joshi to introduce astrology into university curriculums as the first state-backed attempt propagating pseudo-science, it is really in the last five years that political agendas have mixed with religious folklore and creative storytelling, making scientists despair.

 As Prof Jayanth Murthy, director, Indian Institute of Astrophysics, puts it: \xe2\x80\x9cI am not concerned about all the silly stories as science needs its crackpots to fuel it further. However, I am concerned about important people saying dumb things.\xe2\x80\x9d Bharat Ratna recipient Prof CNR Rao says he stays away from these science congresses. \xe2\x80\x9cI don\xe2\x80\x99t go there, fearing my presence may endorse some of these claims. What\xe2\x80\x99s worse is that such lectures are being delivered to children.\xe2\x80\x9d

To UK-based science writer Angela Saini \xe2\x80\x94 whose new book \xe2\x80\x98Superior: The Return of Race Science\xe2\x80\x99 will touch upon the political nature of pseudo-science when it is published in India this year \xe2\x80\x94 such claims smack of \xe2\x80\x9creligious nationalism\xe2\x80\x9d. \xe2\x80\x9cWhen political figures make outlandish claims about ancient flying machines, they are relying on a false body of knowledge gleaned not from hard evidence but by misreading certain religious texts. The reason they do this is to whip up a sense of Hindu religious pride, to undermine the truth for their own political ends, not unlike the Nazis did in Germany in the early 20th century. It was dangerous then, and it is dangerous now,\xe2\x80\x9d says Saini, who sees no reason for Indians to resort to fake, unsubstantiated claims to have pride in their history. \xe2\x80\x9cIndia has a very strong place in global scientific history, as the birthplace for key mathematical ideas, metallurgical technologies and home to the sophisticated Indus Valley civilisation, which had weights and measures. It has been home to such great thinkers as Ramanujan and Bose,\xe2\x80\x9d she says.

The problem is there are many urban, educated, working professionals who fall for the seductive conspiracy theories that mix history, science and legend. One major catalyst for this is WhatsApp. \xe2\x80\x9cEven those who don\xe2\x80\x99t know how to separate chalk from cheese now believe they are experts because they have read WhatsApp forwards,\xe2\x80\x9d says Sule, who quietly saves a collection of such unverified WhatsApp science forwards to be used as material in his myth-busting workshops. He has also made it a point to attend gatherings such as a recent talk titled \xe2\x80\x9cVaimanika Shastra\xe2\x80\x99 (Aeronautical Science) in Mumbai, which was organised to glorify the contributions of ancient Indians to the world so that he can challenge the speakers. \xe2\x80\x9cWe are never taught to question authority,\xe2\x80\x9d says Sule, explaining India\xe2\x80\x99s impoverished scientific temper. This is partly why, along with fellow activists, Sule decided to announce August 20 as National Scientific Temper Day last year and even conducted talks across schools in Mumbai where students asked them questions such as: \xe2\x80\x9cIs it okay to cut your hair on Saturday?\xe2\x80\x9d 

There are also efforts such as Breakthrough Science Society of India\xe2\x80\x99s March for Science \xe2\x80\x94 where people march every year to defend science and scientific outlook \xe2\x80\x94 which are part of the artillery against the war on pseudoscience.

Its other sturdy weapon, of course, is hard data. In 2017, Alt News, a fact-checking website, launched a section on science in 2017 precisely to take on misinformation with evidence-based research. Poker-faced, this section forensically analyses everything from BJP MP Dr Subramanian Swamy\xe2\x80\x99s tweets claiming that \xe2\x80\x9cadmission of women in menstruation period in Sabarimalai was to protect the women from mutation from gravitational matrix\xe2\x80\x9d to AYUSH ministry\xe2\x80\x99s promotion of magnet therapy to cure diseases. 

Sweden-based neuroscientist and science editor of Alt News, does not write about outlandish claims as that is "not impactful at all". Instead, she writes about stories that people rely on expert advice to differentiate between evidence-based science and pseudo science such as as the one tackling vaccine-related rumours for instance..\xe2\x80\x9cEvery time there is a vaccination drive by the government, someone spreads rumours that it kills children or contains anti-fertility agents. These rumours can prove dangerous as vaccines promise immunity from a host of diseases,\xe2\x80\x9d says Shaikh.

Predictably, fighting the good fight extracts its toll. \xe2\x80\x9cWe know what happened to Dabholkar and Pansare, right?\xe2\x80\x9d says Sule, referring to the slain rationalists Narendra Dabholkar and Govind Pansare and adding that he draws strength from TIFR\xe2\x80\x99s supportive leadership. Despite the high stakes, though, the importance of intellectuals and other groups taking on pseudo-science cannot be understated. \xe2\x80\x9cIf we can\xe2\x80\x99t assert the facts then what can we do?\xe2\x80\x9d asks Saini. \xe2\x80\x9cLive in a world in which people think the Earth is flat?\xe2\x80\x9d 

'

b'Reservation has always been a contentious issue, and the move to introduce quotas for upper caste poor has reignited the debate. Ashoka University professor Ashwini Deshpande, who specialises in the economics of affirmative action, tells Sunday Times that economic disadvantage is better addressed by poverty alleviation programmes


The upper caste argument used to be that quotas were anti-merit. What happens to that position now with government pushing an upper caste quota? 


