                    })()



            The complete absence of a hostile reaction to this announcement \xe2\x80\x94 the self-righteous breast-beating about the death of \xe2\x80\x9cmerit\xe2\x80\x9d that we see in response to caste-based quotas \xe2\x80\x94 exposes upper caste hypocrisy. It is clear that their opposition is not to quotas per se, but to the inclusion of lower castes into educational institutions and jobs, arenas that the upper castes feel entitled to. This is not the only instance of hypocrisy: there has never been any upper caste opposition to management or domicile quotas. Their opposition is not to quotas, but to the entry of lower castes. 


How much can quotas influence elections? 
There is a total mismatch between the numbers seeking government jobs and the number of jobs available. Recently, 20 million people applied for 100,000 railway jobs. So, reserving an extra 10% of a very small and shrinking pie is not going to satisfy this huge demand for jobs. I hope the electorate is smart enough to see this as an empty offering and a poll gimmick. Having said that, populist announcements can sometimes influence elections, if the electorate is desperate and looking for a solution to their economic troubles \xe2\x80\x94 Donald Trump in the US and Jair Messias Bolsonaro in Brazil cashed in on economic discontent, offering completely twisted solutions. 


The income criterion for this quota has been fixed at a household income of Rs 8 lakh per annum? Does that really qualify as poor?
Not at all. Around 99% of the rural population has a household income of Rs 8 lakh per annum. An entry-level university lecturer living alone, not poor by any stretch of the imagination, would qualify as \xe2\x80\x9ceconomically backward\xe2\x80\x9d by this calculation. 


What do you think of the shift from caste and social backwardness markers to economic ones?
The rationale of reservation or affirmative action is that it counters systematic group-based disadvantage and discrimination. If poverty elimination is the goal, there is no need to have affirmative action, we need anti-poverty programmes that generate employment, create livelihoods, and help people acquire assets. This move is the wrong one if the government is genuinely keen to address economic disadvantage. 


Given what caste-based reservation has achieved since Independence, is it the best way to create equal opportunities for all? 
There is no single \xe2\x80\x9cbest\xe2\x80\x9d way. There has to be a package of measures that would counter discrimination for groups that are marginalised and stigmatised. Equal opportunity means that everyone should have an equal chance to compete without discrimination and prejudice. It is a reality that Dalits and Adivasis, as well as some religious minorities such as Muslims, on account of their group identity, not income, are excluded from positions and decision making. Reservation is one element in the package of policies that redress this balance somewhat. It is not a complete solution, but an important part of the solution. 


People often get ridiculed for getting into colleges because of quota and not merit. How can egalitarianism be balanced with individual effort?
Those who get in outside of the quota are those who qualified on the basis of their exam marks. While their individual talent cannot be denied, it is also true that they had all kinds of advantages \xe2\x80\x94 good schools, exposure to books, travel \xe2\x80\x94 that created the conditions for them to study. Those higher up in the caste hierarchy are more likely to have these advantages. Higher marks are a combination of innate ability and favourable circumstances. I am not going into the question of whether exam marks are a good indicator of \xe2\x80\x9cmerit\xe2\x80\x9d \xe2\x80\x94 that is a highly debatable question. 


Does affirmative action make its beneficiaries complacent, less competitive? 
There is absolutely no evidence to support this mythical belief. The competition for reserved positions in prestigious institutions like IITs, IIMs or for UPSC jobs is huge. It is a myth that an SC or ST candidate can just walk into these positions. They have to compete against a large number of other reserved category applicants. This is because these coveted positions are too few in relation to the number of candidates. 


Globally, where has affirmative action worked well and what lessons can we draw from there?
Affirmative action is challenged by socially dominant groups. It is under attack pretty much everywhere. A comparison is not straightforward as the context differs. India has quotas, US has preferences, Malaysia has 30% equity quotas for Malays, etc. But everywhere, the fact that there is a middle class among marginalised groups is due to affirmative action. See the contrast between the Dalit middle class in India and Nepal. India has a much stronger and bigger Dalit middle class, and opportunities for upward mobility because of affirmative action. 

'

b'There are many things we want to talk about with family and friends; mortality isn\xe2\x80\x99t one of them. But from death workshops to death cafes, there are plenty of signs that this is changing
Lying in a mass burial pit, it\xe2\x80\x99s hard to not think to yourself: how in the world did I get here? For the 12 participants who undertook this mock exercise at a Delhi workshop recently, the answer was not really morbid. They were there because they wanted to experience the past, present and future of death.



