The Committee of Administrators (CoA) Vinod Rai and BCCI CEO Rahul Johri on Saturday, CoA member Diana Edulji said: "We should be in no hurry to conduct the inquiry" because then it will appear like a "cover-up job".

Edulji is of the firm belief that "with 2 players coming back, the selectors are in the process of sending the replacements immediately and as such the team will be full strength."

The former India captain wants the office-bearers to be involved in taking a decision on the quantum of punishment/penalty that should be meted out to Pandya and Rahul. Citing the example that office-bearers of the BCCI have been asked to depose in front of independent committees in the recent past, Edulji told Rai: "I do not understand why you seem to have a problem with the office bearers advising us in the matter as they are still a part of the system."

While the tussle between CoA and the office-bearers is not new, in this instance it puts the careers of the two cricketers - one 25 years of age and the other 26.

Pandya and Rahul now face the threat of missing out on all cricket for a while. In fact, given the internal political twist being provided to the whole incident, some officials in Indian cricket administration strongly believe the two cricketers stand a good chance of "missing the 2019 ICC World Cup".

Adding to the drama, in a letter addressed to the chairman of in absolute jeopardy. One BCCI office-bearer is convinced that the two players need to be banned anywhere between six months to a year and be made an example of. Speaking to TOI, the office-bearer said: "Indian cricket is an institution. And it cannot be disrespected in this manner".

Edulji cited examples to make her point. A) 82 years ago (1936), Lala Amarnath was sent back from India's pre-Independence tour of England by then captain, the Maharaja of Vijianagaram, for alleged insubordination. B) In the recently concluded football World Cup a Croatian (sic) player was sent back after their first game on disciplinary grounds, Croatia went on to win the World Cup".

"We can't even tell her that it wasn't Croatia but France that won the Cup. She has firm views," said Edulji's colleagues.

Some office-bearers of the board said that "strictest action should be taken." When asked if they're fine with the two players missing out on the World Cup, they said: "Let the investigations decide that."

