But the controversy was kept alive, partly by continued allegations by co-founder NR Narayana Murthy. Eventually, they became a big part of the reason why then chairman R Seshasayee, CEO Vishal Sikka, and several members of the board resigned.

The turbulence forced co-founder Nandan Nilekani to return to the company as chairman. Under Nilekani and the new CEO, Salil Parekh, Infosys conducted a strategic review and found that Skava and Panaya did not fit into the direction they wanted to take the company. While Sikka wanted to give a product orientation to Infosys, under which Panaya and Skava apparently fitted, Nilekani and Parekh wanted to reinforce Infosys's services strengths.

"When companies do these acquisitions, they need to do financial, legal and business due diligence to make it a successful investment," said KR Girish, founder of accounting firm KR Girish and Associates. Panaya had been acquired for $220 million, and Skava for $120 million. In April 2018, Infosys said it expected to sell these assets valued at Rs 2,060 crore ($316 million) and having liabilities of Rs 324 crore ($50 million). In June quarter, the company said it reduced the fair value of Panaya by $39 million and of Skava by $18 million.

But on Friday, at its third quarter results announcement, Parekh said based on the evaluation of proposals received, and negotiations with potential buyers, the company had concluded that it was no longer "highly probable" that the sale would be completed by March. "We now plan to repurpose Skava's business and refocus on Panaya's suite of products," he said.

