                      console.log(isIndia && randomNumber < 0.9 );
                      if(isIndia){
                        // code for showing ctn ad
                         $('#fluid-container').remove();
                         return;
                      }	
                      $('.top2brdiv').remove();
                      $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                    })()




Second, she is routinely bombarded with investment ideas. Property dealers swarm her office and she needs a secretary to just look at the deals. She is unsure but many of them have been bought in the names of her relatives to save tax apparently.

Third, household expenses have skyrocketed, with number of relatives visiting or living in. She trusted her parents to manage her money and while her parents tell her everything is fine, she is not sure.

Fourth, accounts are kept by her father and the secretary, who also negotiate with directors and producers. They decide which products she will promote and the fees. The income tax returns are also filed by them. She signs documents brought to her.

Fifth, there are business ventures that are always being discussed and despite her protests, money is invested in miscellaneous enterprises. These ideas are driven by relatives who live with her, who siphon off money based on their interests.

Sixth, there are men who profess they love her, and offer to take charge of everything. But she can\xe2\x80\x99t trust them, as they are either married, or they are not doing well professionally and may leech off her.

I am appalled looking at the situation. So, how can we help her? First, the high income in this line of business comes with high risk. One flop, and her market rates will change overnight. She also runs the risk of staying for long periods without income. So, she should build assets. A corpus that is set aside and invested in financial assets that can be drawn on when needed is critical. Also, she must buffer her income, by creating an alternative investment income when regular income runs low.

Second, she should ensure that assets, including property, are held in her name, even if that means paying higher tax. She should view everything other than the house she is living in as investments that she will roll over when required.

Three, she must put a limit on the parasitical bleed her income suffers from the hordes of relatives that live off her. She should take back control of her income and hand over a fixed amount to her parents, within which all expenses need to be managed. Her income should be discreetly managed by her secretary and accountant. For this, she should hire and work with good quality staff for these positions.

Fourth, she should place a premium on keeping her financial transactions above board. She will be able to cut out all the operators from her life, if she chooses to hold assets in her name, bill her fees to her bank accounts, and file income tax returns. She will have complete control and freedom to use her money without fear. For this, she can creating a small office of professionals who will set up and manage her affairs.

She has to ring fence her wealth. Who would have thought that there is a problem like having too much money?


The author is Chairperson of The Centre for Investment Education and Learning


'

