            Crisil Ratings senior director Sachin Gupta said, \xe2\x80\x9cA reduction in interest rates will improve the prospects for sale of projects. In these projects, debt servicing is the highest component of expenses.\xe2\x80\x9d The increase in interest cost could be one of the reasons for the decline in bid amounts for the government\xe2\x80\x99s second TOT package, he added.

While last year the government saw the road assets offered under the first TOT package receive bids of Rs 9,681 crore, which is a 55% premium over the base price, the second round \xe2\x80\x94 yet to be awarded \xe2\x80\x94 saw the highest bid at a 14% discount to the base price of Rs 5,362 crore for a 30-year concession. 

Despite the bidding for NHAI projects coming at a discount, it is evident that there is continued interest, given the 30 bids for IL&FS road projects valued at between Rs 25,000 crore to Rs 30,000 crore.

\xe2\x80\x9cThe key reason why the transaction is positive for both parties in the transaction is because of the difference in expectation in terms of return. The long-term equity investors in annuity road projects are OK with a 12-13% return, while the developers typically expect a higher return of around 16-18%,\xe2\x80\x9d said Gupta.

The sales are also a big positive for banks. \xe2\x80\x9cSince the sale cannot happen without a \xe2\x80\x98no objection certificate\xe2\x80\x99 from lenders, any transaction will be positive for banks as they will not allow a deal to go through that is not in their interest.\xe2\x80\x9d


'

b'Chennai: Two wheeler companies are seeking a reduction in the GST slab to distinguish between an item of mass mobility and luxury goods. Currently two wheelers are clubbed along with luxury goods in the highest GST slab of 28%. Although auto bosses in the segment including TVS chairman Venu Srinivasan and Hero Motocorp chairman Pawan Munjal have been vocal about this discrepancy, so far the GST council has not changed the rates.

Auto bosses say two wheelers are an item of mass mobility and therefore extremely price sensitive. The segment is already facing pricing pressure on account of the new insurance slabs as well as the impending BS6 norms. \xe2\x80\x9cGiven the importance of the two-wheeler segment as an item for mass mobility, the GST rates for two-wheelers undoubtedly needs to be reconsidered. It certainly cannot be benchmarked against the prevalent GST rates for luxury goods at 28%, especially given the current state of inconsistencies with the integrated multi-modal public transport systems across India. More so with new safety norms and BSVI integration also around the corner, both of which will increase two-wheeler prices,\xe2\x80\x9d said Venu Srinivasan, chairman, TVS Motor Company.



