2019: Upcoming new SUVs in India18Jaguar I-PaceJaguar electric SUV I-PACEHyundai KonaHyundai's electric SUV KonaAudi e-tronAudi's electric SUV called e-tronJeep RenegadeJeep Renegade SUVBMW X4 SUVBMW X4 SUVTata HarrierTata Harrier SUV Nissan KicksNissan KicksMG Hector SUVMG Motor SUV 'Hector' teaser image

The number of new SUVs is expected to be more than the cumulative launches of sedans and hatchbacks in the country in 2019.






Car buyers seek sturdier vehicles with more power, greater safety The launch roster follows a strong consumer trend where buyers have increasingly shown a preference for sturdier vehicles that offer higher power and greater road safety, not to mention enhanced ground clearance to better manoeuvre rough terrain.

The SUV charge will kick off at the beginning of the year itself with Nissan rolling out its Creta competitor, the aptly named Kicks, on January 22. Expected to be priced upwards of Rs 9 lakh, the model will come in petrol and diesel options.





Tailing the Kicks's launch will be Harrier, the ambitious off-roader from Tata Motors that may be priced upwards of Rs 12 lakh. Sporting the 2-litre diesel engine (that also does duty on Jeep Compass), the Harrier is developed on a Land Rover platform that also underpinned the British brand\xe2\x80\x99s Discovery Sport model. Harrier will compete with the Compass and Mahindra XUV5OO and Tata hopes to achieve the same success with it that it managed with Nexon.

Tata is also expected to drive in a smaller SUV\xe2\x80\x94priced under Rs 10 lakh-\xe2\x80\x94during the year.

On the luxury front, BMW will go all guns blazing. The German major will introduce the X4 (expected price of around Rs 60 lakh) and X7 (over Rs 95 lakh) off-roaders for the first time and also bring in the new-gen version of X5 (Rs 70 lakh). Rival Audi will introduce the powerpacked Audi Q8 (over Rs 1 crore), which will be a step-up of the muscled Q7.

Mercedes Benz will launch the new version of its GLE (around Rs 65 lakh), while Audi will introduce the new Q3 (around Rs 35 lakh). Audi is considering whether it should introduce the Q2 mini SUV, while Mercedes is studying the possibility of GLB (placed between GLA and GLC SUV models).

Apart from Kona electric, Hyundai will drive in a mini SUV, code-named QXI, to compete with Maruti Brezza and Ford Eco Sport at an entry price of around Rs 8 lakh. Also, readying for a rollout is the Jeep Renegade, which will be placed under the Compass SUV and may enable many to join the Jeep family with an entry price of around Rs 10 lakh.

Skoda\xe2\x80\x99s Karoq, new versions of Renault Duster and Range Rover Evoque (around the existing price points), too, are expected to hit the road this year.

Looking beyond SUVs, there will be some exciting launches in sedans and hatchbacks, too, with the Honda Civic (Rs 18 lakh onwards) to make a comeback in the former category. Also, the much awaited Tesla Model 3\xe2\x80\x94which had generated considerable excitement, and bookings, in India when it came out in April 2016\xe2\x80\x94is expected to debut in the country (through the import route).

Further, new-gen versions of BMW 3-Series, Audi\xe2\x80\x99s A8 and Toyota Camry hybrid will also come out in 2019 while, among hatchbacks, there will be upgrades to the Maruti WagonR and a facelift of the Maruti Baleno and Ford Figo.

