For instance, the ITBP has sent a four-member team, headed by their deputy commandant, an inspector and two sub-inspectors to shortlist candidates from 13 sports, including judo, athletics, shooting, kabaddi and more. 

\xe2\x80\x9cWe have picked over 150 athletes and will look for more...Once the Games are over, we will send call-letters to the selected candidates and the recruitment will be done once they fill the forms and other details,\xe2\x80\x9d said sub-inspector Viraj Singh, who is also a shooting coach at the ITBP.

\xe2\x80\x9cNormally, if we shortlist 150 candidates, over 75 join us, and rest opt for other opportunities,\xe2\x80\x9d he added. Singh said that they have selected 36 candidates alone from judo, while 18 have been taken from track and field disciplines.

While ITBP are offering call letters almost immediately after the Games, CISF will be first creating a database and then monitor the potential candidates\xe2\x80\x99 performance for some time. 

\xe2\x80\x9cWe keep recruiting young talented athletes for our sports wing. Since these Games have thousands of athletes at the same venue, it has become easier for us to create a database of potential recruits. We may not offer a job straightway to the shortlisted athletes, but we will mark them and will follow their performances in the coming months. As and when we have job openings, we start rolling offers to the selected ones,\xe2\x80\x9d said former Commonwealth Games champion shooter Samaresh Jung, who has been sent by the CISF to hand-pick talent.

