Coach Stephen Constantine \xe2\x80\x94 whose contract was going to expire at the end of the month \xe2\x80\x94 immediately stepped down, saying his work in India was done and that he had guided the national team to a better place.

The Englishman had taken over the reins for the second time in 2015 and under him, India rose from 173 \xe2\x80\x94 their worstever Fifa rank \xe2\x80\x94 to their second-best position of 96. However, his last season in charge was marred by controversy, with players, including the talismanic Sunil Chhetri, allegedly unhappy with his tactics and revolting against him.

As India now head into an important era, Menezes believes a more illustrious appointment as Constantine\xe2\x80\x99s successor is the need of the hour.

\xe2\x80\x9cWhen Constantine came in 2015, there were no age-group teams in place. The interest in Indian football too was just building. The 2017 U-17 World Cup changed everything for the better. We now have age-group teams in place. More importantly, we have age-group leagues. We have a proper system in place so we can attract better coaches. Constantine did very well especially in developing footballers but we need someone who will take us a step forward,\xe2\x80\x9d Menezes told TOI.

Former India skipper and captain of the team that won the Asian Youth Championship in 1974, Shabbir Ali, says that while the importance of hiring the right coach is important, what should take priority is ensuring that local players are part of the I-League and ISL teams.

\xe2\x80\x9cIf you have local players in a team, more people come to the stadium to support the team. The more people come to the stadium, the better it is for football,\xe2\x80\x9d said Ali. \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s also imperative that the focus should always be on the youth teams. The AIFF has started focussing on them only in the past couple of years and they\xe2\x80\x99ve done a good job so far, but they need to sustain their efforts if India has to become a superpower.

\xe2\x80\x9cI believe we have quality players in the country but they need the right tactics when playing for the national team. Only a good coach can do that and I hope the AIFF makes a right choice,\xe2\x80\x9d he said.

NorthEast United FC defender Keegan Pereira says that though India finished last in the group, there are only positives to take from the tournament.

\xe2\x80\x9cThe truth is that is that everyone thought we were going to get hammered in the group stage. Nobody thought we stood a chance. But people are sad because the team really showed that we have the potential to be up there,\xe2\x80\x9d said Pereira. \xe2\x80\x9cWe were very unlucky against UAE (2-0 loss). That\xe2\x80\x99s football and it happens. Had Chhetri netted that header we would be having a different conversation right now. Now isn\xe2\x80\x99t the time to be sad, it\xe2\x80\x99s the time to support the national team even more.\xe2\x80\x9d

Mumbaikar Pereira, who wore the India jersey on four occasions, says that India need a coach who understands the entire setup.

\xe2\x80\x9cWe actually have quite a few Indian coaches who have gotten the required (coaching) badges. We are such a vast nation and so I feel it\xe2\x80\x99s better to have a coach who knows the system inside out,\xe2\x80\x9d he said.

'

b'WASHINGTON: Using even a small amount of cannabis may cause changes in the brain volume of teenagers, according to a study.


The study, published in The Journal of Neuroscience, is the first to find evidence that an increase in grey matter volume in certain parts of the adolescent brain is a likely consequence of low-level marijuana use.


