b'Coimbatore: The increase in diesel prices has not had any impact on truck rentals which continue to decline due to dip in cargo offerings from factory gates since Nov 2018. Over the last 10 days while diesel prices rose by Rs 2 per litre, truck rentals dipped by 1.5%-2% according to the Indian Foundation of Transport Research and Training (IFTRT). Even in November and December the decline in truck rentals by 10%- 12% (5%-6% in each month) outpaced the reduction in diesel prices. This has led to a steep fall in fleet utilisation, which already has been burdened with indiscriminate fleet expansion of high tonnage goods carriers across all segments, for the last 10 -12 months, IFTRT said. \xe2\x80\x9cThus, under the present dull mood in the manufacturing sector, truck sales would remain tepid in this quarter and negative growth may extend up to September 2019,\xe2\x80\x9d said S P Singh, senior fellow and coordinator, IFTRT. Truck rentals/ retail part-load freight charges have been tumbling since November due to steep drop in manufacturing growth. Full round trips have fallen by 15%-20% in the last ten weeks including during the first half of January 2019, IFTRT stated. \xe2\x80\x9cThe situation is becoming grimmer with 1.5%-2% drop in truck rentals on trunk routes,\xe2\x80\x9d Singh said.Though the supply of cargo from fruit and vegetable production centres to APMCs (Agricultural Produce Marketing Committees) across the country has gone up by 10%-15%, sales of consumer durables, FMCG (fast moving consumer goods) and general merchandise from factory gates have been tepid from various industrial hubs, IFTRT said.

