            We prod him for his opinion on the current political scenario in an election year in the world\xe2\x80\x99s largest democracy. \xe2\x80\x9cUntil a year ago, it was looking like Narendra Modi will come back (to power). But, after the recent elections in some states, it looks difficult. My personal view is we ought to give one more term to Narendra Modi. A 10-year term will help him do a lot more things which he couldn\xe2\x80\x99t achieve in the first term,\xe2\x80\x9d Jindal said.

In the second term, Jindal believes a Modi-led government could pursue some unfinished tasks, such as disinvestments. \xe2\x80\x9cI don\xe2\x80\x99t know if there is any country in the world that has got such a big public sector play other than China. Narendra Modi is one person who believes that the government has no business to be in business. I have nothing against the public sector, but it lacks accountability. There are problems with decision-making. These, and other factors, erode the value of the public sector,\xe2\x80\x9d he said.

The 59-year-old, however, doesn\xe2\x80\x99t see a hung Parliament emerging out of the national elections, likely to be held by May. \xe2\x80\x9cIf all the opposition parties are coming together, they will somehow manage (to pull off forming a government), but how long will that government last will be the question. A \xe2\x80\x98khichdi sarkar\xe2\x80\x99 (coalition government) would be a disaster for the country. There should be one national party in the lead \xe2\x80\x94 it could be the BJP or Congress,\xe2\x80\x9d Jindal said. \xe2\x80\x9cBut whether the Congress will lead the opposition pack is a question.\xe2\x80\x9d Jindal\xe2\x80\x99s younger brother, Naveen Jindal, is a former member of Parliament from Congress.

According to the industrialist, political uncertainty could put a pause on overseas investments. But he does not foresee Indian businessmen deferring their plans. Jindal, who harbours a childhood passion for cars, is moving ahead with expansion plans for his steel and energy businesses and is also looking to enter new areas like paints, electric cars and home solutions (steel doors and windows). A part of his expansion goals will be met through acquisition of stressed assets such as Bhushan Power & Steel and a few thermal energy units. 

Though Jindal has made the highest offer of Rs 19,700 crore for Bhushan Power, the deal is in litigation. If \xe2\x80\x94 and by the time \xe2\x80\x94 Jindal closes this transaction, it will be an expensive one for the industrialist, who is known to be very value-conscious. This is because steel prices are already heading downhill. Being straight forward is a trait Jindal said he acquired from his father, the late O P Jindal. After starting from what he acquired from his father, Jindal made good strides in building his own business empire. It was only recently that JSW became the largest steel producer in India, ahead of the Tatas. On the other hand, global steel giant ArcelorMittal is yet to get a firm hold here. \xe2\x80\x9cIn the past, ArcelorMittal was not serious about India, but now they are. It takes hard work to build a steel plant in India, but it\xe2\x80\x99s not difficult. If JSW can build and Tatas can build, so can ArcelorMittal build a greenfield plant,\xe2\x80\x9d said Jindal, who was competing with ArcelorMittal in the bidding process for Essar Steel.

For the $13-billion JSW group, which acquired its acronym from the places its factories originated \xe2\x80\x94 first south and then west \xe2\x80\x94 steel is its biggest business, contributing as much as 77% ($10 billion) to its revenues. While business2business (B2B) will remain the foundation of the enterprise, Jindal wants to build a business2consumer (B2C) model and, hence, the entry into electric vehicles, sports and decorative paints. \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s early days. We are trying to get a feel of the (new) sectors, but our core businesses will continue to be steel and power,\xe2\x80\x9d said Jindal.

Jindal said that the softening of steel prices could dent the group\xe2\x80\x99s fortunes, but the enterprise is ramping up efforts to cut costs. The consumer-facing ventures of the group are handled by his son, Parth, who, Jindal believes, complements him. \xe2\x80\x9cHe (Parth) likes marketing, HR\xe2\x80\xa6 and I\xe2\x80\x99m more interested in projects and technology,\xe2\x80\x9d said Jindal, who is the only engineer in his family (Parth is an MBA from Harvard). 

While Jindal doesn\xe2\x80\x99t see the 28-year-old Parth occupying the corner office any time soon, he said the group\xe2\x80\x99s various businesses will be spearheaded by professionals (Sebi rule mandates a split in the position of chairman and MD by April 1, 2020). Jindal holds the CMD position in the group\xe2\x80\x99s listed entities \xe2\x80\x94 JSW Steel and JSW Energy.

With the enterprise expanding its portfolio in an environment that is changing, Jindal \xe2\x80\x94 a fitness enthusiast \xe2\x80\x94 wants the group to be \xe2\x80\x9cmobile\xe2\x80\x9d and \xe2\x80\x9cevolving\xe2\x80\x9d as there are \xe2\x80\x9cno comfort positions\xe2\x80\x9d. \xe2\x80\x9cEvery organisation should take challenges as opportunities and not get worried about how the world is changing,\xe2\x80\x9d said Jindal. The approach is much like his life\xe2\x80\x99s mantra \xe2\x80\x94 to be active and not sit quiet.


'

