            By \xe2\x80\x98Chillar items\xe2\x80\x99 Imran Ali means imitation jewellery, footwear, decorative, knick-knacks and mobile accessories etc that are sold and lapped up in abundance by men and women around the Charminar area.

\xe2\x80\x98Qaida English mei hai kya?\xe2\x80\x99 asks a woman passing by to which Imran Ali replies in the negative. For the uninitiated, Qaida are a series of books for beginners to learn Quranic Arabic.

The shop sells Quran and Hadith that are supplied from Delhi but what is visible from outside are neatly stacked colourful boxes adorned with laces, stones and beads that are made locally as well as procured from places like Jaipur. These boxes are used to gift the holy Quran during marriages and it is during wedding season that Imran Ali\xe2\x80\x99s shop does brisk business.

\xe2\x80\x9cEarlier people were fond of reading, but these days who has the time? Everything is available on the internet. Most of the Qurans that we sell are gifted during marriages in these beautiful boxes,\xe2\x80\x9d he says, adding that the shop also resorts to selling knick-knacks like Ittar and Surma during Ramzaan when the shop stays open till midnight.

His other big worry is the latest rule converting Charminar area into a pedestrian zone, which has hit business as people cannot park their vehicles near the shop anymore.

\xe2\x80\x9cPeople are not used to this arrangement. Business has been hit and we are barely able to save anything. Let\xe2\x80\x99s see how long we can sustain\xe2\x80\xa6I hope things improve in the coming days,\xe2\x80\x9d he adds.

Syed Imran Ali is a member of the third generation running this shop that was set up by his grandfather Syed Hassan Ali and later run by Hassan Ali\xe2\x80\x99s brothers -- Syed Akbar Hussain, Azhan Hussian, Syed Shafi Hussain. Later, Imran\xe2\x80\x99s father Syed Ahmad Ali took over.

Imran\xe2\x80\x99s elder brother, Ashfaq, who also has been involved with the shop for over 20 years, says the number of people visiting Charminar has increased over the years, helping bring more footfalls to the shop but the major difference these days is that business starts picking up only in the afternoon

\xe2\x80\x9cOur elders used to open shop early and wind up by 8pm\xe2\x80\xa6 today, we cannot think of leaving before 11 pm,\xe2\x80\x9d he says, adding that higher footfalls don\xe2\x80\x99t necessarily translate into higher sales as inflation too has taken a toll on business.

While Ashfaq and Imran Ali\xe2\x80\x99s shop is not the only one selling Islamic books, most of the 40-45 such shops in the area have a similar tale of disinterested readers, intensifying competition and rising costs of doing business.


'

