RIL chairman Mukesh Ambani attributed the rise in profit to competitive cost positions and integration benefits accruing from the refining and the petrochemicals businesses. Ambani also said that the share of RIL\xe2\x80\x99s consumer businesses is steadily increasing its contribution to the company\xe2\x80\x99s overall profitability.





Earlier, Ambani had said that he wants the retail and digital services businesses to contribute as much to RIL\xe2\x80\x99s earnings as that of its traditional units like petrochemicals and refining. The retail and Jio units accounted for 18% of RIL\xe2\x80\x99s operating profit in the December quarter of fiscal 2019, up from 9.7% a year earlier.

Ramp up of RIL\xe2\x80\x99s petrochemicals plants also helped in boosting the company\xe2\x80\x99s revenues even as softer refining margins impacted the profitability of the refining division. RIL\xe2\x80\x99s Jamnagar refinery, the world\xe2\x80\x99s largest, earned $8.8 from every barrel of crude it processed, lower than $11.6 it earned in the third quarter of fiscal 2018. A few years down the line, RIL would face competition as Adani and BASF have teamed up to foray into the petrochemicals sector with a Rs 16,000 crore factory in Mundra, Gujarat.

Reliance Jio, which added 28 million subscribers in the third quarter, reported a standalone net profit of Rs 831 crore on an operating revenue of Rs 10,383 crore. But the telco\xe2\x80\x99s average revenue per unit (ARPU) declined to Rs 130 per subscriber per month. In the immediate previous quarter, the ARPU was Rs 131.7. Losses in the oil & gas unit widened due to fall in production at its Krishna-Godavari basin and at its US shale gas operations. Healthy festive season sales and new store openings boosted RIL\xe2\x80\x99s retail unit\xe2\x80\x99s revenues. The unit currently has 9,907 stores across 6,400 towns and cities in the country. 

