The work on Z-Morh tunnel in Jammu and Kashmir, which was also bagged by the troubled infrastructure major, has been stuck since last July. The state-owned National Highways and Infrastructure Development Corporation (NHIDCL), which is responsible for building highways in hill states and north-east, has served a termination notice to the IL&FS for no progress in this 6-km tunnel project.

According to sources, after terminating the contract for the Rs 6,800 crore Zojila tunnel (14.2 km) project, NHIDCL has asked its bank to encash the Rs 122 crore bank guarantee that IL&FS had submitted. \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s up to the government to decide when we should go for fresh bidding. IL&FS had not started any major work after the foundation stone for this project was laid on May 19. We are ready to float tenders even by this month end,\xe2\x80\x9d said an official, who did not wish to be named.

He added the progress of work on Z-Morh tunnel is only 28% and as per the norm if the contract is terminated due to defaults on the part of IL&FS, the company won\xe2\x80\x99t get any amount from NHIDCL. In such a situation, both IL&FS and the lenders will lose their entire investment. IL&FS has told NHIDCL that the entity was facing financial crises but it wants to complete the project. Completion of the two projects would ensure round the year connectivity to Leh and Ladakh region.

The new management of IL&FS which took charge after the government took over the company has held several meetings with highways and finance ministry to find a way out.

Officials said after deliberations, the government on Thursday set up a five-member panel headed by Niti Aayog CEO Amitabh Kant to look into each individual stuck project. The committee comprising expenditure, economic affairs and highways, and NHAI chairman has been tasked to suggest broad guidelines to find amicable resolution in such cases.

\xe2\x80\x9cIf any roadmap is suggested by going out of the contract norms such as paying the amount for actual works done, which can be certified by an independent entity or expert, it has to be same for all contracts. Any special package for IL&FS needs government approval. The suggested guidelines that the panel would come out with will be put before the Cabinet for approval,\xe2\x80\x9d said a source.

Sources said simply terminating contracts without paying any amount in stuck projects may result in litigations, which will further delay the projects and push up the cost as well.


'

