                      $('.top2brdiv').remove();
                      $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                    })()




Despite posting some impressive results in Group matches, Kerala were finding the knockouts of Ranji Trophy too steep a mountain to climb. That\xe2\x80\x99s when TC Mathew, the former president of Kerala Cricket Association and the forgotten man of state cricket these days, and S Ramesh, Director Games Operations of KCA, got Whatmore to the state more than two years ago. 

Even before he had the first camp with the Kerala players, the experienced coach had identified the problem areas of the team. 

\xe2\x80\x9cFrom what I understand, the team has gone so far, but unable to take that last step. But the fact that they go so far shows that they have talent. Cricket, like most sports, is played in the head. So if we can increase their knowledge and relieve stress, then they would be able to give you that extra effort and that\xe2\x80\x99s what I will try and do,\xe2\x80\x9d Whatmore had said in an interview with TOI soon after landing in Kochi two years back.

And in the last two years, the Australian has walked the talk. If he managed to take Kerala to the quarterfinals of Ranji Trophy last year, this year he has taken them a step further.

The biggest contribution that Whatmore has made to the Kerala side is that he has turned them into a fierce fighting unit. Even when they were skittled out for a paltry 63 in their match against Madhya Pradesh and conceded a huge lead of 185 in the first innings, the way they fought in the second innings through a century from Vishnu Vinod and gave their rivals a run for their money before going down by five wickets won them many admirers.

Like with Sri Lanka, where he brandished the revolutionary idea of aggressive starts in ODI cricket by opening with Sanath Jayasuriya and Romesh Kaluwitharana and asking them to have a go at the new ball, Whatmore adopted horses-for-courses policy with Kerala team too.

In their must-win last group match against Himachal Pradesh, Whatmore sent out Vinoop Manoharan and Sijomon Joseph, primarily in the team for their bowling skills before the season, at the top of the order. The move clicked. Vinoop (96) and Sijomon (23) made significant contributions as Kerala made short work of the target of 297 in their heroic chase on the final day at Amtar. 

\xe2\x80\x9cI feel Vinoop is close to being an all-rounder while Sijomon\xe2\x80\x99s job is to resist the new ball as long as he could. I asked them to do a job in the previous match because I felt they had the skills to do it. But it is one thing to entrust a job and another to carry that out on the field. And I am glad that the two guys vindicated our decision,\xe2\x80\x9d Whatmore told TOI over phone from Krishnagiri.

Skipper Sachin Baby acknowledges the role played by Whatmore in Kerala\xe2\x80\x99s success. 

\xe2\x80\x9cDav has brought in the experience of coaching all over the world to us. It has been a learning experience for the whole team in the last two years. Now everyone knows what their role is before going to a game,\xe2\x80\x9d said Sachin.

But ask Whatmore the reasons for his success, and the Aussie replies: \xe2\x80\x9cI wish I had an answer to that question. It\xe2\x80\x99s for others to speak about my success. I have my own style of coaching. I give the players as much room as they like to both succeed and fail. They understand when I am not happy. But I like to create a positive dressing room environment where both myself and the players communicate with each other freely,\xe2\x80\x9d said Whatmore.

Given Whatmore\xe2\x80\x99s success with the Kerala team in the last two years, the current dispensation in KCA would do well to hold on to him for the next season.

'

