Nine minutes later, Sandro made it 3-1 for CCFC with a free-kick from 35 yards out that thudded into the top left corner after a foul on Nestor Gordillo.

Manzi then took his tally for the season to 13 with a delightful chip over Gurpreet in the 80th minute.

Aizawl, though, weren\xe2\x80\x99t going to go down without a fight. Isak Vanlalruatfela and Lalkhawpuimawia scored within a minute of each other in injury-time to give themselves a chance of salvaging an improbable 4-4 draw, but the hosts showed enough composure.

Apart from the two late goals, there was a slight concern for CCFC at the end when Manzi was substituted in the 88th minute as a result of pain in his groin. They will hope that it is nothing serious, and that the striker is in prime condition for their next game against Real Kashmir on January 28. \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s nothing serious and I am sure he will be available for the next game,\xe2\x80\x9d coach Akbar Nawas said.

In the first half, it was Aizawl who had the first real opportunity when Ivorian forward Dodoz was through on goal in the 27th minute after a defensive lapse from the hosts, but he wasted the chance with a shot that was comfortably off target.

The former I-League champions were made to pay almost immediately as CCFC took the lead in the very next minute. Pravitto Raju did the spadework with a bursting run on the right flank before finding Nestor, who cleverly left the ball for Sandro to fire home with a powerful right-footer.

