Sharapova, the champion here in 2008 and who fell in the third round to Angelique Kerber last year, ran her opponent ragged this time, coming back from 1-4 to claim the first set. Wozniacki, took the second, but the 6'2'' Sharapova, who powered 37 winners, was at the top of her game at the business end of the meeting. She was as patient as she was precise, winning 17 of the 23 long rallies.

In the 20 tournaments that Sharapova has played since her return to competition almost two years ago, she may have won just one title, but has racked up a number of notches, noted names against who she has come out playing her biggest game. She has beaten world No. 1 Simona Halep, then twice outplayed Jelena Osatpenko last year besides scoring over Karolina Pliskova.

Sharapova arrived for her post-match media conference in a black full-sleeved crop top and high-waisted tights.

"It was a good win. I feel pretty happy about it. I'm even wearing a crop top. I don't pull those out often," she said, indicating to her tee. "It was definitely a match that I looked forward to when the draw came out. Obviously, I had to get there first and so did she. It was as expected a physical match," she said.

Sharapova's celebration was emotional, throwing her hands up and blowing kisses to the crowd. Essential to that exchange on the court was the fact that in her 2-and-a-half hours in the middle, Sharapova played the player standing across the net from her, not the person, with who she has exchanged some stinging barbs.

When asked if the win was more satisfying because of their history, Sharapova said: "I really like winning. I'm just really happy and proud of the way I competed today and I'm into the fourth round. So, that's all that matters."

The Russian will play home hope Ashleigh Barty, in the next round.


Rafa, Roger through

In other results, men's second seed Rafael Nadal beat Aussie teen Alex de Minaur 6-1, 6-2, 6-4; defending champion Roger Federer dismissed American Taylor Fritz 6-2, 7-5, 6-2.


Sabalenka upset

Among the women, fifth-seeded Sloane Stephens beat Croatia's Petra Martic 7-6 (8-6), 7-6 (7-5); crafty Aussie Ashleigh Barty, seeded 15, put out Greek Maria Sakkari 7-5, 6-1; big-hitting 17-year-old Amanda Anisimova shocked Belarusian Aryna Sabalenka, seeded 11, 6-3, 6-2; the American teen will next play eighth-seed Petra Kvitova.

'

