Isro chairman K Sivan said here on Friday, \xe2\x80\x9cPSLVC44 is a new variant of PSLV whose last stage PS-4, which usually becomes dead after ejecting the primary satellite, will remain active for six months. This rocket will carry Kalamsat, built by a student startup from Chennai, and Microsat-R belonging to Defence Research and Development Organisation (DRDO).\xe2\x80\x9d Microsat-R weighing 750 kg is an earth imaging satellite that will be used by the DRDO for surveillance and earth observation.

An earlier version of Kalamsat was launched by Nasa in June 2017.

Sivan said the fourth stage or PS-4 will be moved to a higher circular orbit so as to establish an orbital platform for carrying out experiments. He said, \xe2\x80\x9cStudents and young scientists will not have to bother about launching their experimental satellites or modules but can now focus on doing experiments in space as this PS4 will provide platform and power to their satellite.\xe2\x80\x9d

The Isro chief told TOI, \xe2\x80\x9cWe have received seven applications from students for doing experiments in space. Based on requirements from students, we will launch more such PSLV rockets this year so as to enable students to perform tests in space. This facility will be for free for all students.\xe2\x80\x9d Till now, Isro has launched nine student satellites but not in the new variant of PSLV rocket.


Mobiles with NavIC chipset to hit market in a year: Isro chief

Isro chairman K Sivan on Friday said Indian navigation system NaVIC or desi GPS is very much operational and is being used for various civilian purposes like vehicle tracking and for checking coal pilferage and illegal sand mining. \xe2\x80\x9cSome companies have started using Isro-patented NaVIC chipset along with GPS chipset while manufacturing mobiles. In a year\xe2\x80\x99s time, these mobiles with NaVIC chipset will hit the market and enable people to buy NaVIC mobiles for accessing NaVIC signals for navigation purpose,\xe2\x80\x9d he said. Sivan said Isro wants \xe2\x80\x9cground stations in far-flung areas to increase accuracy of our navigation system. Therefore, we are in talks with Russia, Japan and France to set up ground stations there.\xe2\x80\x9d An Isro source on condition of anonymity said NaVIC is also being used by defence forces for missile tracking. 

