How Sudha and the other runners fare on Sunday morning will depend to a large extent on the prevailing weather conditions. "Weather plays an important role in determining the timings that we manage. For example, it was cool in Kolkata so we did well. But here it's generally hotter so it takes longer for us to finish the race," explained Sudha.

Commencing the race slightly earlier has been a constant request of athletes over the years, but that has not been possible owing to a host of factors that are beyond the organisers' control.

There was a controversy last year when Sudha refused to accept the Rs 30 lakh reward for her performances from the UP government, citing she needed a job more than a cash award. But things seem to be fine now and Sudha was all praise for the facilities that are accorded to athletes who are part of the national camp across various disciplines.

"I have seen the transformation over the years and I can happily say that we get the best facilities to prepare for competitions these days. There is no reason to complain," she said.

However, Gopi Thonakal, who emerged the best Indian male runner in Mumbai last year, clocking 2:16:51, is of the opinion that authorities need to do more by constantly educating athletes about the latest developments taking place in their disciplines.

