            \xc2\xb7 Mumbaikars walk the most in IndiaThese are the findings from a 2018 pan-India survey conducted by GoQii, a fitness tracker. Called the India Fit Report, the study analysed data from 7,00,000 users and tracked their health on parameters such as BMI, blood pressure, stress levels etc., over a span of one year, 2017-2018.The survey found that users walked more in 2018 taking 6,783 steps, up from 6,126 in 2017. Mubaikars walked the most, averaging 7,294 steps a day. The WHO recommends taking 10,000 steps a day for good health. Embracing a healthier lifestyle, Indians drank more water in 2018, 2.17 litres compared to 1.5 litre a year earlier. They also slept more averaging 6.85 hours a night, up from 6.54 hours last year. On the flip side, the number of users above 60 with diabetes doubled and who are overweight rose to 57% from 55% the previous year.While Mumbai residents are walking more, they didn\xe2\x80\x99t fare well on other parameters like sleep, water consumed and stress. Ahmedabad turned out to be the most stressed out city with 45% users admitting to being frazzled while overall 40% of the users said they were stressed.GoQii comes out with this report every year and this year\xe2\x80\x99s focus was on health insurance. \xe2\x80\x9cTrust in healthcare has further deteriorated and there is a dire need to reimagine health. We are aiming to change and get people to move away from a reactive and cure based healthcare to a preventive healthcare system,\xe2\x80\x9d said Vishal Gondal, CEO & founder. The report indicates that despite the increasing awareness about the importance and need for an insurance policy, 20% of the respondents still don\xe2\x80\x99t own insurance policy. Approximately 90% of people believe healthier people should pay lesser premiums on their insurance policies.'

b'It could be a hug, a T-shirt slogan or even a tweet. Sedition is a law that can be weaponised at whim to disrupt a person\xe2\x80\x99s life. At best, it is a nuisance \xe2\x80\x94 as Congress\xe2\x80\x99s Ramya found when she tweeted a picture calling PM Narendra Modi a thief. Punjab\xe2\x80\x99s Navjot Singh Sidhu was slapped with a sedition case after he hugged Pakistan army chief General Bajwa. At worst, and for the less privileged, it can lead to months in prison and endless rounds of police stations and courts. Ask the Jharkhand teen who wore a T-shirt emblazoned with \xe2\x80\x98ISIS\xe2\x80\x99 and ended up in police detention or the ten Kushinagar boys arrested for donning Pakistan cricket team T-shirts and JNU students Kanhaiya Kumar, Umar Khalid and Anirban Bhattacharya who were chargesheeted after three years!

According to NCRB (National Crime Record Bureau) data, there have been only two convictions in the last three years. While there has been a spike in registration of cases since 2014, the police have not filed a chargesheet in 80% of the 179 cases registered between 2014 and 2016. But as writer Arundhati Roy pointed out while commenting on Goa-based writer-activist Anand Teltumbde\xe2\x80\x99s impending arrest under the Unlawful Activities Prevention Act, process is the real punishment in India. 



