            Anushka Singh, who teaches at Ambedkar University Delhi and is the author of Sedition in Liberal Democracies, says the use of colonial-era sedition law has popular acceptance in India. \xe2\x80\x98\xe2\x80\x99Despite being read down by the Supreme Court the law\xe2\x80\x99s popular translation as desh-droh or anti-nation has made it a timeless law. In India when nationalism is part of the electoral agenda,government's use of sedition as a political tool meets with public approval,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 she says.


'I didn\xe2\x80\x99t want to live in fear'


Kamalsy Najmal | 40 author-theatre teacher



Exactly a year ago, Malayalam author Kamalsy Najmal, earlier known as Kamal C Chavara, announced that he no longer wished to live as a writer. He is yet to pick up his pen again. Kamalsy was arrested on December 18, 2016 by the Kozhikode police on a complaint by Bharatiya Janata Yuva Morcha, BJP\xe2\x80\x99s youth wing, which claimed that he had insulted the national anthem in a Facebook post that featured an extract from his 2015 novel, Smashanangalude Note Pusthakam (Notebook of Cemeteries.) Widespread protests prompted his release within a day but the ordeal of police questioning over the next month proved too much for him. \xe2\x80\x98\xe2\x80\x99My wife had just delivered our first child four days ago. I had gone to buy medicine when the police arrested me. There was panic at home,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 recalls Kamalsy.

Soon after his release, he lost his job as a theatre instructor in a school. Regular police visits to his elderly parents and hearing and speech-impaired brother continued for months. \xe2\x80\x9cIn prison, I was insulted with caste abuses and threatened. When I was released, my sick parents were harassed. I didn\xe2\x80\x99t want to live in fear,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 he says. Emotional and hurt, Kamalsy burnt copies of his only novel on January 14, 2018, publicly and announced his death as a writer.
The last one year has been hard. Though a chargesheet is yet to be filed against him, he can no longer get a teaching job and the family must survive on his wife Padmapriya\xe2\x80\x99s meagre salary. \xe2\x80\x98\xe2\x80\x99Even today when I think of that night when he was arrested, I am terrified,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 his wife says.


\xe2\x80\x98They spread stories at my kids\xe2\x80\x99 school that I was a traitor\xe2\x80\x99


Shashidhar Venugopal | 60 police reform activist



 Bengaluru\xe2\x80\x99s Shashidhar Venugopal had spent too many nights in grimy PCR vans. As a police constable for 20 years, he had lived through the low wages-long hours-no leave cycle that most cops are familiar with. In 2016, with the support of a vast majority of the 85,000-strong police force, Venugopal announced that the constabulary would go on mass leave on June 4 seeking better working conditions and implementation of police reforms. His NGO, Akhila Karnataka Police Mahasangha, would helm the protest. But in the early hours of June 3, his Yelahanka home was surrounded by police personnel and he was whisked away on charges of sedition. His frightened wife and two teenaged children watched as the police overturned the house looking for documents and incriminating evidence.

Venugopal spent the next 86 days in prison sequestered as a dangerous prisoner. \xe2\x80\x9cThe police went to my children\xe2\x80\x99s school and spread stories about how I was a traitor. My daughter missed her semester exams and refused to go to class. My son, only 16 then, was almost suicidal. It was a very difficult time,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 he says.

But Venugopal did not give up. He fought the 43 cases slapped against him for daring to take on the system. Many have fallen through. In September 2018, the public prosecutor officially told the court that the government was no longer interested in pursuing the sedition charges. The former cop feels that it is a vindication of his convictions. \xe2\x80\x9cI am a patriot. I am the son of a cop who has grown in an environment where the nation is before everyone. No one can question my integrity,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 he says.


\xe2\x80\x98They\xe2\x80\x99re killing our movement. Donors are deserting us\xe2\x80\x99Akhil Gogoi | 42RTI & peasants\xe2\x80\x99 rights activist



139 That is the latest count of police and court cases against him, says Akhil Gogoi. This includes five cases of sedition slapped in the last six months. But if the administration thought this would break his spirit, it has done the opposite. A vocal opponent of the Citizenship Amendment Bill, on January 7, Gogoi spoke about how the concerns of small states like Assam were subsumed in a large federal structure, and there would come a time when states would question this. Three days later, Gogoi learnt he had been slapped with yet another case of sedition. \xe2\x80\x9cJaise main sabse bada criminal hoon. (It is as if I am the most dangerous criminal). This a colonial law used by fascist governments to control dissent,\xe2\x80\x9d he says.

\xe2\x80\x9cThere is an emergency-like situation in Assam, whether it is freedom of speech or action. So everyone is on the streets in protest,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 says the activist, who has long raised the issue of illegal migrants in his state and championed the cause of RTI through his organisation Krishak Mukti Sangram Samiti (KMSS). 

Cases of sedition and inciting public disorder are only ways to create obstacles for a people\xe2\x80\x99s movement, he says. In 2017, he was imprisoned for 107 days under the National Security Act. \xe2\x80\x9cWhen I was in prison, my son was badly affected and sank into depression. He refused to give his exams or go to school,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 he says. The larger impact of the mounting cases has been to make the public question the movement itself. \xe2\x80\x98\xe2\x80\x99The middle class is scared of supporting us. Our donors have dropped from 120 to 90 in just the last couple of months,\xe2\x80\x99\xe2\x80\x99 he says.

