b'Slipping on a shirt, buttoning it up, tucking it into trousers\xe2\x80\xa6what most people do in less than five minutes takes Justin Vijay Jesudas four times longer. In 2009, a spinal cord injury left him paralysed neck down. While he could use his shoulders, and there was some movement in his elbows and wrists, his fingers didn\xe2\x80\x99t function. \xe2\x80\x9cSo, I needed help with buttons and zips. There was never any privacy as someone would have to assist me,\xe2\x80\x9d recalls the 38-year-old para athlete who works as a researcher with Cognizant in Chennai.

Then, one day he discovered adaptive clothing \xe2\x80\x94 shirts that snap up with Velcro strips, zippers with a big ring attached to pull them up and down, trousers with elastic waist. For Jesudas, it meant freedom and dignity. \xe2\x80\x9cI can dress and undress on my own. Last year I travelled alone to the US for six weeks with a set of adaptive clothes. I needed just 10 minutes to dress,\xe2\x80\x9d he adds.



