            Adaptive fashion aimed at people with physical disabilities or impaired movement is part of a larger shift towards inclusivity. And even mainstream designers are dipping their toe like Tommy Hilfiger who launched a special line, Tommy Adaptive, last year and online retailer Asos which sells disabled-friendly clothing.

In India, home to 2.68 crore differently abled persons, there are only a handful of designers who address the issue. One of them is Shalini Visakan. Married to a wheelchair-bound polio survivor, Visakan started tailoring clothes for her husband in 2016, and now has a client base of around 60 in Chennai.

A graduate from the National Institute of Fashion Technology, Visakan closely studied the problems her husband had wearing regular clothes. \xe2\x80\x9cFor instance, while being transferred from the chair to a car, or to a seat on an airplane he would be lifted with his elbows, which was painful and often resulted in dislocation. So I made trousers with strong cloth loops, like handles, on both sides of the waist. The person can be lifted using these loops. Another customisation is making the crotch area of the trousers bigger so it can accommodate a catheter or a urine cup,\xe2\x80\x9d says Visakan. She also designs adaptive saris which look like a sari but are actually structured like nighties that can be easily thrown on.

Amongst mainstream designers, Wendell Rodricks is perhaps the only one who\xe2\x80\x99s tried his hand at inclusive fashion with Visionnaire, a line of tactile clothing for people with impaired vision. Delhi-based designer Amit Aggarwal has been designing a special collection for Tamana, an NGO working for children with special needs, for the past five years but he hasn\xe2\x80\x99t gone mainstream with it.

In Kochi, Joe Ikareth designs a limited edition, Move Ability Clothing, along with his wife Murielle. The couple has a daughter, Tilottama, whose right arm is partially paralysed. \xe2\x80\x9cShe was our inspiration. We have developed clothes that allow her a sense of independence, develop her confidence and change the way people look at differently abled people,\xe2\x80\x9d says Ikareth, a NIFT graduate.

Most differently abled folks mostly go to regular tailors for customisations. \xe2\x80\x9cBut they never completely understand our unique requirements,\xe2\x80\x9d says J D Madan, a wheelchair-bound entrepreneur who now gets his clothes made by Visakan. \xe2\x80\x9cFor example, the seat portion in a pair of regular trousers provides resistance to a smooth and easy transfer from a wheelchair to the bed or to a car seat, which is done using a slide type of board. But in adaptive clothing the butt area of the trousers is made using a slippery fabric to make the transfer easier,\xe2\x80\x9d he adds.

A few years ago, entrepreneur S Prithviraj\xe2\x80\x99s mother lost her eyesight. Desperate to bring dignity to his mother\xe2\x80\x99s life, Prithviraj looked around for suitable clothing. Not finding any, he decided to make it. Old is Gold, his retail store for the elderly in Chennai, also sells adaptive clothes. \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s a small range, nighties and shirts in three sizes, targeted at the elderly who no longer have a full range of movement because of a ill-health or a surgical procedure. The shoulders have buttons so that the garment can be easily slipped over the head. The nighties are ankle-length to prevent falls,\xe2\x80\x9d says Prithviraj, who unfortunately lost his mother before he could set up the store.

Adaptive fashion is still small in India, but it\xe2\x80\x99s made a start. As Madan puts it: \xe2\x80\x9cBeing different does not exclude us from access to public spaces, jobs, education, and travel so why should it deprive us of a smart wardrobe.\xe2\x80\x9d 

