            While a sugar tax has led to decreased consumption of sugar-sweetened beverages in the West (colas, juices, etc), it isn\xe2\x80\x99t something that has worked in isolation. They have other policies in place too, like restrictions on junk food ads or food display. I believe that India needs a much more comprehensive approach to this public health crisis. For example, teach food and nutrition in schools and colleges based on crop cycle, climate and culture and not just as carb, protein and fat, which is a reductionist view that doesn\xe2\x80\x99t help solve the problem. Impose a junk food tax instead of a sugar or fat tax as there are many other harmful and addictive ingredients in ultra-processed packaged foods. Carry warning signs, like on cigarette packets, on processed foods.

Rising obesity in India is not as much a parenting or personal failure as it is a policy failure. Unplanned cities with no room to walk, poor public transport, worsening air quality, farming subsidies to non-native species like palm plantations and weak public health policies are to be blamed.


You suggest giving children chores in the kitchen to teach them about food. But thanks to the mushrooming of food delivery services, haven\xe2\x80\x99t parents themselves stopped entering it?
Yes, they have, and it\xe2\x80\x99s important that parents lead by example. But this too is a multi-dimensional problem. The Indian male is not entering the kitchen and I believe that gender equality starts from the kitchen. Food delivery ads pick up on this. If the mother is busy, the child should order food online, while the father goes scot-free. Our kids don\xe2\x80\x99t get fat in isolation, we create that obesogenic environment for them both at home and at the policy level.


You make a strong case for native foods \xe2\x80\x94 \xe2\x80\x9cwhat your grandmother ate\xe2\x80\x9d \xe2\x80\x94 for children. But isn\xe2\x80\x99t it an impossible task to persuade a child to give up pizza for a ragi roti?
It may seem impossible to say \xe2\x80\x98no\xe2\x80\x99 to a child\xe2\x80\x99s demand for junk food, but the I.Family project (a five-year study across EU) concludes that saying \xe2\x80\x98no\xe2\x80\x99 helps protect children from cardio-metabolic risks in the future. Taking kids to a farm once a month is helpful. It organically teaches them where food comes from, how it nurtures the soil, bees, birds and makes for a healthy, delicious meal too.





List some common myths about child nutrition.
*One myth is that they must finish a set quantity of food to get energy and strength. Appetite is dynamic. It\xe2\x80\x99s important to teach kids to eat according to the satiety signal.
* It\xe2\x80\x99s okay to let them watch TV or iPad if it helps them finish a meal. But eating while viewing means bigger bites, faster consumption and, eventually, digestion issues.
*They must eat sabzi for vitamins, dals for protein and milk for calcium. There\xe2\x80\x99s no one source for one nutrient. Eat a more diverse diet that\xe2\x80\x99s seasonal, local and traditional.






You establish a link between mobile use and big bellies. Should parents bar gadget use?
Well, some tech giants have famously barred their own children from using gadgets till they are 14-15 years of age. Today kids as young as 18 months (if not younger) are glued to the screen. Regulating gadget use is the only way adults can protect kids from the harms of technology. But, parents should first regulate their own gadget use \xe2\x80\x94 not take it to the bathroom, dining table and bedroom \xe2\x80\x94 and only then expect their kids to follow.


Society often tries to fat shame kids into losing weight. Does that worry you?You can\xe2\x80\x99t shame or scare a child off junk food, you must educate them to eat correctly. As parents, lead by example; as schools, inculcate the importance of agriculture and food basics; as politicians, build liveable, walkable cities and towns. My book is an attempt to raise awareness that this is a problem that is bigger than calories-in-calories-out, and that all stakeholders must do their bit. 


You\xe2\x80\x99ve been Kareena Kapoor\xe2\x80\x99s nutritionist for many years. Have you had any role in what her son Taimur eats?
All children, whether they belong to filmstars or not, are heavily influenced by what their parents eat. So, if your parents eat local, seasonal and traditional food, that\xe2\x80\x99s all you will have access to and that\xe2\x80\x99s all that you will eat. 



'

