                      var adContainer = '<div id="div-gpt-ad-1485328452682222244" data-size="fluid" data-adsslot="TOI/TOI_ROS/TOI_ROS_AS/TOI_ROS_AS_Mrec_MID_300" class="webonly alC"></div>';
                      var randomNumber = Math.random();
                      var isIndia = (window.geoinfo && window.geoinfo.CountryCode === 'IN') && (window.location.href.indexOf('outsideindia') === -1 );
                      console.log(isIndia && randomNumber < 0.9 );
                      if(isIndia){
                        // code for showing ctn ad
                         $('#fluid-container').remove();
                         return;
                      }	
                      $('.top2brdiv').remove();
                      $('#fluid-container').append(adContainer);
                    })()



            If there is no motion detected, the apps will simply remain dormant and behave like \xe2\x80\x9cCurrency Converter\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cBatterySaverMobi\xe2\x80\x9d respectively to avoid detection. However, if these apps detect motion, it will simply deploy the payload-- Anubis Trojan. 

The good news is that the Trojan doesn\xe2\x80\x99t get deployed on its own. Both the apps will try to trick the victim to download upgraded versions of the apps, which actually contains Anubis. Once the Trojan get installed in the phone, these apps will secretly try to steal banking information by enabling a keylogger-- programme to record key types-- and also take screenshots. 

So, if you happen to open a banking website on an infected phone, the screenshot of the website along with password and login ID information will get sent to the command centre of the malware creator. This will give him/her access to your banking details. 

The malware is also capable to accessing contact list, location data and can also record audio secretly. It can also make calls or send SMSes covertly without you getting to know. These abilities help the malware to infect other devices from the contact list of the victim.

'

b'DAVOS: Indian billionaires saw their fortunes swell by Rs 2,200 crore a day last year, with the top 1 per cent of the country's richest getting richer by 39 per cent as against just 3 per cent increase in wealth for the bottom half of the population, an Oxfam study said Monday.


Globally, billionaires' fortunes rose by 12 per cent or $2.5 billion a day in 2018, whereas the poorest half of the world's population saw their wealth decline by 11 per cent, the international rights group said in its annual study released before the start of the five-day World Economic Forum (WEF) Annual Meeting in this Swiss ski resort town.


