b'MELBOURNE: There's a certain lightness about Frances Tiafoe. It may be his smile, a reflection of the Melbourne sun, or his age, he turned 21 on Sunday. Perhaps it's his play, racy and powerful. Or, it's just the way the American carries the burden of race, knowing its weight, but never showing it.


The 39th-ranked pro, from the mid-Atlantic state of Maryland, the last man from his country standing in the Australian Open, outmuscled Bulgarian 20th seed Grigor Dimitrov 7-5, 7-6 (6), 6-7 (1), 7-5 in three hours and 39 minutes to make his first major quarterfinals.


