"I think every single player is unique. That's what I love about the sport, I can go out there and play an 'Ash Barty' style of tennis and I know no one else has that. I get to enjoy it," she said. "I don't watch too many of my matches. I have been there and felt it first-hand. Some of my best memories are from shots I played and moments on the court. It's more enjoying the moment and getting to try and develop and create a fun and free game style that I like to play."

Barty, who looked out of sorts when Sharapova clinched the first set, changed tactics, keeping the ball on her opponent's forehand side, her more powerful flank, but it is also known to crack. "I love to play with freedom and fun and try and create as much variety as possible," the 22-year-old said. "Obviously, my game is built around my serve and forehand and bringing in a variety with the slice. But there are no secrets in tennis. I think every day I try and challenge myself to add another string to my bow in a sense and try and become the complete player."


Collins overwhelms Kerber

In other women's fourth round matches, American Danielle Collins shocked an out-of-sorts second seed Angelique Kerber of Germany 6-0, 6-2. Kerber had won the event in 2016 beating Serena Williams in the final.

Czech Petra Kvitova, seeded eight, put out American teenager Amanda Anisimova 6-2, 6-1. Kvitova, who had a WTA Tour-leading five tournament wins last year, extended her winning streak to nine matches after capturing the Sydney International title last week and her four wins at Melbourne Park this week.

