As Nasa works toward its plan to sustainably return to the Moon, it will be critical to collaborate with both commercial and international partners along the way, the US space agency said.

This approach will enable human expansion across the solar system and bring back to Earth new knowledge and opportunities, it said.

Nasa\xe2\x80\x99s robotic lunar surface missions, which will begin as early as 2020, will focus on scientific understanding of lunar resources, and prepare the lunar surface for a sustained human presence, to include the use of lunar oxygen and hydrogen for future lunar vehicles.

The lunar surface may also serve as a crucial training ground and technology demonstration test site where we will prepare for future human missions to Mars and other destinations, Nasa said.

Science gathered about how lunar dust is ejected upwards during a spacecraf\xe2\x80\x99's landing could inform future missions. Nasa and CNSA have agreed that any significant findings resulting from this coordination activity will be shared.


