In case of a management change of a company through a takeover, the new buyer has to make an open offer to the general public to buy an additional 25% shares.




According to Sebi\xe2\x80\x99s takeover norm, the open offer price has to be the highest of the prices determined under four different parameters \xe2\x80\x94 the negotiated price between the buyer and the selling promoter, a volume-weighted average price of the last 52 weeks before the public announcement about the takeover, the highest price that the buyer paid in the last 26 weeks before the announcement of the takeover, and the volume-weighted average price of 60 trading days before the announcement of the takeover.

Jet\xe2\x80\x99s share price in last 52 weeks has seen a low of Rs 163 and a high of Rs 830 (Monday closing was Rs 276). \xe2\x80\x9cEtihad\xe2\x80\x99s offer price of Rs 150 and its request for open offer not being triggered when it increases stake may not be in accordance with Sebi takeover code. SpiceJet\xe2\x80\x99s founder-promoter Ajay Singh was given exemption from open offer when he acquired the low-cost from its previous owners in 2015, but Sebi has to decide on a case-tocase basis. There has to be a way that meets Sebi norms,\xe2\x80\x9d said the source.

\xe2\x80\x9cAt Rs 150 per share, Etihad has valued Jet roughly at $250 million, or about Rs 1,800 crore (keeping $1 at Rs 72). Lenders got an evaluation of Jet done that was much higher,\xe2\x80\x9d the source added.

While Jet did not comment on this issue of Goyal agreeing to step down at \xe2\x80\x9cright\xe2\x80\x9d valuation, Etihad did not say if it will up its offer price for the airline. It is learnt that Etihad will approach SBI \xe2\x80\x94 which is leading the consortium of lenders with an over Rs 8,200-crore exposure to Jet \xe2\x80\x94 with a free offer this week.

