The programme will target startups both in India and Southeast Asia across sectors like consumer internet, deep tech, enterprise software, healthcare technology, fintech, crypto or direct-to-consumer brands. 

\xe2\x80\x9cThe trigger is the realisation that India is doing so well and the tech ecosystem is exploding, on the other hand, we ran a bunch of founders\xe2\x80\x99 surveys and found they are underserved,\xe2\x80\x9d said Shailendra Singh, MD at Sequoia Capital India, adding that unlike other accelerator programmes which take startups from zero to seed funding, Surge will take them from seed to series A. \xe2\x80\x9cThis programme will work independent of Sequoia and that is why we have a separate brand. We will also have a different team for it.\xe2\x80\x9d

There will also be a fee for participating in Surge, which has been waived off for the first batch. While Sequoia India is currently funding the startups from the $695 million fund it raised last year, in the future it may raise a separate fund for Surge. 

Each batch will last for about 16 weeks and will also see the firm bring top entrepreneurs from its portfolio like hospitality major Oyo\xe2\x80\x99s Ritesh Agarwal, restaurant discovery and food delivery company Zomato\xe2\x80\x99s Deepinder Goyal, Indonesian ride-hailing major Go-Jek\xe2\x80\x99s Nadiem Makrim and software player Freshworks\xe2\x80\x99 Girish Mathrubootham as mentors for these startups. Besides, startups will also have access to an advanced management programme (AMP) by Sequoia\xe2\x80\x99s US unit, which is known as an early backer of companies like Google, Paypal, and WhatsApp. 

At the end of the programme, Sequoia also plans to have an investor week for these startups where it will invite other venture capital firms. 

Sequoia has had some success in backing startups at idea stage in the past like payments player Prizm (acquired by Hitachi) and Citrus (bought by PayU) besides SCIO Health (acquired by EXLService). Over the last 10 years, it has done 91 early-stage investments, which is expected to increase with the Surge programme. 

The development comes at a time when angel and seed-stage funding has been on a steady decline falling to 150 as of September 2018 from 400 in 2015, as per a study from Bain and Indian Private Equity and Venture Capital Association (IVCA). 

This has been due to both the issue of 'angel tax' and fall in startup formation, which has started to pick up now. India added 1,200 new technology startups in 2018 compared with 1,000 last year, software lobby group Nasscom had said in a report on the startup ecosystem in October. The number of new tech startups had fallen for the first time in five years in 2017 as funding had slowed.

Sequoia\xe2\x80\x99s move could prompt other large VC firms to start similar programmes as well. 

\xe2\x80\x9cMost of the companies which have been funded in the last two years were started in 2015 or 2016. This will result in more deals at the early stage and founders will also be encouraged to startup because of availability of capital,\xe2\x80\x9d said Madhukar Sinha, founding partner at seed stage investment firm India Quotient. 

'

