b'MELBOURNE: Serena Williams was perfectly composed. Her hair was pulled back and her gear coordinated. She didn\xe2\x80\x99t quite take the ball on the rise and smack it out of sight like she does at press conferences following a hard loss. The 23-time major winner sat easily, shoulders square, head up, letting the ball come on to her racket before playing it away. Her reasoning may have been faulty, but she offered no excuses.


Williams led 5-1 in the deciding set against the seventh seed Karolina Pliskova in the Australian Open quarterfinals. She had four match points, one in the seventh game, on serve, when she was called for a foot fault (first serve) and then appeared to twist her ankle in the exchange that ensued. She had three more chances to close in the 10thgame on her opponent\xe2\x80\x99s serve. But Williams lost six successive games and went down 4-6, 6-4, 5-7 in two hours and 10 minutes.


