However, the positive results won\xe2\x80\x99t be enough for a club like Real if it doesn\xe2\x80\x99t translate into trophies. The current East Bengal coach Alejandro Menendez, who was in charge of the Real Madrid academy team Castilla from 2009-11 and the youth team from 2005-07, knows a thing or two about the 'win at all costs' mentality of his former employers.

Menendez feels Cristiano Ronaldo, who left the club after last season, perfectly fitted into the Real Madrid idea. 

\xe2\x80\x9cThe market is difficult now to bring a big star. And Real might also be interested in developing that one big player in their own backyard who can take them to greater heights. When you produce homegrown talents, they understand the environment and spirit of the club. Real are undergoing a transition period now. A new generation of players has arrived and they all look exciting. But they cannot forget that in Real, you have to win at all cost.

\xe2\x80\x9cRonaldo was suited to this philosophy. But they thought it was the right time to sell him because they were unsure whether he will keep the same level next season. But the problem is there is no substitute for Ronaldo. Vinicius has shown a lot of promise but this will be a crucial period for him. Every match will be a test because Real will judge him whether he is suited to the win-at-all-cost mentality. Brahim and Isco are similar players, but Isco had faltered. So the youngsters need to get used to the pressure fast. Solari also needs to find the right balance between young talent and experienced campaigners,\xe2\x80\x9d Menendez, under whom the Real youth team won the U-19 championship in 2005-06 season, said. 

\xe2\x80\x9cIt was a title that Real couldn\xe2\x80\x99t win for nine years. I won it with Antonio Adan, Jose Callejon, Juan Mata and others,\xe2\x80\x9d he says.

The coach said that he got a taste of Real\xe2\x80\x99s winning-is-everything philosophy when he was at Castilla. Barcelona, under Pep Guardiola then, was changing the football narrative with their La Masia graduates. But Menendez said their game had no such impact on Real. 

\xe2\x80\x9cThe profile of Castilla is completely different to La Masia. In the Barcelona academy, it\xe2\x80\x99s their philosophy that matters, players are secondary. In Real, players are supreme and they want to create big players, not bothering too much about the style. That\xe2\x80\x99s why you will find Castilla has produced more talent who are plying their trade in top divisions all around the world. The advantage of being in Castilla is you not only develop your technical and tactical skills but also mind and character. So the boys can adapt to other leagues easily,\xe2\x80\x9d Menendez said.

