According to Singh, \xe2\x80\x9cIn future, it can be included as a technology for other agronomic advisories.\xe2\x80\x9d

The engineering committee of TRA headed by Atul Asthana, MD of Goodricke Group, evaluated the progress of the machine on the January 21. Talking to TOI, the Goodricke MD said: \xe2\x80\x9cThe vital part of this invention is that it can be made smartphone-friendly and can be used by anyone who has this software downloaded in his or her handset. The industry badly needed such a tool. Tea Board inspectors can also use this tool anywhere, anytime. Development of both the machines should be backed by all the stakeholders in unison.\xe2\x80\x9d 

Talking to media at the 141st AGM of the Indian Tea Association\xe2\x80\x99s (ITA) Dooars Branch, S Soundarajan, director (tea development), Tea Board of India, said: \xe2\x80\x9cThe machine will strictly maintain the quality of the green tea leaves (tender quality) when it enters the tea factory machines for curling, tearing and crushing.\xe2\x80\x9d 

The Tea Board had imposed some regulations last year to improve quality of made tea. \xe2\x80\x9cThere has been a marginal improvement in price realisation after that. Most of the tea factories are following those regulations. We hope for better days once the machines are installed in the gardens,\xe2\x80\x9d said ITA chairman Vivek Goenka. 

Soundarajan added that even bought-leaf factories will be brought under the purview of the new regulation and that they will also be asked to install the new machine. 


