Early on Friday, stocks of all the eight Zee Group companies crashed on the back of reports that one of the companies, being investigated by the SFIO for demonetisation-related wrongdoings, had a link with a Zee entity (see graphic). At close of trading, group flagship Zee Entertainment had crashed over 26%, while Dish TV was down nearly 33%, Zee Learn 18% and Essel Propack 16%. Other group companies also saw their stock prices slide sharply. Investors across these eight companies together lost over Rs 14,000 crore (about $2 billion) in a single session.




According to disclosures on the NSE, two financing firms \xe2\x80\x94 an IndiaInfoline group company and another from the Edelweiss Group \xe2\x80\x94 together sold on Friday over 2.4 crore shares of Dish TV, or about 1.3% of the equity, for nearly Rs 58 crore. Market players said that Chandra and his family now needs to compensate lenders holding group company stocks for the crash in prices. Else, there could be more selling next week, they added.

According to calculations by fund managers, Zee promoters have a total debt of about Rs 11,000 crore, of which the borrowing from fund houses is nearly Rs 7,600 crore. Against this, the total value of the promoter\xe2\x80\x99s holding in group companies is about Rs 18,000 crore.

Reacting to news reports about money laundering by group entities, a Zee spokesperson said it had always maintained high levels of corporate governance. The clarification came after reports linked Nityank Infrapower & Multiventures to Zee Group companies. The spokesperson clarified that Nityank Infra was an independent company and did not belong to Zee Group. \xe2\x80\x9cThe group at this stage does not wish to comment on the legal matter between Dish TV and Videocon Group, since the same is sub judice.\xe2\x80\x9d It also said that investigations by SFIO relating to the transactions with Nityank Infra did not have any bearing or connection with any of the operating entities of the group.


