Upholding the validity of IBC\xe2\x80\x99s Section 29 which debarred defaulters, their business partners and relatives from participating in the resolution plan of a bankrupt company, the bench in its 150-page judgment dealt with every conceivable challenge to IBC, rejected them all and returned a finding that the code \xe2\x80\x9cpassed constitutional muster\xe2\x80\x9d. Section 29 has dealt a body blow to the Ruias, who have been trying back-door methods to bid and gain control of debt-ridden Essar Steel.

Writing the judgment for the bench, Justice Nariman said while IBC was a legitimate experiment, such exercises had earlier failed, being fraught with errors. \xe2\x80\x9cThe experiment contained in IBC, judged by the generality of its provisions and not by so-called crudities and inequalities that have been pointed out by the petitioners, passes constitutional muster. To stay experimentation in things economic is a grave responsibility, and denial of the right to experiment is fraught with serious consequences to the nation,\xe2\x80\x9d he said.

Stating that IBC was a process that involved all stakeholders, the bench noted with happiness the positives of IBC working and said, \xe2\x80\x9cFlow of financial resources to the commercial sector in India has increased exponentially as a result of financial debts being repaid.\xe2\x80\x9d This observation counters the argument of the opposition that IBC has failed to take off and provide the relief intended to stakeholders and creditors of bankrupt companies.



The bench clarified that the complete ban on relatives of erstwhile promoters, responsible for bankruptcy of a company, from participating in the resolution process would debar only those relatives who had business links with the promoters. It also said that unlike a liquidator, a resolution professional had no adjudicating power and was merely responsible for forwarding the resolution plans to the committee of creditors.



