When the two women walk out onto the Rod Laver Arena on Saturday night, for their first meeting, Kvitova, a two-time major winner, will be looking to finish a script she started writing a little after her hand went under a surgeon\xe2\x80\x99s knife in the winter of 2016. Osaka, seeded four, and at 5\xe2\x80\x9911\xe2\x80\x99, an inch shorter than the eighth seed, will be seeking to wipe out a tearstained memory and replace it with one of a sunnier hue.

Osaka is determined to not let her career be defined by one title. \xe2\x80\x9cI realize the work that I put in during the offseason,\xe2\x80\x9d she said. \xe2\x80\x9cEvery match that I played, I tried my best. It just felt like it was a continuous effort. I can\xe2\x80\x99t believe it, but at the same time, it\xe2\x80\x99s sort of the reality I am in right now. I can only keep going forward from here.\xe2\x80\x9d

The Japanese, ringlets dancing around her shoulders and ready smile, is the higher seed, but the Czech, on a run, having won eight successive finals, and on a 11-match winning streak, has to be the favourite. Even if it\xe2\x80\x99s not by much.

Kvitova, looking leaner and meaner in the New Year, is moving better than ever, making her more dangerous. She said her run in Melbourne was more special than her charge at the All England Club where she won titles in 2011 and 2014. \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s probably a little bit more special because it\xe2\x80\x99s after everything I have been through,\xe2\x80\x9d she said, referring to the knife attack. \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s just different.\xe2\x80\x9d

Kvitova, probably the heaviest ball striker in the women\xe2\x80\x99s game, who nails difficult angles with her serve and strokes, hasn\xe2\x80\x99t signed up for practice on non-match days at Melbourne Park. \xe2\x80\x9cI\xe2\x80\x99m only playing matches,\xe2\x80\x9d she said smiling. \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s helping me, kind of have my mind a little bit more free. I needed those days off after Sydney. I really need to recover and have more energy for matches.\xe2\x80\x9d

Sascha Bajin, Osaka\xe2\x80\x99s coach, said that the Japanese pro could handle the left-handed Kvitova\xe2\x80\x99s craft because she adjusts well and fast. \xe2\x80\x9cShe reads the opponent pretty well. Makes my job a lot easier. We had a lefty out today just to hit a couple of serves, to get ready a little bit. Thankfully, she\xe2\x80\x99s good at picking this up.\xe2\x80\x9d

The winner of Saturday\xe2\x80\x99s final will claim the No. 1 ranking for the first time. \xe2\x80\x9cI\xe2\x80\x99m here to play in the final of the major, and that\xe2\x80\x99s how I have my mind set. If No. 1 happens, it happens. It will just be very nice bonus, but I\xe2\x80\x99m really thinking about the title only,\xe2\x80\x9d the 28-year-old said.

Osaka explained that she\xe2\x80\x99s focused on competing well and not winning. \xe2\x80\x9cWhenever I have a tough loss like I had in Brisbane (to the 27th-ranked Lesia Tsurenko in the semifinals), the last tough loss I had, I feel like I learn a lot from it,\xe2\x80\x9d she said. \xe2\x80\x9cThe biggest thing I took away from that loss was the fact that I didn\xe2\x80\x99t really try 100 percent. I accepted that I was going to lose. For me, at this tournament, I wasn\xe2\x80\x99t really focused on winning. I just wanted to make sure I tried 100 percent on every point.\xe2\x80\x9d

