The 21-year-old Japanese added the Australian Open title to the US Open crown she won in September with a 7-6 (7-2), 5-7, 6-4 win over the sentimental favourite Czech Petra Kvitova on Saturday. Osaka, who was ranked outside of the top-70 only a year ago, was handed the Daphne Akhurst Memorial Cup by Li Na, the Chinese superstar, Asia\xe2\x80\x99s first major winner, who won on these courts in 2014.

On Monday, when Osaka takes over as the new No.1, it\xe2\x80\x99ll be the first time an Asian has held that spot. Kvitova will rise to No.2. \xe2\x80\x9cIt\xe2\x80\x99s an honour to have played Petra in the final of a Grand Slam,\xe2\x80\x9d a subdued Osaka said in her speech before thanking her team. \xe2\x80\x9cI wouldn\xe2\x80\x99t have made it through without you. Behind a tennis player is a team.\xe2\x80\x9d

Later Osaka explained that she was disappointed at letting the first clutch of match points go. \xe2\x80\x9cI was sad. You always have doubts in those situations,\xe2\x80\x9d she said, adding, \xe2\x80\x9cI told myself it\xe2\x80\x99s a final, I\xe2\x80\x99m playing Petra and I can\xe2\x80\x99t be immature. I can\xe2\x80\x99t feel so entitled that I\xe2\x80\x99m playing so well, that I can\xe2\x80\x99t lose. I talk to my dad a lot, he\xe2\x80\x99s a kind of a wise guy, he always says I shouldn\xe2\x80\x99t expect to win easy. I didn\xe2\x80\x99t want to have any regrets.\xe2\x80\x9d



The Czech, who has been quite the story of the Championship, making a Grand Slam final after five years, had her career ripped apart by a knife-wielding intruder two years ago. \xe2\x80\x9cI can\xe2\x80\x99t believe I played the final of a Grand Slam again,\xe2\x80\x9d Kvitova said, fighting back tears. \xe2\x80\x9cWell done, Naomi. Congrats for being No.1. I want to thank my team for sticking with me even when I didn\xe2\x80\x99t know if I would be able to hold the racket again.\xe2\x80\x9d

Both players had chances in the first set. Osaka pulling herself out of a hole in the fifth and seventh games, where she faced breakpoints. Kvitova then hit herself out of trouble in the 12th game, where she faced two break points that were set points. The US Open winner responded by pounding her way through the first set tie-break, dropping just two points.



In the second game a Kvitova forehand won her the first break of the match, lifting the Czech to a 2-0 lead. The 21-year-old replied by sprinting away with four straight games, breaking the two-time Wimbledon champion at love in the fifth game. Osaka\xe2\x80\x99s forehand, a mighty shot, always brutal but now struck with more topspin, is more consistent. It was that shot that set her up for the break in the ninth game, grinding her opponent to 0-40, giving her three match points. Then, a combination of the Czech clawing her way back and Osaka\xe2\x80\x99s nerve shaking, saw Kvitova hold for 4-5 before she levelled scores.

The rapidly unravelling Japanese, who held three match points only in the ninth game, was down 0-40 in the 12th. Osaka, who barely won any points after failing to convert the match points, then obliged with a double fault to hand the set to Kvitova. The 21-year-old then left the court for a bathroom break at the end of the set, head bent and towel draped over her head. If she didn\xe2\x80\x99t comeback composed, Osaka worked herself to a calm that allowed her to compete at her best again.



